var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"title":"Static structural analysis of the Pazy wing","page":"Static structural analysis of the Pazy wing","location":"literate/PazyWingTorsionTest/#Static-structural-analysis-of-the-Pazy-wing","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a static structural analysis, using the Technion's Pazy wing benchmark. More specifically, we'll simulate a coupled bending-torsion test. The sectional properties of the wing's spar and the aerodynamic tip loss function were defined by Riso and Cesnik. That data is publicly available at https://github.com/UM-A2SRL/AePW3-LDWG.\n\n(Image: ) Pazy wing coupled bending-torsion test by Avin et al.\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Static structural analysis of the Pazy wing","location":"literate/PazyWingTorsionTest/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting the variables of our problem. In this example we will analyze the displacements and twist of the clamped wing caused by the attachment of a range of tip masses. Notice that we bring into scope some fixed geometrical and discretization properties of the wing's beam through the function geometrical_properties_Pazy.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Fixed geometrical properties\nnElem,L,chord,normSparPos,_ = geometrical_properties_Pazy()\n\n# Set tip mass range\nmRange = collect(0:0.1:3)\n\n# Initialize outputs\ntip_twist = Array{Float64}(undef,length(mRange))\ntip_OOP = Array{Float64}(undef,length(mRange))\nproblem = Array{SteadyProblem}(undef,length(mRange))"},{"title":"Solving the problem","page":"Static structural analysis of the Pazy wing","location":"literate/PazyWingTorsionTest/#Solving-the-problem","category":"section","text":"In the following loop, we create new model instances for each tip mass value, create and solve the steady problem, and then extract the outputs of interest. The tip mass is positioned in such a way to cause not only bending but also significant torsion of the wing. The model creation process is streamlined with the function create_Pazy, taking the appropriate inputs. Notice that the twist angle is computed as the arcsine of the difference in \"height\" between the leading edge and trailing edge of the wingtip (throught the rotation of the local basis vector pointing in the x2-direction).\n\n# Sweep tip mass\nfor (i,m) in enumerate(mRange)\n    # Create model with current tip mass 80 mm behind the trailing-edge\n    PazyWingTorsionTest,_ = create_Pazy(tipMass=m,ηtipMass=[0;-(chord*(1-normSparPos)+0.08);0])\n    # Create and solve problem\n    problem[i] = create_SteadyProblem(model=PazyWingTorsionTest)\n    solve!(problem[i])\n    # Get twist and OOP displacement at midchord\n    tipNodeFinalState = problem[i].loadstepHistory.states[end][nElem].nodal\n    tip_twist[i] = twist_angle(tipNodeFinalState.p2_b)\n    tip_OOP[i] = tipNodeFinalState.u2[3] - chord*(1/2-normSparPos)*sind(tip_twist[i])\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Static structural analysis of the Pazy wing","location":"literate/PazyWingTorsionTest/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"The post-processing begins by loading the reference data.\n\n# Load reference data\nu3Exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/torsion_u3VsMass_Exp.txt\")\nu3UMNAST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/torsion_u3VsMass_UMNAST.txt\")\nθExp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/torsion_thetaVsMass_Exp.txt\")\nθUMNAST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/torsion_thetaVsMass_UMNAST.txt\")\n\nWe can now plot the out-of-plane (OOP) tip displacement and tip twist as functions of the tip mass. The following \"experimental\" results were taken from Figure 17 of the paper by Avin et al.. The correlation with the reference data is good.\n\ngr()\n\n# Tip midchord OOP displacement (offset from zero tip mass value) vs. tip mass\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"Tip mass [kg]\", ylabel=\"Tip OOP displacement offset [% semispan]\", xlims=[mRange[1],mRange[end]])\nplot!(mRange, (tip_OOP.-tip_OOP[1])/L*100, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nplot!(u3UMNAST[1,:], u3UMNAST[2,:], c=:blue, ls=:dash, lw=2, label=\"UM/NAST\")\nscatter!(u3Exp[1,:], u3Exp[2,:], mc=:red, ms=4, msw=0, label=\"Experimental\")\n\n# Tip twist vs. tip mass\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"Tip mass [kg]\", ylabel=\"Tip twist [deg]\", xlims=[mRange[1],mRange[end]])\nplot!(mRange, tip_twist, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nplot!(θUMNAST[1,:], θUMNAST[2,:], c=:blue, ls=:dash, lw=2, label=\"UM/NAST\")\nscatter!(θExp[1,:], θExp[2,:], mc=:red, ms=4, msw=0, label=\"Experimental\")\n\n(Image: ) (Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Static structural analysis of an arch under follower pressure","page":"Static structural analysis of an arch under follower pressure","location":"literate/archUnderFollowerPressure/#Static-structural-analysis-of-an-arch-under-follower-pressure","category":"section","text":"This example simulates the static response of a clamped arch subjected to a normal (follower) pressure. It illustrates how to set up a steady structural problem for an initially curved beam subjected to follower distributed loads. The problem was defined by Argyris and Symeonidis.\n\n(Image: ) Clamped circular arch under normal pressure by Argyris and Symeonidis\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Beam","page":"Static structural analysis of an arch under follower pressure","location":"literate/archUnderFollowerPressure/#Beam","category":"section","text":"The first step is to create a beam. The arch has radius R and spans over and angle θ. The curvature of the beam is thus 1/R, and the total length is L=Rθ. We define the beam orientation such that the arch spans from an angle -θ/2 to θ/2 about a vertical line. This is done by specifying the rotation parameters from basis A to basis b, p0, with the Euler parameters sequence 3-2-1: the angle of rotation about the second axis is -θ/2. The cross-section has area A and bending moment of inertia Iy. The elastic modulus of the material is E. We discretize the beam into nElem finite elements. In this case the properties of the beam are constant over its length, to see a case where those properties vary, check this example.\n\n# Beam\nusing AeroBeams\n\nR,θ = 2.54, deg2rad(120)\nk2 = 1/R\nL = R*θ\nA,Iy = 4.05e-4, 13.1e-8\nE = 70.4e9\nEA,EIy = E*A,E*Iy\nnElem = 40\nbeam = create_Beam(name=\"arch\",length=L,nElements=nElem,S=[isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=1e12,EA=EA,EIy=EIy)],p0=transform_to_WM([0;-θ/2;0]; from=:E321),k=[0;k2;0]);"},{"title":"BCs","page":"Static structural analysis of an arch under follower pressure","location":"literate/archUnderFollowerPressure/#BCs","category":"section","text":"Now we create the boundary conditions (including the pressure load). The normalized load is defined by the λ variable, and the maximum value of the actual distributed load per unit length is q. We add this constant load to the arch as a distributed follower force initially resolved in basis b. We then clamp both ends of the beam (nodes 1 and nElem+1) by setting the displacements and rotations to zero.\n\n# BCs\nλ = 15\nq = -λ*EIy/R^2\nadd_distributed_loads_to_beam!(beam=beam,loads=Dict(:ff_b_of_x1t => (x1,t)->[0; 0; q]))\nclamp1 = create_BC(name=\"clamp1\",beam=beam,node=1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0,:u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\nclamp2 = create_BC(name=\"clamp2\",beam=beam,node=nElem+1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0,:u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))"},{"title":"Model","page":"Static structural analysis of an arch under follower pressure","location":"literate/archUnderFollowerPressure/#Model","category":"section","text":"We are now ready to create the model itself. It is composed of the beam and the boundary conditions.\n\n# Model\narchUnderFollowerPressure = create_Model(name=\"archUnderFollowerPressure\",beams=[beam],BCs=[clamp1,clamp2])"},{"title":"System solver","page":"Static structural analysis of an arch under follower pressure","location":"literate/archUnderFollowerPressure/#System-solver","category":"section","text":"We can set an optional system solver for the nonlinear algebraic system of equations resulting from the finite element discretization. In this case we need to use an arc-length solver in order to go beyond the snap-through point. An appropriate choice for the maximum arc length in this case, found by trial and error, is 0.1.\n\n# System solver\nsystemSolver = create_ArclengthSolver(maxArcLength=0.1)"},{"title":"Problem","page":"Static structural analysis of an arch under follower pressure","location":"literate/archUnderFollowerPressure/#Problem","category":"section","text":"Now we define the steady problem, by inputting the model and our optional system solver, and then solve it.\n\n# Problem\nproblem = create_SteadyProblem(model=archUnderFollowerPressure,systemSolver=systemSolver)\nsolve!(problem)"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Static structural analysis of an arch under follower pressure","location":"literate/archUnderFollowerPressure/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"We are interested in the behavior of the arch's midpoint transverse displacement (in the x3 direction) as a function of the load. So we can get solution history at all partial load steps. The array of the partial load factors is σVec, and the corresponding midpoint displacements are mid_u3.\n\n# Unpack outputs\nhist = problem.loadstepHistory\nσVec = hist.σ\nstates = hist.states\nmid_u3 = [states[i][div(nElem,2)].nodal.u2[3] for i in 1:length(σVec)]\n\nThe following plot shows the midpoint displacement as a function of the load. Notice that the load-displacement curve is initially very stiff and then softens as the arch approaches the snap-through point.\n\nlw = 2\ngr()\n# Plot displacement\nplot(-mid_u3/R, σVec*λ, c=:black, lw=lw, xlabel=\"Midpoint \\$-u_3/R\\$\", ylabel=\"\\$\\\\lambda\\$\", label=false)\n\n(Image: )\n\nLet's also plot the initial and final deformed shape of the arch, along with the boundary conditions. To do that, we exploit AeroBeams' built-in visualization capabilities, by simply calling the plot_steady_deformation() function accordingly.\n\n# Plot deformed shape\nplot_steady_deformation(problem)\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Flutter analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","page":"Flutter analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/#Flutter-analysis-of-a-wing-with-flared-folding-wingtip-(FFWT)","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a flutter analysis for a wing featuring a flared folding wingtip (FFWT). The influence of the root pitch angle on the behavior of this wing model was studied numerically in Healy's PhD Thesis.\n\n(Image: )\n\nTop view of baseline wing model featuring a FFWT by Healy et al.\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Flutter analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting the variables of our problem. In this example we will analyze the coasting angle and stability of the free FFWT under several root pitch angles. The flare angle of the wingtip is of 15 degrees, and the airspeed is varied. The influence of three-dimensional aerodynamic effects is investigated through the tip loss factor, for which two values are assumed (one leading to some tip loss, and another leading to no tip loss). The hinge is assumed as a universal joint but with the axis of rotation constraint. Only the flutter mechanism arising from the interaction of the first two modes (the wingtip flapping mode and the first OOP bending mode) is analyzed.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Hinge configuration\nhingeConfiguration = :free\n\n# Tip loss factor range\nτRange = [12,Inf]\n\n# Pitch angle range [rad]\nθRange = deg2rad.([0,3,6])\n\n# Airspeed range [m/s]\nURange = collect(1:1:40)\n\n# Discretization\nnElementsInner = 16\nnElementsFFWT = 4\n\n# System solver\nsystemSolver = create_NLsolver(method=:newton,linesearch=true)\n\n# Number of modes to track\nnModes = 2\n\n# Eigensolver method and minimum number of eigenvalues to be computed\neigenmethod = :krylovkit\nnev = 50\n\n# Initialize outputs\neigensols = Array{EigenSolution}(undef,length(τRange),length(θRange),length(URange))\nfreqsOsc = Array{Matrix{Float64}}(undef,length(τRange),length(θRange))\ndampsOsc = Array{Matrix{Float64}}(undef,length(τRange),length(θRange))\nϕHinge = Array{Float64}(undef,length(τRange),length(θRange),length(URange))"},{"title":"Solving the problem","page":"Flutter analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/#Solving-the-problem","category":"section","text":"In the following loops, we create new model instances for each combination of tip loss decay factor, pitch angle and airspeed, create and solve the eigen problem, and then extract the coasting angle of the FFWT (ϕHinge) and the frequencies, dampings and eigenvectors. The model creation process is streamlined with the function create_HealyBaselineFFWT, taking the appropriate inputs.\n\n# Sweep tip loss factor\nfor (i,τ) in enumerate(τRange)\n    # Sweep pitch angle\n    for (j,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n        # Initialize guess solution\n        x0 = zeros(0)\n        # Sweep airspeed\n        for (k,U) in enumerate(URange)\n            # Update model\n            model = create_HealyBaselineFFWT(hingeConfiguration=hingeConfiguration,airspeed=U,pitchAngle=θ,hasTipCorrection=true,tipLossDecayFactor=τ,nElementsInner=nElementsInner,nElementsFFWT=nElementsFFWT)\n            # Create and solve problem\n            problem = create_EigenProblem(model=model,systemSolver=systemSolver,x0=x0,eigenmethod=eigenmethod,nev=nev)\n            solve!(problem)\n            # Eigensolution\n            eigensols[i,j,k] = problem.eigensolution\n            # Hinge fold (coasting) angle\n            ϕHinge[i,j,k] = -rad2deg(problem.model.hingeAxisConstraints[1].state.ϕ)\n            # Set initial guess solution for next iteration\n            x0 = problem.systemArrays.x\n        end\n        # Apply mode tracking\n        freqs,damps,_ = mode_tracking(eigensols[i,j,:])\n        # Get frequencies and dampings of oscillatory modes\n        freqsOsc[i,j],dampsOsc[i,j],_ = filter_tracked_modes(freqs,damps; fmin=Hz2rads(1e-1), maxTracks=nModes)\n    end\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Flutter analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"The post-processing begins by loading the reference data.\n\n# Load reference data\nfold_ST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/fold_ST.txt\")\nfold_VLM = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/fold_VLM.txt\")\nfreq1_ST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/freq1_ST.txt\")\nfreq2_ST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/freq2_ST.txt\")\nfreq1_VLM = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/freq1_VLM.txt\")\nfreq2_VLM = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/freq2_VLM.txt\")\ndamp1_ST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/damp1_ST.txt\")\ndamp2_ST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/damp2_ST.txt\")\ndamp1_VLM = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/damp1_VLM.txt\")\ndamp2_VLM = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange/damp2_VLM.txt\")\n\n# Adjust data for plots by padding matrices with NaN\nmatrices = Dict(\n    :fold_ST => fold_ST,\n    :fold_VLM => fold_VLM,\n    :freq1_ST => freq1_ST,\n    :freq2_ST => freq2_ST,\n    :freq1_VLM => freq1_VLM,\n    :freq2_VLM => freq2_VLM,\n    :damp1_ST => damp1_ST,\n    :damp2_ST => damp2_ST,\n    :damp1_VLM => damp1_VLM,\n    :damp2_VLM => damp2_VLM,\n)\nfor key in keys(matrices)\n    matrices[key] .= [x == \"\" ? NaN : x for x in matrices[key]]\nend\nfold_ST = matrices[:fold_ST]\nfold_VLM = matrices[:fold_VLM]\nfreq1_ST = matrices[:freq1_ST]\nfreq2_ST = matrices[:freq2_ST]\nfreq1_VLM = matrices[:freq1_VLM]\nfreq2_VLM = matrices[:freq2_VLM]\ndamp1_ST = matrices[:damp1_ST]\ndamp2_ST = matrices[:damp2_ST]\ndamp1_VLM = matrices[:damp1_VLM]\ndamp2_VLM = matrices[:damp2_VLM]\n\n\n#\n\nWe can now plot the coasting (fold) angle of the FFWT and the evolution of the frequencies and dampings of the modes of vibration as functions of airspeed for each root pitch angle. The following reference results were taken from Fig. 3.32 of Healy's PhD Thesis. Healy's numerical method is composed of a Rayleigh-Ritz structural model coupled to a vortex-lattice method (VLM) or strip-theory (ST) method for aerodynamics, with the flared folding wingtip being modeled as a point inertia connected to the inner wing via a hinge. The behavior of the fold angle is captured with reasonable accuracy, and the correlation of the frequencies and dampings with the reference data is also good, except for the fact that AeroBeams predicts smaller damping magnitudes.\n\ncolors = cgrad(:rainbow, length(θRange), categorical=true)\nlw = 2\ngr()\n\n# Fold angle - without tip loss\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Fold angle [deg]\", xlims=[0,40], ylims=[-90,30], yticks=-90:30:30, title=\"Without tip loss\", legend=:bottomright)\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=\"Healy (2023) - ST\")\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:solid, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nfor (j,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    plot!(fold_ST[2*j-1,:],fold_ST[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(URange, ϕHinge[2,j,:], c=colors[j], lw=lw, label=\"\\$\\\\theta = $(round(Int,θ*180/π)) \\\\degree\\$\")\nend\n\n# Fold angle - with tip loss\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Fold angle [deg]\", xlims=[0,40], ylims=[-90,30], yticks=-90:30:30, title=\"With tip loss\", legend=:bottomright)\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=\"Healy (2023) - VLM\")\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:solid, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nfor (j,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    plot!(fold_VLM[2*j-1,:],fold_VLM[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(URange, ϕHinge[1,j,:], c=colors[j], lw=lw, label=\"\\$\\\\theta = $(round(Int,θ*180/π)) \\\\degree\\$\")\nend\n\n# V-g-f - without tip loss\nplt31 = plot(ylabel=\"Frequency [Hz]\", xlims=[0,30], ylims=[0,5], title=\"Without tip loss\", legend=:bottomright)\nplt32 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Damping Ratio\", xlims=[0,30], ylims=[-0.5,0.25], legend=:bottomleft)\nplot!(plt31, [NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=\"Healy (2023) - ST\")\nplot!(plt31, [NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:solid, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nfor (j,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    for mode in 1:nModes\n        plot!(plt31, URange, rads2Hz.(freqsOsc[2,j][mode,:]), c=colors[j], lw=lw, label=false)\n        plot!(plt32, URange, dampsOsc[2,j][mode,:]./freqsOsc[2,j][mode,:], c=colors[j], lw=lw, label=false)\n    end\n    plot!(plt31, freq1_ST[2*j-1,:], freq1_ST[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(plt31, freq2_ST[2*j-1,:], freq2_ST[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(plt32, damp1_ST[2*j-1,:], damp1_ST[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(plt32, damp2_ST[2*j-1,:], damp2_ST[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(plt32,[NaN],[NaN], c=colors[j], ls=:solid, lw=lw, label=\"\\$\\\\theta = $(round(Int,θ*180/π)) \\\\degree\\$\")\nend\nplt3 = plot(plt31,plt32, layout=(2,1))\n\n# V-g-f - with tip loss\nplt41 = plot(ylabel=\"Frequency [Hz]\", xlims=[0,30], ylims=[0,5], title=\"With tip loss\", legend=:bottomright)\nplt42 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Damping Ratio\", xlims=[0,30], ylims=[-0.5,0.25], legend=:bottomleft)\nplot!(plt41, [NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=\"Healy (2023) - VLM\")\nplot!(plt41, [NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:solid, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nfor (j,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    for mode in 1:nModes\n        plot!(plt41, URange, rads2Hz.(freqsOsc[1,j][mode,:]), c=colors[j], lw=lw, label=false)\n        plot!(plt42, URange, dampsOsc[1,j][mode,:]./freqsOsc[1,j][mode,:], c=colors[j], lw=lw, label=false)\n    end\n    plot!(plt41, freq1_VLM[2*j-1,:], freq1_VLM[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(plt41, freq2_VLM[2*j-1,:], freq2_VLM[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(plt42, damp1_VLM[2*j-1,:], damp1_VLM[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(plt42, damp2_VLM[2*j-1,:], damp2_VLM[2*j,:], c=colors[j], ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=false)\n    plot!(plt42,[NaN],[NaN], c=colors[j], ls=:solid, lw=lw, label=\"\\$\\\\theta = $(round(Int,θ*180/π)) \\\\degree\\$\")\nend\nplt4 = plot(plt41,plt42, layout=(2,1))\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Trimming a flying-wing HALE","page":"Trimming a flying-wing HALE","location":"literate/heliosTrim/#Trimming-a-flying-wing-HALE","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a trim analysis of an aircraft in free flight. For that we take a model of the Helios aircraft, a 75 meters wingspan flying-wing HALE. This aircraft model was described in Patil and Hodges:\n\n(Image: ) Flying-wing HALE model geometry by Patil and Hodges\n\n(Image: ) Flying-wing properties by Patil and Hodges\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Trimming a flying-wing HALE","location":"literate/heliosTrim/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting up the variables of our problem. We select the airfoil for the wing, and the optional flags to include the vertical beam pods and of setting the payload on the wing. Our set up matches that of Patil and Hodges. We will investigate the longitudinal trim of the aircraft at both rigid and elastic (nominal) configurations, using the payload at the center station as the varying parameter.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Flag to include beam pods\nbeamPods = true\n\n# Flag to set payload on wing\npayloadOnWing = false\n\n# Aerodynamic solver\naeroSolver = Indicial()\n\n# System solver\nNR = create_NewtonRaphson(absTol=1e-6,relTol=1e-6,trimSolverMethod=:LM)\n\n# Airspeed [m/s]\nU = feet2meters(40)\n\n# Stiffness factor range\nλRange = [1,50]\n\n# Payload range [lb]\nPRange = collect(0:20:500)\n\n# Initialize outputs\nproblem = Array{TrimProblem}(undef,length(λRange),length(PRange))\ntrimAoA = Array{Float64}(undef,length(λRange),length(PRange))\ntrimThrust = Array{Float64}(undef,length(λRange),length(PRange))\ntrimδ = Array{Float64}(undef,length(λRange),length(PRange))"},{"title":"Solving the problem","page":"Trimming a flying-wing HALE","location":"literate/heliosTrim/#Solving-the-problem","category":"section","text":"At this point we can sweep the stiffness factor and payload vectors to find the trim solution at each value.\n\n# Sweep stiffness factor\nfor (i,λ) in enumerate(λRange)\n    # Sweep payload\n    for (j,P) in enumerate(PRange)\n        # Model\n        helios,midSpanElem,_ = create_Helios(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,beamPods=beamPods,payloadOnWing=payloadOnWing,stiffnessFactor=λ,payloadPounds=P,airspeed=U,δIsTrimVariable=true,thrustIsTrimVariable=true)\n        # Initial guess for the trim problem\n        x0 = j==1 ? zeros(0) : problem[i,j-1].systemArrays.x\n        # Create and solve trim problem\n        problem[i,j] = create_TrimProblem(model=helios,systemSolver=NR,x0=x0)\n        solve!(problem[i,j])\n        # Trim results\n        trimSol = problem[i,j].trimSolution\n        aeroVars = problem[i,j].loadstepHistory.aeroVariables\n        trimAoA[i,j] = rad2deg(aeroVars[end][midSpanElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ)\n        trimThrust[i,j] = trimSol.trimLoads[1]\n        trimδ[i,j] = rad2deg(trimSol.trimδ[1])\n    end\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Trimming a flying-wing HALE","location":"literate/heliosTrim/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"The first post-processing step is to load the reference data by Patil and Hodges.\n\n# Load reference data\nαFlexibleRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Helios/trim_AoA_flexible.txt\")\nαRigidRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Helios/trim_AoA_rigid.txt\")\nδFlexibleRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Helios/trim_delta_flexible.txt\")\nδRigidRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Helios/trim_delta_rigid.txt\")\nTRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Helios/trim_thrust.txt\")\n\nWe now plot the trim outputs as functions of the payload, for both rigid and elastic configurations. The agreement with the reference is very good. Notice that the thrust (per motor) remains almost constant, for both rigid and flexible aircraft, because the drag slope with flap deflection is assumed null (c_d_delta = 0) and induced drag is neglected, so that only the parasite drag (c_d_0) component is present.\n\n# Plot configurations\ncolors = cgrad(:rainbow, length(λRange), categorical=true)\nlabels = [\"Flexible\" \"Rigid\"]\nlw = 2\nms = 3\nmsw = 0\nts = 10\nfs = 16\ngr()\n\n# Trim root angle of attack\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"Payload [lb]\", ylabel=\"Trim root AoA [deg]\", xlims=[0,500], ylims=[0,5], tickfont=font(ts), guidefont=font(fs), legend=:bottomleft, legendfontsize=12)\nplot!([NaN], [NaN], c=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN], [NaN], c=:black, ms=ms, label=\"Patil & Hodges (2006)\")\nfor (i,λ) in enumerate(λRange)\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], c=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=labels[i])\nend\nfor (i,λ) in enumerate(λRange)\n    plot!(PRange, trimAoA[i,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, label=false)\n    if i==1\n        scatter!(αFlexibleRef[1,:], αFlexibleRef[2,:], c=colors[i], ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\n    else\n        scatter!(αRigidRef[1,:], αRigidRef[2,:], c=colors[i], ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\n    end\nend\n\n# Trim elevator deflection\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"Payload [lb]\", ylabel=\"Trim elevator deflection [deg]\", xlims=[0,500], ylims=[0,8], tickfont=font(ts), guidefont=font(fs))\nfor (i,λ) in enumerate(λRange)\n    plot!(PRange, trimδ[i,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, label=false)\n    if i==1\n        scatter!(δFlexibleRef[1,:], δFlexibleRef[2,:], c=colors[i], ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\n    else\n        scatter!(δRigidRef[1,:], δRigidRef[2,:], c=colors[i], ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\n    end\nend\n\n# Trim thrust per motor\nplt3 = plot(xlabel=\"Payload [lb]\", ylabel=\"Trim thrust per motor [N]\", xlims=[0,500], ylims=[0,40], tickfont=font(ts), guidefont=font(fs))\nfor (i,λ) in enumerate(λRange)\n    plot!(PRange, trimThrust[i,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, label=false)\n    scatter!(TRef[1,:], TRef[2,:], c=colors[i], ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\nend\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Modal analysis of a rotating beam","page":"Modal analysis of a rotating beam","location":"literate/sweptTipRotor/#Modal-analysis-of-a-rotating-beam","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a modal (eigen) analysis, using a swept-tip rotor. We will investigate the vibration modes of this rotor at several rotating speeds and several angles of the tip. This problem was proposed by Hodges, Shang and Cesnik, based on the experiments of Epps and Chandra.\n\n(Image: )\n\nGeometry of the swept-tip rotor by Hodges, Shang and Cesnik\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Modal analysis of a rotating beam","location":"literate/sweptTipRotor/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"To set up the problem, we need to define the geometric and material properties of the beams, to later create them. We also define the number of elements for each beam, the number of vibration modes to extract and the ranges for the angular velocity and the tip angle. Notice that the beam is clamped at a position r0 relative to the axis of rotation, which is input to the function create_Model through the argument firstNodePosition. Moreover, the model assembly assumes by default that all the beams given by the beams argument are connected in sequence (e.g., the first node of tipBeam is the same as the last of baseBeam).\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Geometric properties\nL1,L2 = 31.5, 6.0\nb,H = 1, 0.063\nA,Iy,Iz = b*H, b*H^3/12, H*b^3/12\nJ = Iy + Iz\nKsy,Ksz,Kt = 5/6, 1/14.625, 1/65.852\nr0 = [2.5; 0.0; 0.0]\n\n# Material properties (aluminum)\nE = 1.06e7\nG = E/(2*(1+0.325))\nρ = 2.51e-4\n\n# Sectional stiffness and inertia matrices\nS = isotropic_stiffness_matrix(EA=E*A,GAy=G*A*Ksy,GAz=G*A*Ksz,GJ=G*J*Kt,EIy=E*Iy,EIz=E*Iz)\nI = inertia_matrix(ρ=ρ,A=A,Iy=Iy,Iz=Iz)\n\n# Discretization variables\nnElem1 = 20\nnElem2 = 10\n\n# Range of angular velocity [rad/s]\nωRange = rpm2rads.([0,500,750])\n\n# Range of tip beam angles [rad]\ntipAngleRange = deg2rad.(0:2.5:45)\n\n# Number of modes to track\nnModes = 8\n\n# Eigensolver method and minimum number of eigenvalues to be computed\neigenmethod = :fulldecomp\nnev = 30\n\n# Beam frame\nbaseBeam = create_Beam(name=\"baseBeam\",length=L1,nElements=nElem1,S=[S],I=[I])\ntipBeam = create_Beam(name=\"tipBeam\",length=L2,nElements=nElem2,S=[S],I=[I])\n\n# BCs\nclamp = create_BC(name=\"clamp\",beam=baseBeam,node=1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0,:u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\n\n# Create model\nsweptTipRotor = create_Model(name=\"sweptTipRotor\",beams=[baseBeam,tipBeam],BCs=[clamp],firstNodePosition=r0,units=create_UnitsSystem(length=\"in\",force=\"lbf\",frequency=\"Hz\"))\n\n# Initialize outputs\nproblem = Array{EigenProblem}(undef,length(ωRange),length(tipAngleRange))\neigensols = Array{EigenSolution}(undef,length(ωRange),length(tipAngleRange))\nfreqs = Array{Matrix{Float64}}(undef,length(ωRange))"},{"title":"Solving the problem","page":"Modal analysis of a rotating beam","location":"literate/sweptTipRotor/#Solving-the-problem","category":"section","text":"We now solve an eigenproblem at each combination of tip angle and angular velocity, and extract the modal frequencies. Notice that we set the angular velocity of the entire model through the angular velocity of basis mathcalA, using the variable ω_A. AeroBeams will automatically solve the steady problem to find the deformed state of the model, and then perform an eigen-analysis around that state.\n\n# Loop over sweep variables\nfor (i,ω) in enumerate(ωRange)\n    # Set angular velocity of basis A\n    update_ω_A!(sweptTipRotor,ω_A=[0; 0; ω])\n    # Loop tip angles\n    for (j,tipAngle) in enumerate(tipAngleRange)\n        # Update tipBeam orientation angle\n        update_beam_orientation!(sweptTipRotor; beam=tipBeam,p0=transform_to_WM([-tipAngle,0,0],from=:E321))\n        # Create and solve eigenproblem\n        problem[i,j] = create_EigenProblem(model=sweptTipRotor,eigenmethod=eigenmethod,nev=nev)\n        solve!(problem[i,j])\n        # Eigensolution\n        eigensols[i,j] = problem[i,j].eigensolution\n    end\n    # Apply mode tracking\n    freqs[i],_ = mode_tracking(eigensols[i,:])\n    # Get frequencies of oscillatory modes in Hz\n    freqs[i],_ = filter_tracked_modes(rads2Hz.(freqs[i]); fmin=eps(), maxTracks=nModes)\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Modal analysis of a rotating beam","location":"literate/sweptTipRotor/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"We begin by loading the experimental values by Epps and Chandra.\n\n# Experimental values\nexpTipAngles = [0, 15, 30, 45]\nexpFreqs1 = [1.4 1.8 1.7 1.6; 10.2 10.1 10.2 10.2; 14.8 14.4 14.9 14.7]\nexpFreqs2 = [10.3 10.2 10.4 10.4; 25.2 25.2 23.7 21.6; 36.1 34.8 30.7 26.1]\nexpFreqs3 = [27.7 27.2 26.6 24.8; 47.0 44.4 39.3 35.1; 62.9 55.9 48.6 44.8]\nexpFreqs4 = [95.4 87.5 83.7 78.8; 106.6 120.1 122.6 117.7; 132.7 147.3 166.2 162.0]\n\nNow we plot the evolution of the modal frequencies with the tip angle for the range of angular velocities. The numerical results agree well with the experimental ones, and match those described by Hodges, Shang and Cesnik and Yu and Blair.\n\ngr()\ncolors = cgrad(:darkrainbow, length(ωRange), categorical=true)\nlw = 2\nms = 5\nmsw = 0\n\n# 1st bending mode\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"Tip sweep angle [deg]\", ylabel=\"Frequency [Hz]\", title=\"1st bending\", legend=(0.15,0.4))\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN],[NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"Epps & Chandra (1996)\")\nfor (i,ω) in enumerate(ωRange)\n    mode = 1\n    plot!(rad2deg.(tipAngleRange),freqs[i][mode,:], lc=colors[i], lw=lw, label=false)\n    scatter!(expTipAngles,expFreqs1[i,:], mc=colors[i], ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"\\$\\\\omega\\$ = $(round(Int,rads2rpm(ω))) rpm\")\nend\n\n# 2nd bending\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"Tip sweep angle [deg]\", ylabel=\"Frequency [Hz]\", title=\"2nd bending\", legend=(0.15,0.4))\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN],[NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"Epps & Chandra (1996)\")\nfor (i,ω) in enumerate(ωRange)\n    mode = i < 3 ? 2 : 3\n    plot!(rad2deg.(tipAngleRange),freqs[i][mode,:], lc=colors[i], lw=lw, label=false)\n    scatter!(expTipAngles,expFreqs2[i,:], mc=colors[i], ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"\\$\\\\omega\\$ = $(round(Int,rads2rpm(ω))) rpm\")\nend\n\n# 3rd bending mode\nplt3 = plot(xlabel=\"Tip sweep angle [deg]\", ylabel=\"Frequency [Hz]\", title=\"3rd bending\", legend=:topright)\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN],[NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"Epps & Chandra (1996)\")\nfor (i,ω) in enumerate(ωRange)\n    mode = 4\n    plot!(rad2deg.(tipAngleRange),freqs[i][mode,:], lc=colors[i], lw=lw, label=false)\n    scatter!(expTipAngles,expFreqs3[i,:], mc=colors[i], ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"\\$\\\\omega\\$ = $(round(Int,rads2rpm(ω))) rpm\")\nend\n\n# Coupled bending-torsion modes at ω = 750 rpm\nplt4 = plot(xlabel=\"Tip sweep angle [deg]\", ylabel=\"Frequency [Hz]\", title=\"Coupled bending-torsion at \\$\\\\omega\\$ = 750 rpm\", legend=(0.1,0.99))\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN],[NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"Epps & Chandra (1996)\")\nmodes = [5,6,7]\nmodeLabels = [\"1T/5B\",\"5B/1T\",\"4B/1T\"]\nfor (i,m) in enumerate(modes)\n    plot!(rad2deg.(tipAngleRange),freqs[3][m,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, label=false)\n    scatter!(expTipAngles,expFreqs4[i,:], c=colors[i], ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=colors[i], mc=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=modeLabels[i])\nend\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Creating-a-HALE-aircraft-model","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to create a conventional aircraft model, the high-altitude long-endurance (HALE) aircraft described by Patil, Hodges and Cesnik:\n\n(Image: ) HALE model geometry by Patil, Hodges and Cesnik\n\n(Image: ) HALE model data by Patil, Hodges and Cesnik\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here.\n\nThe contents of the present example are wrapped into the function create_cHALE, in which you may check all the keyword arguments that we'll define below.\n\n# Define input arguments\nusing AeroBeams\n\naltitude=20e3\naeroSolver=Indicial()\nderivationMethod=AD()\nflapSolver=TableLookup()\ngustSolver=IndicialGust(:Kussner)\nstabilizersAero=true\nincludeVS=true\nnElemWing=20\nnElemHorzStabilizer=10\nnElemTailBoom=10\nnElemVertStabilizer=5\n∞=1e12\nstiffnessFactor=1\ntorsionStiffnessFactor=1\nk1=0\nk2=0\nairspeed=0\nδElevIsTrimVariable=false\nδAilIsTrimVariable=false\nδRuddIsTrimVariable=false\nthrustIsTrimVariable=false\nδElev=nothing\nδAil=nothing\nδRudd=nothing\nthrust=0\ng=standard_atmosphere(altitude).g\nwingCd0=0\nwingcnδ=2.5\nwingcmδ=-0.35\nwingcdδ=0.15\nstabsCd0=0\nstabscnδ=2.5\nstabscmδ=-0.35\nstabscdδ=0.15\nupdateAirfoilParameters=false\nhasInducedDrag=true"},{"title":"Validation","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Validation","category":"section","text":"The first step is the validation of the inputs, for which reasonable values are necessary.\n\n# Validate\n@assert iseven(nElemWing) \"nElemWing must be even\"\n@assert iseven(nElemHorzStabilizer) \"nElemHorzStabilizer must be even\"\n@assert ∞ > 1e8 \"rigid structural properties must be greater than 1e8\"\n@assert stiffnessFactor > 0 \"stiffnessFactor must be greater than 0\"\n@assert wingCd0 >= 0 \"wingCd0 must be non-negative\"\n@assert stabsCd0 >= 0 \"stabsCd0 must be non-negative\"\n@assert airspeed >= 0 \"airspeed must be non-negative\"\n@assert g >= 0 \"g must be non-negative\"\nδElevIsInput = !isnothing(δElev)\nδAilIsInput = !isnothing(δAil)\nδRuddIsInput = !isnothing(δRudd)\nif δElevIsInput\n    @assert !δElevIsTrimVariable \"cannot set δElevIsTrimVariable to true when δElev is provided as input\"\nend\nif δElev isa Real\n    δElevConst = δElev\n    δElev = t -> δElevConst\nend\nif δAilIsInput\n    @assert !δAilIsTrimVariable \"cannot set δAilIsTrimVariable to true when δAil is provided as input\"\nend\nif δAil isa Real\n    δAilConst = δAil\n    δAil = t -> δAilConst\nend\nif δRuddIsInput\n    @assert !δRuddIsTrimVariable \"cannot set δRuddIsTrimVariable to true when δRudd is provided as input\"\nend\nif δRudd isa Real\n    δRuddConst = δRudd\n    δRudd = t -> δRuddConst\nend\nif thrust isa Real\n    thrustConst = thrust\n    thrust = t -> thrustConst\nend"},{"title":"Atmosphere","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Atmosphere","category":"section","text":"Let's initialize an instance of the International Standard Atmosphere (ISA) at the specified altitude using the function standard_atmosphere.\n\n# Atmosphere\natmosphere = standard_atmosphere(altitude)"},{"title":"Airfoils","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Airfoils","category":"section","text":"The airfoils of the wing and stabilizers are created with the function create_Airfoil. However, the aerodynamic parameters of the airfoils are modified with respect to a reference flat plate airfoil by first creating a new instance of the airfoils' databases with the function modified_database.\n\n# Airfoils\nwFlapSiteID = 75\ndbWingAirfoil = modified_database(\n    \"FlatPlate\";\n    attached = (\n        cd₀ = wingCd0,\n    ),\n    flap = (\n        wFlapSiteID => create_FlapParameters(wingcdδ,wingcmδ,wingcnδ),\n    )\n)\nwAirfoil = create_Airfoil(database=dbWingAirfoil,Ma=airspeed/atmosphere.a)\n\nsFlapSiteID = 75\ndbStabAirfoil = modified_database(\n    \"FlatPlate\";\n    attached = (\n        cd₀ = stabsCd0,\n    ),\n    flap = (\n        sFlapSiteID => create_FlapParameters(stabscdδ,stabscmδ,stabscnδ),\n    )\n)\nsAirfoil = create_Airfoil(database=dbStabAirfoil,Ma=airspeed/atmosphere.a)"},{"title":"Wing aerodynamic surface","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Wing-aerodynamic-surface","category":"section","text":"In AeroBeams, each beam can have an aerodynamic surface attached to it. The wing surface is defined by an airfoil, wAirfoil, the normalized position of the beam's reference line (spar) on the chord, wNormSparPos, the normalized position of the flap (aileron) hinge on the chord, wNormFlapPos, and the normalized endpoints of the flap (aileron) span over the beam's arclength, wNormFlapSpan. To create an aerodynamic surface, we use the function create_AeroSurface.\n\n# Wing surface\nwChord = 1\nwNormSparPos = 0.5\nwNormFlapPos = wFlapSiteID/100\nwFlapSize = 0.25\nwLNormFlapSpan = [0, wFlapSize]\nwRNormFlapSpan = [1-wFlapSize, 1]\n\nwingSurfLeft = create_AeroSurface(\n            ppSolver=aeroSolver,\n            gustSolver=gustSolver,\n            derivationMethod=derivationMethod,\n            flapSolver=flapSolver,\n            airfoil=wAirfoil,\n            c=wChord,\n            normSparPos=wNormSparPos,\n            normFlapPos=wNormFlapPos,\n            normFlapSpan=wLNormFlapSpan,\n            δ = δAilIsInput ? δAil : nothing,\n            δIsTrimVariable=δAilIsTrimVariable,\n            updateAirfoilParameters=updateAirfoilParameters,\n            hasInducedDrag=hasInducedDrag,\n            hasSymmetricCounterpart=true\n            )\n\nwingSurfRight = create_AeroSurface(\n        ppSolver=aeroSolver,\n        gustSolver=gustSolver,\n        derivationMethod=derivationMethod,\n        flapSolver=flapSolver,\n        airfoil=wAirfoil,\n        c=wChord,\n        normSparPos=wNormSparPos,\n        normFlapPos=wNormFlapPos,\n        normFlapSpan=wRNormFlapSpan,\n        δ = δAilIsInput ? δAil : nothing,\n        δIsTrimVariable=δAilIsTrimVariable,\n        updateAirfoilParameters=updateAirfoilParameters,\n        hasInducedDrag=hasInducedDrag,\n        hasSymmetricCounterpart=true\n        )"},{"title":"Wing beam (spar)","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Wing-beam-(spar)","category":"section","text":"Next, we'll define the structural properties of the beam. Notice that we have the option of specifying a stiffness factor, stiffnessFactor, for the relevant properties of the wing. We then use the built-in functions isotropic_stiffness_matrix and inertia_matrix to create the sectional stiffness (force-strain relations) and inertia (velocity-displacement relation) matrices.\n\n# Beam properties\nLw = 16\nwGJ,wEIy,wEIz = 1e4, 2e4, 4e6\nwGJ,wEIy,wEIz = AeroBeams.multiply_scalars!(stiffnessFactor, wGJ,wEIy,wEIz)\nwGJ *= torsionStiffnessFactor\nwρA,wρIs = 0.75,0.1\nwρIy,wρIz = (wEIy/wEIz)*wρIs,(1-wEIy/wEIz)*wρIs\nSwing = isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=∞,GJ=wGJ,EIy=wEIy,EIz=wEIz)\nIwing = inertia_matrix(ρA=wρA,ρIy=wρIy,ρIz=wρIz)\n\nIn order to position the center of the wing at the origin of the frame A, we need the position of the first node of the assembly, at the left wingtip. That firstNodePosition vector will be a function of the undeformed wing curvature k2 (which is zero in the original model). Similarly, the initial angle of twist of the wing, ψ, is a function of the undeformed wing twist curvature, k1 (also zero in the original model).\n\n# Initial position for first node of left wing\nρ = 1/k2\nθ = Lw/ρ\nx = ρ*sin(θ)\nz = ρ*(1-cos(θ))\nfirstNodePosition = iszero(k2) ?\n                    [-Lw; 0; 0] :\n                    [-x; 0; -z]\n\n# Initial angle of twist\nr = 1/k1\nψ = Lw/r\n\nTo create the beams of the left and right wings, we use the function create_Beam. The rotation parameters from the underformed beam basis (b) to the global basis (A), namely p0, are set for the left wing such that the left and right wings become symmetric about the x1-plane of basis A. We also create an antissymetric link between the ailerons of the left and right wings, using the function create_FlapLink.\n\n# Wing beams\nleftWing = create_Beam(\n        name=\"leftWing\",\n        length=Lw,\n        nElements=div(nElemWing,2),\n        S=[Swing],\n        I=[Iwing],\n        k=[-k1; k2; 0],\n        p0=transform_to_WM([0; -θ; ψ], from=:E321),\n        surface=wingSurfLeft,\n        )\n\nrightWing = create_Beam(\n    name=\"rightWing\",\n    length=Lw,\n    nElements=div(nElemWing,2),\n    S=[Swing],\n    I=[Iwing],\n    k=[k1; k2; 0],\n    surface=wingSurfRight,\n    )\n\n# Link wing ailerons\naileronLink = create_FlapLink(masterBeam=rightWing,slaveBeams=[leftWing],δMultipliers=[-1])"},{"title":"Payload","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Payload","category":"section","text":"The payload is modeled as a point inertia at the center of the wing spar. It is created with the constructor create_PointInertia, and then added to the center of the wing through the function add_point_inertia_to_beam!.\n\n# Payload\npayloadMass = 50\npayloadInertia = 200\npayload = create_PointInertia(\n        mass=payloadMass,\n        η=[-Lw/div(nElemWing,2)/2; 0; 0],\n        Iyy=payloadInertia,\n        Izz=payloadInertia,\n        Ixx=payloadInertia\n        )\nadd_point_inertia_to_beam!(beam=rightWing,elementID=1,pointInertia=payload)"},{"title":"Tail boom","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Tail-boom","category":"section","text":"The tail boom is assumed to be a rigid beam, devoid of aerodynamic surfaces, and rigidly connected to wing spar. Some of its sectional inertia matrix properties are assumed with reasonable values, since they are not given in the reference.\n\n# Tail boom\nLt = 10\ntρA,tρIy,tρIz = 0.08,wρIy/10,wρIz/10\nStail = isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=∞)\nItail = inertia_matrix(ρA=tρA,ρIy=tρIy,ρIz=tρIz)\ntailBoom = create_Beam(\n        name=\"tailBoom\",\n        length=Lt,\n        nElements=nElemTailBoom,\n        S=[Stail],\n        I=[Itail],\n        p0=transform_to_WM([-π/2; 0; 0], from=:E321),\n        )"},{"title":"Horizontal stabilizer","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Horizontal-stabilizer","category":"section","text":"The horizontal stabilizer's aerodynamic surface is constructed similarly to the wing's, though it depends on whether the elevator deflecton is a trim variable or not (through the flags δElevIsTrimVariable and δElevIsInput).\n\n# Horizontal stabilizer surface\nhChord = 0.5\nhNormSparPos = 0.5\nhNormFlapPos = sFlapSiteID/100\nhNormFlapSpan = [0, 1]\n\nhsSurf = stabilizersAero ?\n        create_AeroSurface(\n            ppSolver=aeroSolver,\n            gustSolver=gustSolver,\n            derivationMethod=derivationMethod,\n            flapSolver=flapSolver,\n            airfoil=sAirfoil,\n            c=hChord,\n            normSparPos=hNormSparPos,\n            normFlapPos=hNormFlapPos,\n            normFlapSpan=hNormFlapSpan,\n            δ = δElevIsInput ? δElev : nothing,\n            δIsTrimVariable=δElevIsTrimVariable,\n            updateAirfoilParameters=updateAirfoilParameters,\n            hasInducedDrag=hasInducedDrag,\n            hasSymmetricCounterpart=false\n        ) :\n        nothing\n\nThe horizontal stabilizer beam (spar) is also created analogously to the wing, albeit now we input the firstNodeOffset keyword to create_Beam in order to the define the position of the stabilizer's first node at the left tip.\n\n# Horizontal stabilizer beam\nLh = 5\nhρA,hρIy,hρIz = 0.08,wρIy/10,wρIz/10\nShorz = isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=∞)\nIhorz = inertia_matrix(ρA=hρA,ρIy=hρIy,ρIz=hρIz)\nhorzStabilizer = create_Beam(\n        name=\"horzStabilizer\",\n        length=Lh,\n        firstNodeOffset=[-Lh/2; 0; 0],\n        nElements=nElemHorzStabilizer,\n        S=[Shorz],\n        I=[Ihorz],\n        surface=hsSurf\n        )"},{"title":"Vertical stabilizer","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Vertical-stabilizer","category":"section","text":"The construction of the vertical stabilizer is again similar to that of the wing and horizontal counterpart.\n\n# Vertical stabilizer surface\nvChord = 0.5\nvNormSparPos = 0.5\nvNormFlapPos = sFlapSiteID/100\nvNormFlapSpan = [0, 1]\n\nvsSurf = stabilizersAero ?\n            create_AeroSurface(\n                ppSolver=aeroSolver,\n                gustSolver=gustSolver,\n                derivationMethod=derivationMethod,\n                flapSolver=flapSolver,\n                airfoil=sAirfoil,\n                c=vChord,\n                normSparPos=vNormSparPos,\n                normFlapPos=vNormFlapPos,\n                normFlapSpan=vNormFlapSpan,\n                δ = δRuddIsInput ? δRudd : nothing,\n                δIsTrimVariable=δRuddIsTrimVariable,\n                updateAirfoilParameters=updateAirfoilParameters,\n                hasInducedDrag=hasInducedDrag,\n                hasSymmetricCounterpart=false\n            ) :\n            nothing\n\n# Vertical stabilizer beam\nLv = 2.5\nvρA,vρIy,vρIz = 0.08,hρIy,hρIz\nSvert = isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=∞)\nIvert = inertia_matrix(ρA=vρA,ρIy=vρIy,ρIz=vρIz)\nvertStabilizer = create_Beam(\n        name=\"vertStabilizer\",\n        length=Lv,\n        nElements=nElemVertStabilizer,\n        S=[Svert],\n        I=[Ivert],\n        p0=transform_to_WM([0; -π/2; 0], from=:E321),\n        surface=vsSurf\n        )"},{"title":"Thrust force","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Thrust-force","category":"section","text":"The thrust force of the vehicle is modeled as a follower concentrated force acting at the wing's center node (node 1 of rightWing), initially pointing in the x2-direction of the undeformed beam basis. Whether it is to take a time-dependent user input value or to be a trim variable depends on the user's choice. It is created using the function create_BC.\n\n# Propeller thrust force\npropThrust = create_BC(\n        name=\"propThrust\",\n        beam=rightWing,\n        node=1,\n        conditions=Dict(:Ff2b=>thrust),\n        toBeTrimmed=[thrustIsTrimVariable]\n        )"},{"title":"Model assembly","page":"Creating a HALE aircraft model","location":"literate/cHALEmodel/#Model-assembly","category":"section","text":"The beams composing the model depend on whether the vertical stabilizer is to be included, through the flag includeVS. The connections between those beams have to be set through the connections dictionary, in which each key-value pair defines the beams and the corresponding nodes to be connected. The model is created with the appropriate inputs using the function create_Model. Notice that the keyword argument firstNodePosition defines the position of the first node of the first beam of the model, namely, the node at the left wingtip. Also, the velocity of the body-attached global frame A with respect to an inertial frame is set through v_A.\n\n# Beams of the model\nbeams = includeVS ?\n        [leftWing,rightWing,tailBoom,horzStabilizer,vertStabilizer] :\n        [leftWing,rightWing,tailBoom,horzStabilizer]\n\n# Beam connections\nconnections = includeVS ?\n[\n    (rightWing => leftWing, 1 => div(nElemWing,2)+1),\n    (tailBoom => rightWing, 1 => 1),\n    (horzStabilizer => tailBoom, div(nElemHorzStabilizer,2)+1 => nElemTailBoom+1),\n    (vertStabilizer => tailBoom, 1 => nElemTailBoom+1)\n] :\n[\n    (rightWing => leftWing, 1 => div(nElemWing,2)+1),\n    (tailBoom => rightWing, 1 => 1),\n    (horzStabilizer => tailBoom, div(nElemHorzStabilizer,2)+1 => nElemTailBoom+1),\n]\n\n# Aircraft model\ncHALE = create_Model(\n        name=\"cHALE\",\n        beams=beams,\n        BCs=[propThrust],\n        firstNodePosition=firstNodePosition,\n        gravityVector=[0; 0; -g],\n        connections=connections,\n        v_A=[0; airspeed; 0],\n        flapLinks=[aileronLink],\n        atmosphere=atmosphere,\n        )\n\nFinally, we may visually check the undeformed assembly through the function plot_undeformed_assembly:\n\n# Visualization\nplot_undeformed_assembly(cHALE, view=(30,30))\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of the Pazy wing","page":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of the Pazy Wing","location":"literate/PazyWingPitchRange/#Steady-aeroelastic-analysis-of-the-Pazy-wing","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a steady aeroelastic analysis, using the Technion's Pazy wing benchmark. The sectional properties of the wing's spar and the aerodynamic tip loss function were defined by Riso and Cesnik. The data is publicly available at https://github.com/UM-A2SRL/AePW3-LDWG.\n\n(Image: )\n\nPazy wing in the wind tunnel by Avin et al.\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of the Pazy Wing","location":"literate/PazyWingPitchRange/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting the variables of our problem. In this example we will analyze the displacements and twist of the clamped wing under several combinations of root pitch angle and airspeed, which are defined by the arrays θRange and URange. The wing was tested with a balance mass of 0.01 kg attached to its tip store, at a position of 40 mm behind the trailing-edge. Notice that we bring into scope some fixed geometrical and discretization properties of the wing's beam through the function geometrical_properties_Pazy.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Aerodynamic solver\naeroSolver = Indicial()\n\n# Airfoil section\nairfoil = deepcopy(FlatPlate)\n\n# Flag for upright position\nupright = true\n\n# Gravity\ng = 0\n\n# Flag for small angles approximation\nsmallAngles = true\n\n# Fixed geometrical and discretization properties\nnElem,L,chord,normSparPos,nodalPositions = geometrical_properties_Pazy()\n\n# Tip mass (for test 1, 0.01 kg, 40 mm behind the trailing-edge)\ntipMass = 0.01\nηtipMass = [0; -chord*(1-normSparPos)-0.04; 0]\n\n# Root pitch angle [rad] and airspeed [m/s] ranges\nθRange = deg2rad.([3,5,7])\nURange = vcat(1e-3,1:1:100)\n\n# Initialize outputs\nproblem = Array{SteadyProblem}(undef,length(θRange),length(URange))\ntip_OOP = Array{Float64}(undef,length(θRange),length(URange))\ntip_IP = Array{Float64}(undef,length(θRange),length(URange))\ntip_twist = Array{Float64}(undef,length(θRange),length(URange))\ntip_AoA = Array{Float64}(undef,length(θRange),length(URange))"},{"title":"Solving the problem","page":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of the Pazy Wing","location":"literate/PazyWingPitchRange/#Solving-the-problem","category":"section","text":"In the following loops, we create new model instances with the combination of pitch angle and airspeed, create and solve the steady problem, and then extract the outputs of interest. The model creation process is streamlined with the function create_Pazy, taking the appropriate inputs. Notice that the twist angle is computed as the arcsine of the difference in \"height\" between the leading edge and trailing edge of the wingtip (throught the rotation of the local basis vector pointing in the x2-direction).\n\n# Sweep root angle\nfor (i,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    # Initialize guess solution\n    x0 = zeros(0)\n    # Sweep airspeed\n    for (j,U) in enumerate(URange)\n        # Update model\n        PazyWingPitchRange,_ = create_Pazy(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,airfoil=airfoil,upright=upright,pitchAngle=θ,airspeed=U,gravityVector=[0,0,-g],tipMass=tipMass,ηtipMass=ηtipMass,smallAngles=smallAngles)\n        # Create and solve problem\n        problem[i,j] = create_SteadyProblem(model=PazyWingPitchRange,x0=x0)\n        solve!(problem[i,j])\n        # Get tip twist, AoA, IP and OOP displacement at beam reference line\n        tipElemFinalAero = problem[i,j].loadstepHistory.aeroVariables[end][nElem]\n        tipNodeFinalState = problem[i,j].loadstepHistory.states[end][nElem].nodal\n        tip_twist[i,j] = twist_angle(tipNodeFinalState.p2_b)\n        tip_OOP[i,j] = tipNodeFinalState.u2_b[3]\n        tip_IP[i,j] = -tipNodeFinalState.u2_b[2]\n        tip_AoA[i,j] = rad2deg(tipElemFinalAero.unsteadyKin.αₑ)\n        # Set initial guess solution for next iteration\n        x0 = problem[i,j].systemArrays.x\n    end\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of the Pazy Wing","location":"literate/PazyWingPitchRange/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"The post-processing begins by loading the reference data.\n\n# Load reference data\ntip_u3VsU_rootPitch5_Exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_u3VsU_rootPitch5_Exp.txt\")\ntip_u3VsU_rootPitch5_UMNAST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_u3VsU_rootPitch5_UMNAST.txt\")\ntip_u3VsU_rootPitch7_Exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_u3VsU_rootPitch7_Exp.txt\")\ntip_u3VsU_rootPitch7_UMNAST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_u3VsU_rootPitch7_UMNAST.txt\")\ntip_thetaVsU_rootPitch5_Exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch5_Exp.txt\")\ntip_thetaVsU_rootPitch5_UMNAST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch5_UMNAST.txt\")\ntip_thetaVsU_rootPitch7_Exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch7_Exp.txt\")\ntip_thetaVsU_rootPitch7_UMNAST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch7_UMNAST.txt\")\ntip_u3Vsq_rootPitch3_ExpTest1 = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_u3Vsq_rootPitch3_ExpTest1.txt\")\ntip_u3Vsq_rootPitch5_ExpTest1 = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_u3Vsq_rootPitch5_ExpTest1.txt\")\ntip_u3Vsq_rootPitch7_ExpTest1 = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/tip_u3Vsq_rootPitch7_ExpTest1.txt\")\nρTest2 = 2*1050/43^2 # Estimated air density for test 2, from Table 13 of Avin et al.'s paper\n\nWe can now plot the outputs as a function of airspeed for each of the root pitch angles. The following \"experimental\" results were taken from Figure 33 of the paper by Avin et al.. Note that the experimental twist angle was actually estimated by Riso and Cesnik using the diffence between the leading- and trailing-edge out-of-plane displacements of Avin et al.. The correlation with the experimental data and the reference numerical solution is very good.\n\ncolors = cgrad(:rainbow,  length(θRange), categorical=true)\ngr()\n\n# Tip OOP displacement vs. airspeed\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Tip OOP displacement [% semispan]\", xlims=[0,60], ylims=[0,50], legend=:topleft)\nplot!([NaN], [NaN], c=:black, lw=2, ls=:solid, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nplot!([NaN], [NaN], c=:black, lw=2, ls=:dashdot, label=\"UM/NAST\")\nscatter!([NaN], [NaN], c=:black, ms=4, label=\"Exp. (test 1)\")\nscatter!([NaN], [NaN], c=:black, shape=:diamond, ms=4, label=\"Exp. (test 2)\")\nfor (i,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    plot!(URange, tip_OOP[i,:]/L*100, c=colors[i], lw=2, ls=:solid, label=string(\"\\$\\\\alpha_r=\",round(Int,rad2deg(θ)),\"^\\\\circ\\$\"))\n    if i==1\n        scatter!(sqrt.(2*tip_u3Vsq_rootPitch3_ExpTest1[1,:]/ρTest2), tip_u3Vsq_rootPitch3_ExpTest1[2,:]*100/L, mc=colors[i], shape=:diamond, ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\n    elseif i==2\n        plot!(tip_u3VsU_rootPitch5_UMNAST[1,:], tip_u3VsU_rootPitch5_UMNAST[2,:], lw=2, ls=:dashdot, c=colors[i], label=false)\n        scatter!(tip_u3VsU_rootPitch5_Exp[1,:], tip_u3VsU_rootPitch5_Exp[2,:], mc=colors[i], ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\n        scatter!(sqrt.(2*tip_u3Vsq_rootPitch5_ExpTest1[1,:]/ρTest2), tip_u3Vsq_rootPitch5_ExpTest1[2,:]*100/L, mc=colors[i], shape=:diamond, ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\n    elseif i==3\n        plot!(tip_u3VsU_rootPitch7_UMNAST[1,:], tip_u3VsU_rootPitch7_UMNAST[2,:], lw=2, ls=:dashdot, c=colors[i], label=false)\n        scatter!(tip_u3VsU_rootPitch7_Exp[1,:], tip_u3VsU_rootPitch7_Exp[2,:], mc=colors[i], ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\n        scatter!(sqrt.(2*tip_u3Vsq_rootPitch7_ExpTest1[1,:]/ρTest2), tip_u3Vsq_rootPitch7_ExpTest1[2,:]*100/L, mc=colors[i], shape=:diamond, ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\n    end\nend\n\n# Tip twist vs. airspeed\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Tip twist [deg]\", xlims=[0,60], legend=:topleft)\nfor (i,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    plot!(URange, tip_twist[i,:], c=colors[i], lw=2, label=false)\n    if i==2\n        plot!(tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch5_UMNAST[1,:], tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch5_UMNAST[2,:], lw=2, ls=:dashdot, c=colors[i], label=false)\n        scatter!(tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch5_Exp[1,:], tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch5_Exp[2,:], mc=colors[i], ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\n    elseif i==3\n        plot!(tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch7_UMNAST[1,:], tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch7_UMNAST[2,:], lw=2, ls=:dashdot, c=colors[i], label=false)\n        scatter!(tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch7_Exp[1,:], tip_thetaVsU_rootPitch7_Exp[2,:], mc=colors[i], ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\n    end\nend\n\n# Tip AoA vs. airspeed\nplt3 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Tip AoA [deg]\", xlims=[0,60], legend=:bottomright)\nfor (i,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    plot!(URange, tip_AoA[i,:], c=colors[i], lw=2, label=false)\nend\n\n# Tip in-plane displacement vs. airspeed\nplt4 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Tip IP displacement [% semispan]\", xlims=[0,60], legend=:topleft)\nfor (i,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    plot!(URange, tip_IP[i,:]/L*100, c=colors[i], lw=2, label=false)\nend\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\nLet's leverage the built-in function plot_steady_outputs with the appropriate inputs to plot the lift coefficient over the span of the wing.\n\n# Lift coefficient over span for the last problem (θ = θRange[end] and U = URange[end])\nplot_steady_outputs(problem[end],outputs=[\"cl\"],colorScheme=:grays,lw=2)\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\nFinally, we can visualize the deformed state of the wing using the plot_steady_deformation with the appropriate arguments. Notice that by default, gravitational loads are plotted in yellow, and aerodynamic loads in green.\n\n# Deformed state of last problem (θ = θRange[end] and U = URange[end])\ndeformationPlot = plot_steady_deformation(problem[end],view=(45,30),plotBCs=true,plotDistLoads=true)\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Flutter analysis of the Pazy wing","page":"Flutter analysis of the Pazy wing","location":"literate/PazyWingFlutterPitchRange/#Flutter-analysis-of-the-Pazy-wing","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a flutter (eigen) analysis, using the Technion's Pazy wing benchmark. The sectional properties of the wing's spar and the aerodynamic tip loss function were defined by Riso and Cesnik. The data is publicly available at https://github.com/UM-A2SRL/AePW3-LDWG.\n\n(Image: )\n\nPazy wing in the wind tunnel by Avin et al.\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Flutter analysis of the Pazy wing","location":"literate/PazyWingFlutterPitchRange/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting the variables of our problem. In this example we will analyze the flutter onset and offset boundaries of the clamped wing under several root pitch angles, defined by the array θRange.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Pitch angle range [rad]\nθRange = unique([vcat(0:0.25:1)...,vcat(1:0.5:7)...])\n\n# Airspeed range [m/s]\nURange = vcat(1e-3,1:1:120)\n\n# Aerodynamic solver\naeroSolver = Indicial()\n\n# Airfoil section\nairfoil = deepcopy(FlatPlate)\n\n# Flag for tip correction\nhasTipCorrection = true\n\n# Tip correction function type\ntipLossType = \"Exponential\"\n\n# Flag for upright position\nupright = true\n\n# Gravity\ng = 0\n\n# Flag for small angles approximation\nsmallAngles = true\n\n# Fixed geometrical and discretization properties\nnElem,L,chord,normSparPos = geometrical_properties_Pazy()\n\n# Number of modes to track\nnModes = 5\n\n# Eigensolver method and minimum number of eigenvalues to be computed\neigenmethod = :krylovkit\nnev = 90\n\n# Initialize outputs\neigensols = Array{EigenSolution}(undef,length(θRange),length(URange))\ntip_OOP = Array{Float64}(undef,length(θRange),length(URange))\nfreqsOsc = Array{Matrix{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))\ndampsOsc = Array{Matrix{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))\nfreqsNonOsc = Array{Matrix{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))\ndampsNonOsc = Array{Matrix{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))\nflutterOnsetSpeeds = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))\nflutterOnsetFreqs = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))\nflutterOnsetModes = Array{Vector{Int}}(undef,length(θRange))\nflutterOnsetDisp = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))\nflutterOffsetSpeeds = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))\nflutterOffsetFreqs = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))\nflutterOffsetModes = Array{Vector{Int}}(undef,length(θRange))\nflutterOffsetDisp = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(θRange))"},{"title":"Solving the problem","page":"Flutter analysis of the Pazy wing","location":"literate/PazyWingFlutterPitchRange/#Solving-the-problem","category":"section","text":"In the following loops, we create new model instances with the combination of pitch angle and airspeed, create and solve the eigenproblem, and then extract the outputs of interest. The model creation process is streamlined with the function create_Pazy, taking the appropriate inputs. Once the solutions for all airspeeds of each root pitch angle are found, we compute the flutter onset and offset speeds (if any), and the respective tip out-of-plane displacement of the wing for each vibration mode.\n\n# Sweep root angle\nfor (i,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    # Initialize guess solution\n    x0 = zeros(0)\n    # Sweep airspeed\n    for (j,U) in enumerate(URange)\n        # Model\n        PazyWingFlutterPitchRange,_ = create_Pazy(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,airfoil=airfoil,hasTipCorrection=hasTipCorrection,tipLossType=tipLossType,upright=upright,pitchAngle=deg2rad(θ),airspeed=U,gravityVector=[0,0,-g],smallAngles=smallAngles)\n        # Create and solve problem\n        problem = create_EigenProblem(model=PazyWingFlutterPitchRange,eigenmethod=eigenmethod,nev=nev,x0=x0)\n        solve!(problem)\n        # Tip OOP displacement at midchord\n        states = problem.loadstepHistory.states\n        tipTwist = twist_angle(states[end][nElem].nodal.p2_b, outputUnit=:deg)\n        tip_OOP[i,j] = -(states[end][nElem].nodal.u2[1] - chord*(1/2-normSparPos)*sind(tipTwist))/L*100\n        # Eigensolution\n        eigensols[i,j] = problem.eigensolution\n        # Set initial guess solution for next iteration\n        x0 = problem.systemArrays.x\n    end\n    # Apply mode tracking\n    freqs,damps,_ = mode_tracking(eigensols[i,:])\n    rads2Hz!(freqs)\n    # Get frequencies and dampings of oscillatory and non-oscillatory modes\n    freqsOsc[i],dampsOsc[i],_ = filter_tracked_modes(freqs,damps; fmin=1e-1, fmax=150, maxTracks=nModes)\n    freqsNonOsc[i],dampsNonOsc[i],_ = filter_tracked_modes(freqs,damps; fmin=0, fmax=0)\n    # Flutter onset and offset speeds, frequencies and tip OOP displacements\n    flutterOnsetSpeeds[i],flutterOnsetFreqs[i],flutterOnsetModes[i],flutterOnsetDisp[i],flutterOffsetSpeeds[i],flutterOffsetFreqs[i],flutterOffsetModes[i],flutterOffsetDisp[i] = find_flutter(URange,freqsOsc[i],dampsOsc[i],tip_OOP[i,:])\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Flutter analysis of the Pazy wing","location":"literate/PazyWingFlutterPitchRange/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"Post-processing begins by loading the reference experimental data by Drachinski et al. and numerical data by Riso & Cesnik, by Riso & Cesnik and of Sharpy.\n\n# Load reference data\nrootPitchVelUp = [3, 5, 7]\nrootPitchVelDown = [3, 5]\nflutterOnsetVelUp = [49, 43, 38]\nflutterOffsetVelUp = [58, 51, 46]\nflutterOnsetVelDown = [55, 48]\nflutterOffsetVelDown = [40, 36]\nflutterOnsetDispUp = [23, 24.5, 26]\nflutterOnsetDispDown = [28.5, 31.5]\nflutterBoundaryDisp_UMNAST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/flutterBoundaryDisp_UMNAST.txt\")\nflutterBoundaryPitch_UMNAST = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/flutterBoundaryPitch_UMNAST.txt\")\nflutterBoundaryDisp_UMNAST_PanelCoeffs = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/flutterBoundaryDisp_UMNAST_PanelCoeffs.txt\")\nflutterBoundaryPitch_UMNAST_PanelCoeffs = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/Pazy/flutterBoundaryPitch_UMNAST_PanelCoeffs.txt\")\nflutterBoundary_UVsPitch_Lambda0_Sharpy = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/sweptPazy/flutterBoundary_UVsPitch_Lambda0_Sharpy.txt\")\nflutterBoundary_dispVsU_Lambda0_Sharpy = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/sweptPazy/flutterBoundary_dispVsU_Lambda0_Sharpy.txt\")\n\nThe Pazy wing is known from the tests of Drachinski et al. to have a hump flutter mode, that is, a mode that becomes unstable over a certain region of airspeeds. The size of this region is dependent on the root pitch angle of the wing. Let us now plot the flutter boundaries of this hump mode. There is good agreement with the experimental data, but notice that to capture the hysterysis on the flutter onset and offset we would need an analysis more complex than an eigenvalue one. The UM/NAST data by Riso and Cesnik, Riso and Cesnik and the Sharpy results are also plotted for comparison.\n\nts = 10\nfs = 16\nlfs = 9\nlw = 2\nms = 10\nms2 = 4\nmsw = 0\ngr()\n# Flutter onset and offset speeds vs root pitch angle\n# Extract flutter onset and offset data for the hump mode\nhumpMode = 3\nUfOn = Float64[]\nUfOff = Float64[]\nufOn = Float64[]\nufOff = Float64[]\nfor i in eachindex(θRange)\n    for m in eachindex(flutterOnsetModes[i])\n        if flutterOnsetModes[i][m] == humpMode\n            push!(UfOn, flutterOnsetSpeeds[i][m])\n            push!(ufOn, flutterOnsetDisp[i][m])\n            break\n        end\n    end\n    for m in eachindex(flutterOffsetModes[i])\n        if flutterOffsetModes[i][m] == humpMode\n            push!(UfOff, flutterOffsetSpeeds[i][m])\n            push!(ufOff, flutterOffsetDisp[i][m])\n            break\n        end\n    end\nend\n\n# Flutter onset and offset speeds vs root pitch angle\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Root pitch angle [deg]\", xlims=[30,90], ylims=[0,7.25], xticks=collect(30:10:90), yticks=collect(0:1:7), legend=:topright, tickfont=font(ts), guidefont=font(fs), legendfontsize=lfs)\nplot!(Shape(vcat(UfOn,reverse(UfOff)),vcat(θRange,reverse(θRange))), fillcolor = plot_color(:red, 0.25), lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams hump flutter region\")\nscatter!(flutterOnsetVelUp, rootPitchVelUp, shape=:rtriangle, mc=:red, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"Test onset up\")\nscatter!(flutterOffsetVelUp, rootPitchVelUp, shape=:rtriangle, mc=:green, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"Test offset up\")\nscatter!(flutterOnsetVelDown, rootPitchVelDown, shape=:ltriangle, mc=:red, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"Test onset down\")\nscatter!(flutterOffsetVelDown, rootPitchVelDown, shape=:ltriangle, mc=:green, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"Test offset down\")\nplot!(flutterBoundaryPitch_UMNAST[1,:], flutterBoundaryPitch_UMNAST[2,:], c=:olive, ls=:dash, lw=lw, marker=:circle, ms=ms2, msw=msw, label=\"UM/NAST (Exp. loss)\")\nplot!(flutterBoundaryPitch_UMNAST_PanelCoeffs[1,:], flutterBoundaryPitch_UMNAST_PanelCoeffs[2,:], c=:brown, ls=:dash, lw=lw, marker=:circle, ms=ms2, msw=msw, label=\"UM/NAST (Panel coeffs.)\")\nplot!(flutterBoundary_UVsPitch_Lambda0_Sharpy[1,1:19], flutterBoundary_UVsPitch_Lambda0_Sharpy[2,1:19], c=:magenta, ls=:dash, lw=lw, marker=:diamond, ms=ms2, msw=msw, label=\"Sharpy (VLM)\")\n\n# Flutter onset and offset speeds vs tip OOP displacement for varying root pitch angle\nθ2plot = [0.5,1,2,3,5,7]\nindθ2plot = findall(x -> any(isapprox(x, θ; atol=1e-10) for θ in θ2plot), θRange)\nθcolors = cgrad([:blue, :red], length(θ2plot), categorical=true)\nxθ = [ 5,  9, 12, 14, 16, 17.5]\nyθ = [59, 55, 48, 44, 38, 34]\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"Tip OOP displacement [% semispan]\", ylabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", xlims=[0,32], ylims=[30,90], xticks=collect(0:5:30), yticks=collect(30:10:90), legend=:bottomleft, tickfont=font(ts), guidefont=font(fs), legendfontsize=lfs)\nplot!(Shape(vcat(ufOn,reverse(ufOff)),vcat(UfOn,reverse(UfOff))), fillcolor = plot_color(:red, 0.25), lw=lw, label=false)\nfor (n,ind) in enumerate(indθ2plot)\n    θ = θ2plot[n]\n    plot!(tip_OOP[ind,:], URange, c=θcolors[n], ls=:dash, lw=2, label=false)\n    annotate!([xθ[n]],[yθ[n]], text(\"\\$$(round(θ2plot[n],digits=1)) ^\\\\circ\\$\", 10, :bottom, θcolors[n]))\nend\nscatter!(flutterOnsetDispUp, flutterOnsetVelUp, shape=:rtriangle, mc=:red, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\nscatter!(flutterOnsetDispDown, flutterOnsetVelDown, shape=:ltriangle, mc=:red, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\nplot!(flutterBoundaryDisp_UMNAST[1,:], flutterBoundaryDisp_UMNAST[2,:], c=:olive, ls=:dash, lw=lw, marker=:circle, ms=ms2, msw=msw, label=false)\nplot!(flutterBoundaryDisp_UMNAST_PanelCoeffs[1,:], flutterBoundaryDisp_UMNAST_PanelCoeffs[2,:], c=:brown, ls=:dash, lw=lw, marker=:circle, ms=ms2, msw=msw, label=false)\nplot!(flutterBoundary_dispVsU_Lambda0_Sharpy[1,:]*100,flutterBoundary_dispVsU_Lambda0_Sharpy[2,:], c=:magenta, ls=:dash, lw=lw, marker=:diamond, ms=ms2, msw=msw, label=false)\n\n(Image: ) (Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Modal analysis of a two-story frame","page":"Modal analysis of a two-story frame","location":"literate/twoStoryFrame/#Modal-analysis-of-a-two-story-frame","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a modal analysis, using a two-story beam frame. Moreover, it also shows how to assemble a model where the beams are not all connected in sequence. This problem was proposed by Petyt.\n\n(Image: )\n\nGeometry of the two-story frame by Petyt\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem variables","page":"Modal analysis of a two-story frame","location":"literate/twoStoryFrame/#Problem-variables","category":"section","text":"The first step is to define the variables of our problem: geometric and material properties, and discretization. There are two types of beams in the frame: vertical ones have cross-section base b1 and horizontal ones have base b2, but all are made of the same material. We also apply an uniform discretization.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Geometric properties\nL = 1\nb1,b2,h = 5e-2, 15e-2, 5e-2\nA1,Iy1,Iz1 = b1*h, b1*h^3/12, h*b1^3/12\nA2,Iy2,Iz2 = b2*h, b2*h^3/12, h*b2^3/12\nJ1 = A1^4/(Iy1+Iz1)/40\nJ2 = A2^4/(Iy2+Iz2)/40\nKt1,Kt2 = 10/9, 3.0864\n\n# Assembly angles\nα = [0, 0, π/2, 0, 0, π/2, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, π/2, 0, 0, π/2]\nβ = -[π/2, π/2, 0, -π/2, -π/2, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, -π/2, π/2, 0, -π/2, -π/2, 0]\n\n# Material properties\nE = 219.9e9\nν = 0.25\nG = E/(2*(1+ν))\nρ = 7.9e3\n∞ = 1e12\n\n# Stiffness and inertia matrices\nS1 = isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=∞,EA=E*A1,GJ=G*J1*Kt1,EIy=E*Iy1,EIz=E*Iz1)\nS2 = isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=∞,EA=E*A2,GJ=G*J2*Kt2,EIy=E*Iy2,EIz=E*Iz2)\nI1 = inertia_matrix(ρ=ρ,A=A1,Iy=Iy1,Iz=Iz1)\nI2 = inertia_matrix(ρ=ρ,A=A2,Iy=Iy2,Iz=Iz2)\n\nS = [S1, S1, S2, S1, S1, S2, S2, S2, S2, S2, S1, S1, S2, S1, S1, S2]\nI = [I1, I1, I2, I1, I1, I2, I2, I2, I2, I2, I1, I1, I2, I1, I1, I2]\n\n# Number of elements for each beam\nnElem = 10"},{"title":"Beams of the frame","page":"Modal analysis of a two-story frame","location":"literate/twoStoryFrame/#Beams-of-the-frame","category":"section","text":"In the following we create the array of beams. Notice that their disposition in space is defined by the rotation parameters p0 from the body-attached (global) basis mathcalA to the undeformed beam basis b^+. Moreover, since this is a frame, the beams are not all connected in sequence. That means there are connections between beams, which need to be specified. That is done by setting beamConnections (a vector of beam connection pairs) and nodeConnections (a vector of the corresponding node pairs that are connected).\n\n# Beams\nbeams = [create_Beam(\n    name=\"beam$i\",\n    length=L,\n    nElements=nElem,\n    S=[S[i]],\n    I=[I[i]],\n    p0=transform_to_WM([α[i], β[i], 0],from=:E321))\n    for i in 1:16\n    ]\n\n# Beam and node connections\nconnections = [\n    (beams[2] => beams[1], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[3] => beams[2], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[4] => beams[3], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[5] => beams[4], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[6] => beams[1], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[6] => beams[4], nElem+1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[7] => beams[1], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[8] => beams[4], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[9] => beams[2], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[10] => beams[3], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[11] => beams[7], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[12] => beams[7], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[12] => beams[9], nElem+1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[13] => beams[9], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[13] => beams[10], nElem+1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[14] => beams[13], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[14] => beams[8], nElem+1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[15] => beams[8], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[16] => beams[7], 1 => nElem+1),\n    (beams[16] => beams[8], nElem+1 => nElem+1),\n]"},{"title":"Boundary conditions","page":"Modal analysis of a two-story frame","location":"literate/twoStoryFrame/#Boundary-conditions","category":"section","text":"The frame is clamped at the four base points.\n\n# BCs\nclampedBeams = [beams[1],beams[5],beams[11],beams[15]]\nclampedNodes = [1,nElem+1,nElem+1,nElem+1]\nclamps = [create_BC(\n    name=\"clamp$i\",\n    beam=beam,\n    node=node,\n    conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0,:u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\n    for (i,(beam,node)) in enumerate(zip(clampedBeams,clampedNodes))\n    ]"},{"title":"Model","page":"Modal analysis of a two-story frame","location":"literate/twoStoryFrame/#Model","category":"section","text":"Our model is composed of the beams and boundary conditions. We further specify the units, merely for plotting purposes, using the constructor create_UnitsSystem.\n\n# Model\ntwoStoryFrame = create_Model(\n    name=\"twoStoryFrame\",\n    beams=beams,\n    connections=connections,\n    BCs=clamps,\n    units=create_UnitsSystem(length=\"m\",frequency=\"Hz\")\n    )\n\np1 = plotundeformedassembly(twoStoryFrame, view=(30,30)) display(p1)"},{"title":"System solver","page":"Modal analysis of a two-story frame","location":"literate/twoStoryFrame/#System-solver","category":"section","text":"We create a linear system solver, which will be used to solve the eigenproblem.\n\n# System solver\nsolver = create_LinearSolver()"},{"title":"Create and solve problem","page":"Modal analysis of a two-story frame","location":"literate/twoStoryFrame/#Create-and-solve-problem","category":"section","text":"We create and solve the eigenproblem, specifying the model, the linear system solver, the eigensolver method, and the number of eigenvalues to be computed.\n\n# Number of modes to track\nnModes = 4\n\n# Eigensolver method and minimum number of eigenvalues to be computed\neigenmethod = :krylovkit\nnev = 40\n\n# Problem\nproblem = create_EigenProblem(\n    model=twoStoryFrame,\n    systemSolver=solver,\n    eigenmethod=eigenmethod,\n    nev=nev\n    )\nsolve!(problem)"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Modal analysis of a two-story frame","location":"literate/twoStoryFrame/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"We can now extract the frequencies of vibration and compare to the reference finite element solution. The agreement is very good. Notice that only the first and seconding bending mode (swaying modes) in a specific direction are compared, since this problem is symmetric.\n\n# Unpack eigenvalues\nλ = problem.eigensolution.λ\n\n# Get oscillatory frequencies in Hz and keep desired number of modes\nfreqs,_ = filter_tracked_modes(rads2Hz.(imag.(λ)), fmin=eps(), maxTracks=nModes)\n\n# Reference frequencies (in Hz)\nrefFreqs = [11.8, 34.1]\n\n# Display relative errors\nϵ_rel = freqs[[1,4]]./refFreqs .- 1.0\nprintln(\"Relative frequency errors: $(round.(100ϵ_rel, sigdigits=2)) %\")\nRelative frequency errors: [0.58, 0.62] %\n\nWe can also visualize the mode shapes using the function plot_mode_shapes with the appropriate arguments. They compare well to thosed described in the reference.\n\nmodesPlot = plot_mode_shapes(problem,nModes=nModes,view=(45,30),legendPos=(0.4,0.95))\n\n(Image: ) (Image: )\n\nSwaying modes of the two-story frame by Petyt\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Gust response of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","page":"Gust response of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/#Gust-response-of-a-wing-with-flared-folding-wingtip-(FFWT)","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a dynamic gust response analysis for a wing featuring a flared folding wingtip (FFWT). The influence of a one-minus-cosine gust frequency on the behavior of this wing model was studied numerically by Healy's PhD Thesis.\n\n(Image: )\n\nTop view of baseline wing model featuring a FFWT by Healy et al.\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Gust response of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting the variables of our problem. In this example we will analyze the response of the coasting (fold) angle and wing root bending moment (WRBM) to a series of one-minus-cosine gusts with different frequencies (meaning different durations).\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Gust frequency range [Hz]\nωRange = [1,3,10]\n\n# Gust maximum vertical velocity [m/s]\nUg = 0.5\n\n# Hinge configuration\nhingeConfiguration = :free\n\n# Root pitch angle [rad]\nθ = deg2rad(7.5)\n\n# Airspeed [m/s]\nU = 18\n\n# Discretization\nnElementsInner = 16\nnElementsFFWT = 8\nnElem = nElementsInner + nElementsFFWT\n\n# Tip loss options (adjusted to match experimental data)\nhasTipCorrection = true\ntipLossDecayFactor = 17\n\n# Time variables\nΔt = 2.5e-3\ntf = 2\n\n# System solver\nsystemSolver = create_NLsolver(method=:newton,linesearch=true)\n\n# Initialize outputs\ndynamicProblem = Array{DynamicProblem}(undef,length(ωRange))\nt = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(ωRange))\nM2root = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(ωRange))\nϕ = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(ωRange))"},{"title":"Solving the problem","page":"Gust response of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/#Solving-the-problem","category":"section","text":"In the following loop, we create new gust and model instances for each gust frequency, create and solve the initial steady problem to use as the initial solution to the dynamic problem, which yields the time responses of the coasting angle of the FFWT (ϕ) and the out-of-plane bending moment at the root (M2root). The model creation process is streamlined with the function create_HealyBaselineFFWT, taking the appropriate inputs.\n\n# Loop gust frequency range\nfor (i,ω) in enumerate(ωRange)\n    # Display progress\n    # Gust\n    gust = create_OneMinusCosineGust(duration=1/ω,verticalVelocity=Ug)\n    # Model\n    model = create_HealyBaselineFFWT(hingeConfiguration=hingeConfiguration,airspeed=U,pitchAngle=θ,hasTipCorrection=hasTipCorrection,tipLossDecayFactor=tipLossDecayFactor,nElementsInner=nElementsInner,nElementsFFWT=nElementsFFWT,gust=gust)\n    # Solve steady problem for initial conditions\n    steadyProblem = create_SteadyProblem(model=model,systemSolver=systemSolver)\n    solve!(steadyProblem)\n    # Create and solve dynamic problem\n    dynamicProblem[i] = create_DynamicProblem(model=model,finalTime=tf,Δt=Δt,systemSolver=systemSolver,x0=steadyProblem.systemArrays.x)\n            solve!(dynamicProblem[i])\n    # Unpack numerical solution\n    sol = dynamicProblem[i].solutionHistory\n    states = sol.states\n    hacStates = sol.hacStates\n    t[i] = sol.t\n    M2root[i] = [states[j][1].nodal.M1[2] for j in eachindex(t[i])]\n    ϕ[i] = [rad2deg(hacStates[j][1].ϕ) for j in eachindex(t[i])]\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Gust response of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"First, we choose which of Healy's reference model we will use. Then, we load the corresponding data.\n\n# Select reference data (choose between \"baseline\" or \"ASMVLM\")\nrefModel = \"ASMVLM\"\n\n# Load reference data\nΔWRBM_Healy_1Hz = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/DWRBM_\"*refModel*\"_1Hz.txt\")\nΔWRBM_Healy_3Hz = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/DWRBM_\"*refModel*\"_3Hz.txt\")\nΔWRBM_Healy_10Hz = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/DWRBM_\"*refModel*\"_10Hz.txt\")\nfold_Healy_1Hz = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/fold_\"*refModel*\"_1Hz.txt\")\nfold_Healy_3Hz = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/fold_\"*refModel*\"_3Hz.txt\")\nfold_Healy_10Hz = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating/fold_\"*refModel*\"_10Hz.txt\")\n\nWe can now plot the increments in the fold angle and root bending moment of the wing for each of the gusts. The following reference results were taken from Fig. 3.34 of Healy's PhD Thesis. The evolution of the fold angle and root bending moment is closely matched for the first few peaks, but then the correlation with the reference deteriorates somewhat: AeroBeams predicts oscillations with less damping and with a different frequency content. Nevertheless, the maximum loads, which are arguably the most important outputs, are captured with reasonable accuracy.\n\ngr()\ncolors = cgrad(:rainbow, length(ωRange), categorical=true)\nlw = 2\n\n# Fold angle increment\nplt_ϕ = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Fold angle increment [deg]\", ylims=[-20,20], tickfont=font(10), guidefont=font(16), legendfontsize=12, legend=:topright)\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], c=:black, lw=lw, ls=:solid, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nplot!([NaN],[NaN], c=:black, lw=lw, ls=:dashdot, label=\"Healy (2023)\")\nfor (i,ω) in enumerate(ωRange)\n    plot!(t[i], -(ϕ[i] .- ϕ[i][1]), c=colors[i], lw=lw, ls=:solid, label=\"\\$\\\\omega_g = $ω\\$ Hz\")\n    if ω==1\n        plot!(fold_Healy_1Hz[1,:],fold_Healy_1Hz[2,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, ls=:dashdot, label=false)\n    elseif ω==3\n        plot!(fold_Healy_3Hz[1,:],fold_Healy_3Hz[2,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, ls=:dashdot, label=false)\n    else ω==10\n        plot!(fold_Healy_10Hz[1,:],fold_Healy_10Hz[2,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, ls=:dashdot, label=false)\n    end\nend\nsavefig(\"HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating_phi.svg\")\n\n# Root OOP bending moment increment\nplt_ΔWRBM = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"ΔWRBM [N.m]\", tickfont=font(10), guidefont=font(16))\nfor (i,ω) in enumerate(ωRange)\n    plot!(t[i], -(M2root[i] .- M2root[i][1]), c=colors[i], lw=lw, ls=:solid, label=false)\n    if ω==1\n        plot!(ΔWRBM_Healy_1Hz[1,:],ΔWRBM_Healy_1Hz[2,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, ls=:dashdot, label=false)\n    elseif ω==3\n        plot!(ΔWRBM_Healy_3Hz[1,:],ΔWRBM_Healy_3Hz[2,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, ls=:dashdot, label=false)\n    elseif ω==10\n        plot!(ΔWRBM_Healy_10Hz[1,:],ΔWRBM_Healy_10Hz[2,:], c=colors[i], lw=lw, ls=:dashdot, label=false)\n    end\nend\nsavefig(\"HealyBaselineFFWTOMCGustFloating_DWRBM.svg\")\n\n(Image: ) (Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Static structural analysis of composite beams","page":"Static structural analysis of composite beams","location":"literate/compositeCantileverMD/#Static-structural-analysis-of-composite-beams","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a static structural analysis of composite beams. We will investigate the deflection of several composite beams under tip loads caused by attached weights. This problem was proposed by Minguet and Dugundji:\n\n(Image: )\n\nBeam geometry by Minguet and Dugundji\n\n(Image: )\n\nLaminate properties by Minguet and Dugundji\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Static structural analysis of composite beams","location":"literate/compositeCantileverMD/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting up the variables of our problem. We create three beams, each of a different composite laminate, clamp them ar the root, and apply the tip load. Notice that only beam 1 has a diagonal sectional stiffness matrix, whereas the others have coupling terms.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Properties common to all beams (length, in meters, and number of elements)\nL = 0.55\nnElem = 22\n\n# Root rotation angles [rad]\nθRange = π/4*[0, 1]\n\n# Gravity [m/s²]\ng = standard_atmosphere(0).g\n\n# Tip load mass [kg] and weight\nm = 0.5\nW = m*g\n\n# Beam 1\nbeam1 = create_Beam(name=\"beam1\",length=L,nElements=nElem,S=[isotropic_stiffness_matrix(EA=3.7e6,GAy=2.6e5,GAz=2.9e5,GJ=0.183,EIy=0.707,EIz=276)])\n\n# Beam 2\nbeam2 = create_Beam(name=\"beam2\",length=L,nElements=nElem,S=[anisotropic_stiffness_matrix(EA=4e6,GAy=2.6e5,GAz=5.5e5,GJ=0.368,EIy=0.522,EIz=298,c12=-2.7e5,c45=-0.102)])\n\n# Beam 3\nbeam3 = create_Beam(name=\"beam3\",length=L,nElements=nElem,S=[anisotropic_stiffness_matrix(EA=3.9e6,GAy=1.1e6,GAz=1.2e5,GJ=1.18,EIy=0.983,EIz=290,c14=-522)])\n\n# BCs\nclamp1 = create_BC(name=\"clamp1\",beam=beam1,node=1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0, :u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\ntipLoad1 = create_BC(name=\"tipLoad1\",beam=beam1,node=nElem+1,conditions=Dict(:F3A=>-W))\nclamp2 = create_BC(name=\"clamp2\",beam=beam2,node=1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0, :u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\ntipLoad2 = create_BC(name=\"tipLoad2\",beam=beam2,node=nElem+1,conditions=Dict(:F3A=>-W))\nclamp3 = create_BC(name=\"clamp3\",beam=beam3,node=1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0, :u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\ntipLoad3 = create_BC(name=\"tipLoad3\",beam=beam3,node=nElem+1,conditions=Dict(:F3A=>-W))\n\n# Models\ncompositeCantilever1 = create_Model(name=\"compositeCantilever1\",beams=[beam1],BCs=[clamp1,tipLoad1])\ncompositeCantilever2 = create_Model(name=\"compositeCantilever2\",beams=[beam2],BCs=[clamp2,tipLoad2])\ncompositeCantilever3 = create_Model(name=\"compositeCantilever3\",beams=[beam3],BCs=[clamp3,tipLoad3])\n\n# Set system solver options\nσ0 = 0\nσstep = 0.1\nNR = create_NLsolver(initLoadFac=σ0,maxLoadFacStep=σstep)\n\n# Initialize outputs: displacements at x1 = 500 mm\nσVec = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,3,2)\nu1_500mm = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,3,2)\nu2_500mm = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,3,2)\nu3_500mm = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,3,2)"},{"title":"Problem solution","page":"Static structural analysis of composite beams","location":"literate/compositeCantileverMD/#Problem-solution","category":"section","text":"We can sweep the range of root rotation angle, θRange, to find the solution at each value for each beam.\n\n# Loop root angles\nfor (j,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n    # Update beams' rotation\n    update_beam_orientation!(compositeCantilever1,beam=beam1,p0=transform_to_WM([0;0;θ];from=:E321))\n    update_beam_orientation!(compositeCantilever2,beam=beam2,p0=transform_to_WM([0;0;θ];from=:E321))\n    update_beam_orientation!(compositeCantilever3,beam=beam3,p0=transform_to_WM([0;0;θ];from=:E321))\n    # Create and solve the problems\n    problem1 = create_SteadyProblem(model=compositeCantilever1,systemSolver=NR)\n    solve!(problem1)\n    problem2 = create_SteadyProblem(model=compositeCantilever2,systemSolver=NR)\n    solve!(problem2)\n    problem3 = create_SteadyProblem(model=compositeCantilever3,systemSolver=NR)\n    solve!(problem3)\n    # Save outputs\n    hist1 = problem1.loadstepHistory\n    hist2 = problem2.loadstepHistory\n    hist3 = problem3.loadstepHistory\n    stt1 = hist1.states\n    stt2 = hist2.states\n    stt3 = hist3.states\n    σVec[1,j] = hist1.σ\n    σVec[2,j] = hist2.σ\n    σVec[3,j] = hist3.σ\n    u1_500mm[1,j] = [stt1[i][nElem-1].nodal.u1[1] for i in 1:length(σVec[1,j])]\n    u1_500mm[2,j] = [stt2[i][nElem-1].nodal.u1[1] for i in 1:length(σVec[2,j])]\n    u1_500mm[3,j] = [stt3[i][nElem-1].nodal.u1[1] for i in 1:length(σVec[3,j])]\n    u2_500mm[1,j] = [stt1[i][nElem-1].nodal.u1[2] for i in 1:length(σVec[1,j])]\n    u2_500mm[2,j] = [stt2[i][nElem-1].nodal.u1[2] for i in 1:length(σVec[2,j])]\n    u2_500mm[3,j] = [stt3[i][nElem-1].nodal.u1[2] for i in 1:length(σVec[3,j])]\n    u3_500mm[1,j] = [stt1[i][nElem-1].nodal.u1[3] for i in 1:length(σVec[1,j])]\n    u3_500mm[2,j] = [stt2[i][nElem-1].nodal.u1[3] for i in 1:length(σVec[2,j])]\n    u3_500mm[3,j] = [stt3[i][nElem-1].nodal.u1[3] for i in 1:length(σVec[3,j])]\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Static structural analysis of composite beams","location":"literate/compositeCantileverMD/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"Let's load the reference experimental solution by Minguet and Dugundji.\n\n# Load reference solution\nu1_b1_th0_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b1_th0_u1.txt\")\nu2_b1_th0_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b1_th0_u2.txt\")\nu3_b1_th0_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b1_th0_u3.txt\")\nu1_b1_th45_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b1_th45_u1.txt\")\nu2_b1_th45_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b1_th45_u2.txt\")\nu3_b1_th45_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b1_th45_u3.txt\")\nu1_b2_th0_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b2_th0_u1.txt\")\nu2_b2_th0_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b2_th0_u2.txt\")\nu3_b2_th0_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b2_th0_u3.txt\")\nu1_b2_th45_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b2_th45_u1.txt\")\nu2_b2_th45_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b2_th45_u2.txt\")\nu3_b2_th45_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b2_th45_u3.txt\")\nu1_b3_th0_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b3_th0_u1.txt\")\nu2_b3_th0_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b3_th0_u2.txt\")\nu3_b3_th0_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b3_th0_u3.txt\")\nu1_b3_th45_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b3_th45_u1.txt\")\nu2_b3_th45_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b3_th45_u2.txt\")\nu3_b3_th45_ref = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/compositeCantileverMD/b3_th45_u3.txt\")\n\nFinally, we may plot the displacements at x_1 = 500 mm for each beam in each configuration. The correlation with the experimental data is good, and matches the numerical results of Minguet and Dugundji as well (not shown).\n\ngr()\n\n# Plot configurations\nlabels = [\"\\$-u_1\\$\" \"\\$u_2\\$\" \"\\$-u_3\\$\"]\nxLabel = \"\\$-u_1, u_2, -u_3\\$ [m]\"\nyLabel = \"Load [grams]\"\ncolors = [:blue,:orange,:green]\nlw = 2\nms = 5\nmsw = 0\n\n# Beam 1, θ=0⁰\nplt11 = plot(xlabel=xLabel, ylabel=yLabel, title=\"Beam 1, \\$\\\\theta=0^{\\\\degree}\\$\")\nplot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN], [NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, label=\" Experimental\")\nfor i=1:3\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], c=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=labels[i])\nend\nfor (x, c) in zip([-u1_500mm[1,1], u2_500mm[1,1], -u3_500mm[1,1]], colors)\n    plot!(x, σVec[1,1]*W/g*1e3, c=c, lw=lw, label=false)\nend\nfor (x, y, c) in zip([u1_b1_th0_ref[1,:], u2_b1_th0_ref[1,:], u3_b1_th0_ref[1,:]], [u1_b1_th0_ref[2,:], u2_b1_th0_ref[2,:], u3_b1_th0_ref[2,:]], colors)\n    scatter!(x, y, c=c, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\nend\n\n# Beam 1, θ=45⁰\nplt12 = plot(xlabel=xLabel, ylabel=yLabel, title=\"Beam 1, \\$\\\\theta=45^{\\\\degree}\\$\")\nplot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN], [NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, label=\"  Experimental\")\nfor i=1:3\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], c=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=labels[i])\nend\nfor (x, c) in zip([-u1_500mm[1,2], u2_500mm[1,2], -u3_500mm[1,2]], colors)\n    plot!(x, σVec[1,2]*W/g*1e3, c=c, lw=lw, label=false)\nend\nfor (x, y, c) in zip([u1_b1_th45_ref[1,:], u2_b1_th45_ref[1,:], u3_b1_th45_ref[1,:]], [u1_b1_th45_ref[2,:], u2_b1_th45_ref[2,:], u3_b1_th45_ref[2,:]], colors)\n    scatter!(x, y, c=c, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\nend\n\n# Beam 2, θ=0⁰\nplt21 = plot(xlabel=xLabel, ylabel=yLabel, title=\"Beam 2, \\$\\\\theta=0^{\\\\degree}\\$\")\nplot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN], [NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, label=\"  Experimental\")\nfor i=1:3\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], c=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=labels[i])\nend\nfor (x, c) in zip([-u1_500mm[2,1], u2_500mm[2,1], -u3_500mm[2,1]], colors)\n    plot!(x, σVec[2,1]*W/g*1e3, c=c, lw=lw, label=false)\nend\nfor (x, y, c) in zip([u1_b2_th0_ref[1,:], u2_b2_th0_ref[1,:], u3_b2_th0_ref[1,:]], [u1_b2_th0_ref[2,:], u2_b2_th0_ref[2,:], u3_b2_th0_ref[2,:]], colors)\n    scatter!(x, y, c=c, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\nend\n\n# Beam 2, θ=45⁰\nplt22 = plot(xlabel=xLabel, ylabel=yLabel, title=\"Beam 2, \\$\\\\theta=45^{\\\\degree}\\$\", legend=:bottomright)\nplot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN], [NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, label=\"  Experimental\")\nfor i=1:3\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], c=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=labels[i])\nend\nfor (x, c) in zip([-u1_500mm[2,2], u2_500mm[2,2], -u3_500mm[2,2]], colors)\n    plot!(x, σVec[2,2]*W/g*1e3, c=c, lw=lw, label=false)\nend\nfor (x, y, c) in zip([u1_b2_th45_ref[1,:], u2_b2_th45_ref[1,:], u3_b2_th45_ref[1,:]], [u1_b2_th45_ref[2,:], u2_b2_th45_ref[2,:], u3_b2_th45_ref[2,:]], colors)\n    scatter!(x, y, c=c, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\nend\n\n# Beam 3, θ=0⁰\nplt31 = plot(xlabel=xLabel, ylabel=yLabel, title=\"Beam 3, \\$\\\\theta=0^{\\\\degree}\\$\")\nplot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN], [NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, label=\"  Experimental\")\nfor i=1:3\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], c=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=labels[i])\nend\nfor (x, c) in zip([-u1_500mm[3,1], u2_500mm[3,1], -u3_500mm[3,1]], colors)\n    plot!(x, σVec[3,1]*W/g*1e3, c=c, lw=lw, label=false)\nend\nfor (x, y, c) in zip([u1_b3_th0_ref[1,:], u2_b3_th0_ref[1,:], u3_b3_th0_ref[1,:]], [u1_b3_th0_ref[2,:], u2_b3_th0_ref[2,:], u3_b3_th0_ref[2,:]], colors)\n    scatter!(x, y, c=c, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\nend\n\n# Beam 3, θ=45⁰\nplt32 = plot(xlabel=xLabel, ylabel=yLabel, title=\"Beam 3, \\$\\\\theta=45^{\\\\degree}\\$\")\nplot!([NaN], [NaN], lc=:black, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN], [NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, label=\"  Experimental\")\nfor i=1:3\n    plot!([NaN], [NaN], c=colors[i], m=colors[i], lw=lw, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=labels[i])\nend\nfor (x, c) in zip([-u1_500mm[3,2], u2_500mm[3,2], -u3_500mm[3,2]], colors)\n    plot!(x, σVec[3,2]*W/g*1e3, c=c, lw=lw, label=false)\nend\nfor (x, y, c) in zip([u1_b3_th45_ref[1,:], u2_b3_th45_ref[1,:], u3_b3_th45_ref[1,:]], [u1_b3_th45_ref[2,:], u2_b3_th45_ref[2,:], u3_b3_th45_ref[2,:]], colors)\n    scatter!(x, y, c=c, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=false)\nend\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Flutter and divergence of a typical aeroelastic section","page":"Flutter and divergence of a typical aeroelastic section","location":"literate/typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence/#Flutter-and-divergence-of-a-typical-aeroelastic-section","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a flutter/divergence analysis of a 2-DOF typical aeroelastic section. The typical section used was defined by Hodges and Pierce.\n\n(Image: ) Typical section\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Flutter and divergence of a typical aeroelastic section","location":"literate/typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting up the variables of our problem: we'll model the 2-DOF (pitch and plunge) typical section, which is a two-dimensional idealization, by setting up a rigid wing supported by displacement and rotation springs. We leverage the function typical_section_data to extract the typical section parameters.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Aerodynamic solver\naeroSolver = Inflow()\n\n# Atmosphere\naltitude = 0\natmosphere = standard_atmosphere(altitude)\nρ = atmosphere.ρ\n\n# Given typical section data\ntypicalSectionName = \"HP-1\"\nairfoil = deepcopy(FlatPlate)\na,e,μ,rα²,σ,ωα,c = typical_section_data(typicalSectionName)\n\n# Derived typical section data: semichord, mass per unit length, pitching inertia, distance from elastic axis to mass axis, plunge stiffness and pitch stiffness\nb = c/2\nm = μ * π*ρ*b^2\nIα = rα²*m*b^2\ne2 = -(e-a)*b\nkh = m*(σ*ωα)^2\nkα = Iα*ωα^2\n\n# Number of elements for the wing (the results are independent of it)\nnElem = 2\n\nWe now create the aerodynamic surface to be attached to the wing's beam, the attachment springs, and the rigid beam of the wing. Two sliding journals (allowing only pitch and plunge motion) are added at the wingtips, further removing any elastic effects of the beam.\n\n# Aerodynamic surface\nsurf = create_AeroSurface(ppSolver=aeroSolver,airfoil=airfoil,c=c,normSparPos=(a+1)/2,updateAirfoilParameters=false)\n\n# Rigid wing\nL = 1\n∞ = 1e10\nwing = create_Beam(name=\"wing\",length=L,nElements=nElem,S=[isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=∞)],I=[inertia_matrix(ρA=m,ρIy=Iα/2,ρIz=Iα/2,e2=e2)],surface=surf)\n\n# Attachment springs (in pitch and plunge)\npitchSpring = create_Spring(kp=kα)\nplungeSpring = create_Spring(ku=kh)\nadd_spring_to_beam!(beam=wing,spring=plungeSpring,nodeID=div(nElem,2)+1,p=[0,π/2,0])\nadd_spring_to_beam!(beam=wing,spring=pitchSpring,nodeID=div(nElem,2)+1)\n\n# BCs\njournal1 = create_BC(name=\"journal-1\",beam=wing,node=1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0,:u2A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\njournal2 = create_BC(name=\"journal-2\",beam=wing,node=nElem+1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0,:u2A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\n\nThe model consists of the beam and boundary conditions. We also select the airspeed range for analysis, the number of oscillatory modes to be extracted and initialize the outputs.\n\n# Model\ntypicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence = create_Model(name=\"typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence\",beams=[wing],BCs=[journal1,journal2])\n\n# Airspeed range\nURangeNorm = collect(0.025:0.025:3)\nURange = b * ωα * URangeNorm\n\n# Number of modes to track\nnModesOsc = 2\nnModesNonOsc = 1\n\n# Eigensolver method and minimum number of eigenvalues to be computed\neigenmethod = :krylovkit\nnev = 20\n\n# Initialize outputs\neigensols = Array{EigenSolution}(undef,length(URange))"},{"title":"Problem solution","page":"Flutter and divergence of a typical aeroelastic section","location":"literate/typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence/#Problem-solution","category":"section","text":"We're now ready to solve the eigenproblem at each airspeed value.\n\n# Initialize guess solution\nx0 = zeros(0)\n# Sweep airspeed\nfor (i,U) in enumerate(URange)\n    # Update airspeed on model\n    update_v_A!(typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence,v_A=[0,U,0])\n    # Create and solve eigenproblem\n    problem = create_EigenProblem(model=typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence,eigenmethod=eigenmethod,nev=nev,x0=x0)\n    solve!(problem)\n    # Eigensolution\n    eigensols[i] = problem.eigensolution\n    # Set initial guess solution for next iteration\n    global x0 = problem.systemArrays.x\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Flutter and divergence of a typical aeroelastic section","location":"literate/typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"The first post-processing step is to use the built-in mode tracking algorithm to enhance our chances of correctly tracking modes, while also extracting frequencies and dampings from the eigensolution. Then, we filter the tracked modes into oscillatory and non-oscillatory categories. With that, we can find the flutter and divergence variables of interest.\n\n# Apply mode tracking\nfreqs,damps,_ = mode_tracking(eigensols)\n\n# Get frequencies and dampings of oscillatory and non-oscillatory modes\nfreqsOsc,dampsOsc,_ = filter_tracked_modes(freqs,damps; fmin=eps(), fmax=1.5*ωα, maxTracks=nModesOsc, dropOutsideBounds=true)\nfreqsNonOsc,dampsNonOsc,_ = filter_tracked_modes(freqs,damps; fmin=0, fmax=0, maxTracks=nModesNonOsc)\n\n# Flutter speed and frequency\nflutterOnsetSpeeds,flutterOnsetFreqs,_ = find_flutter(URange,freqsOsc,dampsOsc)\nflutterSpeed = minimum(flutterOnsetSpeeds)\nflutterFreq = minimum(flutterOnsetFreqs)\n\n# Divergence speed\ndivergenceSpeeds,divergenceMode,_ = find_divergence(URange,freqsNonOsc,dampsNonOsc)\ndivergenceSpeed = minimum(divergenceSpeeds)\n\nWe also load a reference solution obtained using the p method for stability analysis using the unsteady aerodynamic theory of Peters et al., as proposed in problem 5.7 of Hodges and Pierce.\n\n# Load reference data\nfreqsRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence/freqs.txt\")\ndampsRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence/damps.txt\")\n\nWe'll visualize the results through the V-g-f diagrams, i.e., the evolution of the frequencies and dampings (both normalized by the in vacuo pitching frequency) with airspeed. The flutter speed is that at which the damping of an oscillatory mode, in this case the pitch mode, becomes positive. Conversely, the divergence speed is that at which the damping of a non-oscillatory mode becomes positive. The results match almost exactly.\n\ngr()\n\n# Plot configurations\nmodeColors = cgrad(:rainbow, nModesOsc+nModesNonOsc, categorical=true)\nmodeLabels = [\"Plunge\", \"Pitch\", \"Non-oscillatory\"]\nlw = 3\nmsw = 0\n\n# Frequency plot\nplt11 = plot(ylabel=\"Frequency ratio\", xlims=extrema(URangeNorm), ylims=[0,1.02])\nfor mode in 1:nModesOsc\n    plot!(URangeNorm, freqsOsc[mode,:]/ωα, c=modeColors[mode], lw=lw, label=false)\nend\nplot!(freqsRef[1,:], freqsRef[2,:], ls=:dash, lw=1, marker=:circle, ms=2, msw=msw, c=:black, label=false)\n# Damping plot\nplt12 = plot(xlabel=\"Normalized airspeed, \\$U/b\\\\omega_\\\\alpha\\$\", ylabel=\"Damping ratio\", xlims=extrema(URangeNorm), ylims=[-0.15,0.15], legend=:topleft)\nfor mode in 1:nModesOsc\n    plot!(URangeNorm, dampsOsc[mode,:]/ωα, c=modeColors[mode], lw=lw, label=modeLabels[mode])\nend\nfor mode in 1:nModesNonOsc\n    plot!(URangeNorm, dampsNonOsc[mode,:]/ωα, c=modeColors[nModesOsc+mode], lw=lw, label=modeLabels[nModesOsc+mode])\nend\nplot!(dampsRef[1,:], dampsRef[2,:], ls=:dash, lw=1, marker=:circle, ms=2, msw=msw, c=:black, label=\"Hodges & Pierce (2011) - \\$p\\$ method\")\nplt1 = plot(plt11, plt12, layout=(2,1))\n\n(Image: )\n\nFinally, we show the numerical values of the nondimensional flutter speed and frequency. The values computed by Hodges & Pierce are 2.165 and 0.6545, respectively.\n\n# Nondimensional speeds and frequencies\nUd = divergenceSpeed / (b * ωα)\nUf = flutterSpeed / (b * ωα)\nωf = flutterFreq / ωα\n\n# Reference values\nUfRef = 2.165\nωfRef  = 0.6545\n\n# Relative errors (%)\nϵUf = 100 * (Uf./UfRef .- 1)\nϵωf  = 100 * (ωf./ωfRef .- 1)\n\n# Pretty table of results\ndata = [\n    \"Divergence speed\"   Ud  missing missing\n    \"Flutter speed\"      Uf  UfRef   ϵUf\n    \"Flutter frequency\"  ωf  ωfRef   ϵωf\n]\npretty_table(\n    data;\n    column_labels = [\"\", \"AeroBeams\", \"Hodges & Pierce\", \"Δ [%]\"],\n    title         = \"Pitch-plunge typical section\",\n    formatters    = [\n        (v, i, j) -> (v isa Number) ? round(v, sigdigits=4) : v,\n        (v, i, j) -> (j == 4 && v isa Number) ? string(round(v, digits=2), \" %\") : v,\n    ],\n    source_notes  = \"Reference: Hodges & Pierce, Introduction to Structural Dynamics and Aeroelasticity\",\n)\n                Pitch-plunge typical section\n┌───────────────────┬───────────┬─────────────────┬─────────┐\n│                   │ AeroBeams │ Hodges & Pierce │   Δ [%] │\n├───────────────────┼───────────┼─────────────────┼─────────┤\n│  Divergence speed │     2.828 │         missing │ missing │\n│     Flutter speed │     2.168 │           2.165 │  0.14 % │\n│ Flutter frequency │    0.6541 │          0.6545 │ -0.06 % │\n└───────────────────┴───────────┴─────────────────┴─────────┘\nReference: Hodges & Pierce, Introduction to Structural Dynamics and Aeroelasti ⋯\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Index","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Index","category":"section","text":"Pages = [\"publicAPI.md\"]"},{"title":"Public API","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Public-API","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating an aerodynamic derivatives solver","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-an-aerodynamic-derivatives-solver","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating an aerodynamic solver","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-an-aerodynamic-solver","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating an airfoil","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-an-airfoil","category":"section","text":"The following airfoils are predefined: FlatPlate, NACA0002, NACA0012, NACA0015, NACA0018, NACA23012A, VERTOL23010, HeliosWingAirfoil, HeliosPodAirfoil, BWBAirfoil and cHALEairfoil. Use deepcopy to get an independent copy before modifying one of them."},{"title":"Creating an aerodynamic surface","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-an-aerodynamic-surface","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating an atmosphere","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-an-atmosphere","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating boundary conditions","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-boundary-conditions","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a beam","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-beam","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a gust","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-gust","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a link","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-link","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a model","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-model","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a point inertia","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-point-inertia","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a hinge axis constraint","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-hinge-axis-constraint","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating and solving a problem","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-and-solving-a-problem","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a sample model","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-sample-model","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a spring","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-spring","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a system solver","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-system-solver","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Creating a units system","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Creating-a-units-system","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Utilities","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Utilities","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Visualizing the results","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#Visualizing-the-results","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"AeroBeams.AD","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.AD","category":"type","text":"AD (Automatic Differentiation) DerivationMethod composite type\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.FD","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.FD","category":"type","text":"FD (Finite Differences) DerivationMethod composite type\n\nFields\n\nmethod::FiniteDifferenceMethod\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.QuasiSteady","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.QuasiSteady","category":"type","text":"QuasiSteady AeroSolver composite type\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.Indicial","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.Indicial","category":"type","text":"Indicial AeroSolver composite type\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.BLi","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.BLi","category":"type","text":"Incompressible modified Beddoes-Leishman AeroSolver composite type\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.BLo","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.BLo","category":"type","text":"Original Beddoes-Leishman AeroSolver composite type\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.Inflow","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.Inflow","category":"type","text":"Inflow AeroSolver composite type\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.TableLookup","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.TableLookup","category":"type","text":"TableLookup FlapAeroSolver composite type\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.ThinAirfoilTheory","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.ThinAirfoilTheory","category":"type","text":"ThinAirfoilTheory FlapAeroSolver composite type\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.IndicialGust","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.IndicialGust","category":"type","text":"IndicialGust GustAeroSolver composite type\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_FlapParameters","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_FlapParameters","category":"function","text":"create_FlapParameters(cdδ, cmδ, cnδ)\n\nCreates a flap parameter set for a TableLookup FlapAeroSolver\n\nArguments\n\ncdδ::Real: change in drag coefficient due to unit flap deflection\ncmδ::Real: change in moment coefficient due to unit flap deflection\ncnδ::Real: change in normal force coefficient due to unit flap deflection\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_Airfoil","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_Airfoil","category":"function","text":"create_Airfoil(; kwargs...)\n\nAirfoil constructor\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nname::Union{Missing,String}: name of the airfoil\ndatabase::Union{Missing,AirfoilDatabase}: airfoil database\nMa::Real: Mach number\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_all_airfoil_parameters!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_all_airfoil_parameters!","category":"function","text":"update_all_airfoil_parameters!(airfoil,Ma)\n\nUpdate all airfoil parameters based on the current Mach number.\n\nArguments\n\nairfoil::Airfoil: airfoil object to update\nMa::Real: Mach number\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.modified_database","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.modified_database","category":"function","text":"modified_database(name; attached,bli,blo,flap)\n\nCreates a new airfoil database by modifying selected parameters of an existing one.\n\nScalar values are automatically expanded to all Mach numbers.\n\nArguments\n\nname::String: name of the airfoil whose database will be modified\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nattached::NamedTuple: named tuple of attached flow parameters to modify\nbli::NamedTuple: named tuple of BLi parameters to modify\nblo::NamedTuple: named tuple of BLo parameters to modify\nflap::NamedTuple: named tuple of flap parameters to modify\n\nExample\n\n\n\ndb = modifieddatabase(     \"FlatPlate\";     attached = (         cd₀ = 0.015,         cnα = 6.1,     ),     bli = (         α₀N = deg2rad(2.0),     ),     flap = (         75 => createFlapParameters(0.30,-0.22,1.05),     ) )\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_AeroSurface","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_AeroSurface","category":"function","text":"create_AeroSurface(; kwargs...)\n\nAerodynamic surface constructor\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nppSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic ppSolver for pitch-plunge-induced loads\nflapSolver::FlapAeroSolver: aerodynamic ppSolver for flap-induced loads\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: aerodynamic ppSolver for gust-induced loads\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for calculation of aerodynamic derivatives\nairfoil::Airfoil: airfoil section\nc::Union{<:Function,Real}: chord\nΛ::Union{<:Function,Real}: sweep angle [rad]\nφ::Union{<:Function,Real}: twist angle [rad]\nnormSparPos::Union{<:Function,Real}: normalized position of the spar (beam reference line) on the chord\nnormFlapSpan::Union{Nothing,Vector{<:Real}}: normalized position of the trailing-edge flap along the span (beam arclength)\nnormFlapPos::Union{Nothing,Real}: normalized position of the trailing-edge flap hinge on the chord\nδIsTrimVariable::Bool: flag for trailing-edge deflection being a trim variable\nδ::Union{Nothing,<:Function,Real}: trailing-edge deflection [rad]\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters with local airspeed\nhasTipCorrection::Bool: flag to employ a tip correction on 2D aerodynamic coefficients\ntipLossFunctionIsAirspeedDependent::Bool: flag for tip loss function being airspeed dependent\ntipLossFunction::Union{Nothing,<:Function}: corresponding tip loss function\ntipLossDecayFactor::Real: corresponding tip loss factor\nsmallAngles::Bool: flag to employ small angles approximation on the calculation of the angle of attack\nhasInducedDrag::Bool: flag to include induced drag generated by the lifting surface\nhasSymmetricCounterpart::Bool: flag to indicate the surface has a symmetric counterpart (for the computation of aspect ratio and induced drag)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.standard_atmosphere","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.standard_atmosphere","category":"function","text":"standard_atmosphere(altitude::Real)\n\nAtmosphere constructor based on the International Standard Atmosphere (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/InternationalStandardAtmosphere)\n\nArguments\n\naltitude::Real: altitude\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.air_properties_from_pressure_and_temperature","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.air_properties_from_pressure_and_temperature","category":"function","text":"air_properties_from_pressure_and_temperature(p::Real,T::Real,R::Real,γ::Real,β::Real,S::Real)\n\nCalculates air properties from given pressure and temperature.\n\nArguments\n\np::Real: pressure\nT::Real: temperature\nR::Real: specific gas constant of air (default: 287.05864074988347)\nγ::Real: specific heat ratio (default: 1.4)\nβ::Real: Sutherland's law constant (default: 1.458e-6)\nS::Real: Sutherland's law constant (default: 110.4)\n\nReturns\n\nρ::Float64: air density\nμ::Float64: dynamic viscosity\na::Float64: speed of sound\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_BC","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_BC","category":"function","text":"create_BC(; kwargs...)\n\nBC constructor\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nname::String: name of the BC\nbeam::Beam: beam at which the BC is applied\nnode::Int: node of the beam at which the BC is applied\nconditions::Dict{Symbol,<:Any}: BC definition where each key is a BC type symbol and each value is either a scalar constant or a function of time returning a scalar\ntoBeTrimmed::AbstractVector{Bool}: flag vector on whether the BC is to be trimmed\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.reset_BC_beam!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.reset_BC_beam!","category":"function","text":"reset_BC_beam!(bc::BC, beam::Beam)\n\nUpdates the beam of an existing BC and recomputes beam-dependent BC data\n\nArguments\n\nbc::BC: BC to update\nbeam::Beam: new beam associated with the BC\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_Beam","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_Beam","category":"function","text":"create_Beam(; kwargs...)\n\nBeam constructor\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nname::String: name of the beam\nlength::Union{Missing,<:Real}: (arc)length of the beam\nr::Union{Missing,AbstractVector{<:Real}}: position vector of the beam's endpoint relative to the assembly's origin (basis A)\nk::AbstractVector{<:Real}: undeformed beam's curvature vector per unit length\np0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wiener-Milenkovic rotation parameters from basis A to basis b(0)\nS::AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}: array of sectional stiffness matrices\nI::AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}: array of sectional inertia matrices\nnElements::Int: number of elements for discretization\nnormalizedNodalPositions::AbstractVector{<:Real}: normalized nodal positions of beam elements\nhingedNodes::AbstractVector{Int}: nodes with a hinge\nhingedNodesDoF::AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{Bool}}: respective hinged degrees-of-freedom\nsurface::Union{Nothing,AeroSurface}: attached aerodynamic surface\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_beam_orientation!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_beam_orientation!","category":"function","text":"update_beam_orientation!(; beam, p0)\n\nUpdates the orientation of the beam\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\np0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wiener-Milenkovic rotation parameters from basis A to basis b(0)\n\n\n\n\n\nupdate_beam_orientation!(model; beam, p0)\n\nUpdates the assembly data of the model, after changing the initial orientation of a beam\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: the beam to update\np0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: new initial Wiener-Milenkovic rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.add_distributed_loads_to_beam!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.add_distributed_loads_to_beam!","category":"function","text":"add_distributed_loads_to_beam!(; beam, loads)\n\nAdds loads to the beam\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\nloads::Dict{Symbol,<:Function}: dictionary whose keys are load types and values are the respective load functions\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.add_initial_conditions_to_beam!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.add_initial_conditions_to_beam!","category":"function","text":"add_initial_conditions_to_beam!(; beam,conditions)\n\nAdds initial generalized displacements and velocities to the beam. Any combination of them can be specified: the components that are not specified (null) are obtained from the specified ones through the governing equations, so that the initial states of the dynamic problem are always consistent. In particular, the initial states are such that:\n\nThe specified displacements and rotations are satisfied, and the unspecified ones are those of the static equilibrium (which makes the initial rotations compatible with the initial displacements, and vice-versa)\nThe specified velocities are satisfied, and the unspecified ones follow the specified ones as in a quasi-static motion (e.g., the angular velocities that accompany specified displacements' rates, and vice-versa)\nThe initial accelerations satisfy the equations of motion\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\nconditions::Dict{Symbol,<:Function}: dictionary whose keys are initial condition types (:u0_of_x1, :p0_of_x1, :udot0_of_x1 and :pdot0_of_x1) and values are the respective functions over arclength. These return the initial displacements, rotation parameters and their rates, respectively, resolved in the undeformed beam basis (b)\n\n\n\n\n\nadd_initial_conditions_to_beam!(model; beam,conditions)\n\nUpdates the initial states of a model's elements and nodes, after adding initial generalized displacements and velocities to a beam of the model\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\nconditions::Dict{Symbol,<:Function}: dictionary whose keys are initial condition types and values are the respective functions over arclength\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.add_point_inertia_to_beam!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.add_point_inertia_to_beam!","category":"function","text":"add_point_inertia_to_beam!(; beam,elementID,pointInertia)\n\nAdds a point inertia to a particular element of the beam\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\nelementID::Int: local ID (on the beam) of the element to which the point inertia is attached\npointInertia::PointInertia: point inertia\n\n\n\n\n\nadd_point_inertia_to_beam!(model; beam,elementID,pointInertia)\n\nUpdates the model after adding point inertias to the beam\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\nelementID::Int: local ID (on the beam) of the element to which the point inertia is attached\npointInertia::PointInertia: point inertia\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.add_spring_to_beam!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.add_spring_to_beam!","category":"function","text":"add_spring_to_beam!(; beam, springs, nodeID, p, basis)\n\nAdds a simply-attached spring to a beam\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\nspring::Spring: spring\nnodeID::Int: local ID (on the beam) of the node to which the spring is attached\nΔu0::Real: translational pre-deformation\nΔp0::Real: rotational pre-deformation\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler 3-2-1 rotation parameters from basis b of the attachment node to the spring's basis s\nbasis::Symbol: basis in which the spring's attachment is defined, either :b or :A\n\n\n\n\n\nadd_spring_to_beam!(model; beam,spring,nodeID,Δu0,Δp0,p)\n\nUpdates the model after adding simply-attached springs to a beam\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\nspring::Spring: spring\nnodeID::Int: local ID (on the beam) of the node to which the spring is attached\nΔu0::Real: translational pre-deformation\nΔp0::Real: rotational pre-deformation\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler 3-2-1 rotation parameters from basis b of the attachment node to the spring's basis s\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.add_spring_to_beams!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.add_spring_to_beams!","category":"function","text":"add_spring_to_beams!(; beams, spring, nodesLocalIDs, p, Δu0, Δp0, basis)\n\nAdds a doubly-attached spring to the beams\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::Vector{Beam}: reference beams\nspring::Spring: doubly-attached spring\nnodesLocalIDs: local IDs (on the respective beams) of the nodes to which the spring is attached\nΔu0::Real: translational pre-deformation\nΔp0::Real: rotational pre-deformation\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler 3-2-1 rotation parameters from basis b of the attachment node to the spring's basis s\nbasis::Symbol: basis in which the spring's attachment is defined, either :b or :A\n\n\n\n\n\nadd_spring_to_beams!(model; beams, spring, nodesLocalIDs, Δu0, Δp0, p)\n\nUpdates the model after adding a doubly-attached spring to the beams\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::Vector{Beam}: reference beams\nspring::Spring: doubly-attached spring\nnodesLocalIDs: local IDs (on the respective beams) of the nodes to which the spring is attached\nΔu0::Real: translational pre-deformation\nΔp0::Real: rotational pre-deformation\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler 3-2-1 rotation parameters from basis b of the attachment node to the spring's basis s\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.add_surface_to_beam!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.add_surface_to_beam!","category":"function","text":"add_surface_to_beam!(; beam, surface)\n\nAdds an aerodynamic surface to the beam\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\nsurface::AeroSurface: aerodynamic surface\n\n\n\n\n\nadd_surface_to_beam!(model; beam,surface)\n\nUpdates the model after adding an aerodynamic surface to the beam\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: reference beam\nsurface::AeroSurface: aerodynamic surface\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.remove_all_loads_from_beams!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.remove_all_loads_from_beams!","category":"function","text":"remove_all_loads_from_beams!(; beams)\n\nRemoves all loads from the beams\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::AbstractVector{Beam}: array of beams\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.remove_all_initial_conditions_from_beams!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.remove_all_initial_conditions_from_beams!","category":"function","text":"remove_all_initial_conditions_from_beams!(; beams)\n\nRemoves all initial conditions from the beams\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::AbstractVector{Beam}: array of beams\n\n\n\n\n\nremove_all_initial_conditions_from_beams!(model; beams)\n\nUpdates the model after removing all initial conditions from the beams\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::AbstractVector{Beam}: array of beams\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.remove_all_springs_from_beams!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.remove_all_springs_from_beams!","category":"function","text":"remove_all_springs_from_beams!(; beams)\n\nRemoves all springs attached to the beams\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::AbstractVector{Beam}: array of beams\n\n\n\n\n\nremove_all_springs_from_beams!(model; beams)\n\nUpdates the model after removing all springs attached to the beams\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::AbstractVector{Beam}: array of beams\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.remove_all_point_inertias_from_beams!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.remove_all_point_inertias_from_beams!","category":"function","text":"remove_all_point_inertias_from_beams!(; beams)\n\nRemoves all point inertias attached to the beams\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::AbstractVector{Beam}: array of beams\n\n\n\n\n\nremove_all_point_inertias_from_beams!(model; beams=beams)\n\nUpdates the model after removing all point inertias attached to the beams\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::AbstractVector{Beam}: array of beams\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.remove_surface_from_beams!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.remove_surface_from_beams!","category":"function","text":"remove_surface_from_beams!(; beams)\n\nRemoves the aerodynamic surface attached to the beams\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::AbstractVector{Beam}: array of beams\n\n\n\n\n\nremove_surface_from_beams!(model; beams)\n\nUpdates the model after removing the aerodynamic surface attached to the beams\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeams::AbstractVector{Beam}: array of beams\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_SharpEdgedGust","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_SharpEdgedGust","category":"function","text":"create_SharpEdgedGust(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a sharp-edged gust\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ninitialTime::Real: time when the gust begins\nduration::Real: duration of the gust\nconvectiveVelocity::Real: convective velocity of the gust (in longitudinal direction)\nverticalVelocity::Real: peak vertical velocity of the gust (in lift direction)\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler rotation parameters (3-2-1 sequence) from the inertial basis to the gust basis\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_OneMinusCosineGust","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_OneMinusCosineGust","category":"function","text":"create_OneMinusCosineGust(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a one-minus-cosine gust\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ninitialTime::Real: time when the gust begins\nduration::Real: duration of the gust\nconvectiveVelocity::Real: convective velocity of the gust (in longitudinal direction)\nverticalVelocity::Real: peak vertical velocity of the gust (in lift direction)\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler rotation parameters (3-2-1 sequence) from the inertial basis to the gust basis\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_Continuous1DGust","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_Continuous1DGust","category":"function","text":"create_Continuous1DGust(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a continuous 1D gust\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nspectrum::Symbol: spectrum of the PSD: von Kármán (:vK) or Dryden (:Dryden)\ngenerationMethod::Symbol: method for generation of gust velocity (:sinusoids or :whiteNoise)\ninitialTime::Real: time when the gust begins\nduration::Real: duration of the gust\ngenerationDuration::Real: duration of the gust considered for its generation\ncomponents::Vector{Int}: components of gust velocity to consider\nL::Real: turbulence length scale [m]\nωmin::Real: minimum frequency of the PSD [rad/s]\nωmax::Real: maximum frequency of the PSD [rad/s]\nUref::Real: reference airspeed\nconvectiveVelocity::Real: convective velocity of the gust (in longitudinal direction)\nσ::Real: turbulence intensity (RMS) of gust velocity component\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler rotation parameters (3-2-1 sequence) from the inertial basis to the gust basis\nseed::Int: seed for random numbers generation (reproducibility)\nplotPSD::Bool: flag to plot the PSD\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_DiscreteSpaceGust","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_DiscreteSpaceGust","category":"function","text":"create_DiscreteSpaceGust(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a discrete space gust\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ntype::Symbol: type of gust\ngustLength::Real: length of the gust (in longitudinal direction)\ngustWidth::Real: width of the gust (in spanwise direction)\nconvectiveVelocity::Real: convective velocity of the gust (in longitudinal direction)\nverticalVelocity::Real: peak vertical velocity of the gust (in lift direction)\nc0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: position vector of the front of the gust, resolved in the inertial basis\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler rotation parameters (3-2-1 sequence) from the inertial basis to the gust basis\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_Continuous1DSpaceGust","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_Continuous1DSpaceGust","category":"function","text":"create_Continuous1DSpaceGust(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a continuous 1D space gust\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nspectrum::Symbol: spectrum of the PSD: von Kármán (\"vK\") or Dryden (\"Dryden\")\ngustLength::Real: length of the gust (in longitudinal direction)\nN::Int: number of nodes for length discretization\nL::Real: turbulence length scale\nσ::Real: turbulence intensity (RMS) of gust velocity components\nc0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: position vector of the front of the gust, resolved in the inertial basis\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler rotation parameters (3-2-1 sequence) from the inertial basis to the gust basis\nseed::Int: seed for random numbers generation (reproducibility)\nplotPSD::Bool: flag to plot the PSD\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_Continuous2DSpaceGust","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_Continuous2DSpaceGust","category":"function","text":"create_Continuous2DSpaceGust(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a continuous 2D space gust\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nspectrum::Symbol: spectrum of the PSD: von Kármán (\"vK\") or Dryden (\"Dryden\")\ngustLength::Real: length of the gust (in longitudinal direction)\ngustWidth::Real: width of the gust (in lateral direction)\nNx::Int: number of nodes for length discretization\nNy::Int: number of nodes for width discretization\nL::Real: turbulence length scale\nσ::Real: turbulence intensity (RMS) of gust velocity component\nc0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: position vector of the front of the gust, resolved in the inertial basis\np::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Euler rotation parameters (3-2-1 sequence) from the inertial basis to the gust basis\nseed::Int: seed for random numbers generation (reproducibility)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.stochastic_gust_velocity_from_white_noise","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.stochastic_gust_velocity_from_white_noise","category":"function","text":"stochastic_gust_velocity_from_white_noise(spectrum::Symbol, t::AbstractVector{<:Real}, URef::Real, L::Real, σ::Real)\n\nComputes a stochastic gust velocity array by coloring a white noise signal with the appropriate spectrum.\n\nArguments\n\nspectrum::Symbol: The spectrum type (currently only :vK is supported).\nt::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Time vector (must be evenly spaced).\nURef::Real: Reference velocity.\nL::Real=762: Characteristic length scale (default: 762).\nσ::Real=1: Standard deviation of the gust velocity (default: 1).\n\nReturns\n\nU::Vector{Float64}: Gust velocity component in the x-direction.\nV::Vector{Float64}: Gust velocity component in the y-direction.\nW::Vector{Float64}: Gust velocity component in the z-direction.\nf::Vector{Float64}: Frequency vector.\nΦu::Vector{Float64}: Power spectral density of the x-component.\nΦv::Vector{Float64}: Power spectral density of the y-component.\nΦw::Vector{Float64}: Power spectral density of the z-component.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.remove_gust!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.remove_gust!","category":"function","text":"remove_gust!(model)\n\nRemoves the gust from the model and updates the model assembly\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_TrimLoadsLink","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_TrimLoadsLink","category":"function","text":"create_TrimLoadsLink(; kwargs...)\n\nTrim loads link constructor (a link between trim loads so that they are equal)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmasterBC::BC: master BC\nslaveBCs::Vector{BC}: slave BCs\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_FlapLink","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_FlapLink","category":"function","text":"create_FlapLink(; kwargs...)\n\nFlap link constructor (a link between flapped surfaces)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmasterBeam::Beam: beam of the master surface\nslaveBeams::Vector{Beam}: beams of the slave surfaces\nδMultipliers::Vector{<:Real}: multiplication factors of flap deflection in slave surfaces relative to the master surface\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_Model","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_Model","category":"function","text":"create_Model(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nname::String: name of the model\nunits::UnitsSystem: units system\nfirstNodePosition::AbstractVector{<:Real}: position vector from basis A to first node of the first beam, resolved in basis A\ngravityVector::AbstractVector{<:Real}: gravity vector, resolved in the inertial frame I\nbeams::Vector{Beam}: beams in the assembly\nconnections::Vector{<:Tuple{<:Pair{Beam,Beam},<:Pair{Int,Int}}}: beam/node connection pairs ((beamThis => beamOther, nodeThis => nodeOther))\nBCs::Vector{BC}: boundary condtions\np_A0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: initial Wiener-Milenković rotation parameters that bring basis I to basis A\nu_A::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: displacement of basis A relative to basis I\nv_A::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: velocity of basis A relative to basis I\nω_A::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: angular velocity of basis A relative to basis I\nvdot_A::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: acceleration of basis A relative to basis I\nωdot_A::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: angular acceleration of basis A relative to basis I\ntrimLoadsLinks::Vector{TrimLoadsLink}: links between trim loads\nflapLinks::Vector{FlapLink}: links between flapped surfaces\nhingeAxisConstraints::Vector{<:HingeAxisConstraint}: hinge axis constraints\natmosphere::Atmosphere: atmosphere\ngust::Union{Nothing,Gust}: gust\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.add_BCs_to_model!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.add_BCs_to_model!","category":"function","text":"add_BCs_to_model!(model; BCs)\n\nAdds boundary conditions of the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nBCs::Vector{BC}: boundary conditions to add\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_model_BCs!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_model_BCs!","category":"function","text":"update_model_BCs!(model; BCs)\n\nUpdates the boundary conditions of the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nBCs::Vector{BC}: new array of boundary conditions\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_model_links!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_model_links!","category":"function","text":"update_model_links!(model; trimLoadsLinks,flapLinks)\n\nUpdates trim loads and flap deflection links of the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\ntrimLoadsLinks::Vector{TrimLoadsLink}: new array of trim loads links\nflapLinks::Vector{FlapLink}: new array of flap deflection links\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_model_hinge_axis_constraints!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_model_hinge_axis_constraints!","category":"function","text":"update_model_hinge_axis_constraints!(model; hingeAxisConstraints)\n\nUpdates the hinge axis constraint of the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nhingeAxisConstraints::Vector{HingeAxisConstraint}: new array of hinge axis constraints\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_initial_rotation_of_basis_A!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_initial_rotation_of_basis_A!","category":"function","text":"update_initial_rotation_of_basis_A!(model; p_A0)\n\nUpdates the initial rotation of basis A (and related kinematics) in the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\np_A0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: initial rotation of basis A\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_u_A!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_u_A!","category":"function","text":"update_u_A!(model; u_A)\n\nUpdates the displacement of basis A (and related kinematics) in the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nu_A::Union{AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: displacement of basis A\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_v_A!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_v_A!","category":"function","text":"update_v_A!(model; v_A)\n\nUpdates the velocity of basis A (and related kinematics) in the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nv_A::Union{AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: velocity of basis A\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_vdot_A!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_vdot_A!","category":"function","text":"update_vdot_A!(model; vdot_A)\n\nUpdates the acceleration of basis A (and related kinematics) in the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nvdot_A::Union{AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: acceleration of basis A\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_ω_A!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_ω_A!","category":"function","text":"update_ω_A!(model; ω_A)\n\nUpdates the angular velocity of basis A (and related kinematics) in the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nω_A::Union{AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: angular velocity of basis A\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.update_ωdot_A!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.update_ωdot_A!","category":"function","text":"update_ωdot_A!(model; ωdot_A)\n\nUpdates the angular acceleration of basis A (and related kinematics) in the model.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model: reference model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nωdot_A::Union{AbstractVector{<:Real},<:Function}: angular acceleration of basis A\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_PointInertia","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_PointInertia","category":"function","text":"create_PointInertia(; kwargs...)\n\nPoint inertia constructor (a point mass with mass moment of inertia to be attached to a beam element)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmass::Real: mass\nη::AbstractVector{<:Real}: position vector relative to element's midpoint's reference line\nIxx::Real: mass moment of inertia about the x1-axis\nIyy::Real: mass moment of inertia about the x2-axis\nIzz::Real: mass moment of inertia about the x3-axis\nIxy::Real: mass product of inertia with respect to the x1-axis and x2-axis\nIxz::Real: mass product of inertia with respect to the x1-axis and x3-axis\nIyz::Real: mass product of inertia with respect to the x2-axis and x3-axis\ninertiaMatrix::Union{Missing,AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}: mass moment of inertia matrix\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_HingeAxisConstraint","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_HingeAxisConstraint","category":"function","text":"create_HingeAxisConstraint(; kwargs...)\n\nHinge axis constraint constructor\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbeam::Beam: beam\nmasterOnLeft::Bool: flag for the master element being on the left of the slave element\nlocalHingeAxis::AbstractVector{<:Real}: three-element vector defining the hinge axis, resolved in the undeformed (b) basis of the beam\npHValue::Union{Nothing,Real}: constrained value for the Wiener-Milenkovic rotation at the hinge\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_SteadyProblem","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_SteadyProblem","category":"function","text":"create_SteadyProblem(; kwargs...)\n\nSteady problem constructor\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmodel::Model: model\nsystemSolver::SystemSolver: system solver\nx0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: initial states' guess\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_TrimProblem","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_TrimProblem","category":"function","text":"create_TrimProblem(; kwargs...)\n\nTrim problem constructor\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmodel::Model: model\nsystemSolver::NonlinearSolver: system solver\ngetInertiaMatrix::Bool: flag to compute inertia matrix\nx0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: initial states' guess\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_EigenProblem","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_EigenProblem","category":"function","text":"create_EigenProblem(; kwargs...)\n\nEigen problem constructor\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmodel::Model: model\neigenmethod::Symbol: eigenvalue computation method (default: :krylovkit)\nsystemSolver::SystemSolver: system solver\nnev::Int: number of eigenvalues to be computed\nx0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: initial states' guess\nrefTrimProblem::Union{Nothing,TrimProblem}: reference trim problem containing steady solution\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_DynamicProblem","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_DynamicProblem","category":"function","text":"create_DynamicProblem(; kwargs...)\n\nDynamic problem constructor. Unless skipInitialStatesUpdate is set, the initial states and their rates (velocities and accelerations) are made consistent with the equations of motion, the motion of basis A, the BCs and, when applicable, the initial conditions of the beams (see add_initial_conditions_to_beam!), without any user-defined tuning.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmodel::Model: model\nsystemSolver::SystemSolver: system solver\nskipInitialStatesUpdate::Bool: flag to skip update of initial states\ntrackingTimeSteps::Bool: flag to track time step solutions\ntrackingFrequency::Int: frequency of time steps in which to track solution\ndisplayProgress::Bool: flag to display progress\ndisplayFrequency::Union{Nothing,Int}: frequency of time steps in which to display progress\ninitialTime::Real: initial time\nΔt::Union{Nothing,Real}: time step\nfinalTime::Union{Nothing,Real}: final time\ntimeVector::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{<:Real}}: time vector\nx0::AbstractVector{<:Real}: initial states' guess\nrefDynamicProblem::Union{DynamicProblem,Nothing}: reference problem for continuation of dynamic solution\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.solve!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.solve!","category":"function","text":"solve!(problem)\n\nSolves a problem.\n\nArguments\n\nproblem::Problem: the problem instance to be solved.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.solve_eigen!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.solve_eigen!","category":"function","text":"solve_eigen!(problem)\n\nSolves an eigenvalue problem whose steady-state solution has already been obtained.\n\nArguments\n\nproblem::EigenProblem: the eigenvalue problem instance to be solved.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.geometrical_properties_Pazy","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.geometrical_properties_Pazy","category":"function","text":"geometrical_properties_Pazy()\n\nReturns the fixed geometrical (and discretization) properties of the Pazy wing (https://github.com/UM-A2SRL/AePW3-LDWG)\n\nOutputs\n\nnElem::Int: number of elements\nL::Float64: total length\nchord::Float64: chord\nnormSparPos::Float64: normalized spar position\nnodalPositions::Vector{Float64}: normalized nodal positions\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.tip_loss_function_Pazy","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.tip_loss_function_Pazy","category":"function","text":"tip_loss_function_Pazy(type::String,θ::Real,U::Real,Λ::Real)\n\nComputes the tip loss function for the Pazy wing's tip correction function\n\nArguments\n\ntype::String: tip loss function type\nθ::Real: root pitch angle [rad]\nU::Real: airspeed [m/s]\nΛ::Real: sweep angle [rad]\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.typical_section_data","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.typical_section_data","category":"function","text":"typical_section_data(name::String)\n\nReturns the data of the typical section of given name\n\nArguments\n\nname::String: name of the typical section\n\nOutputs\n\na: position of elastic axis (semichords aft of midchord)\ne: position of mass axis (semichords aft of midchord)\nμ: mass ratio\nrα²: squared radius of gyration in pitch \nσ: ratio of plunge to pitch frequency in vacuo\nωα: pitch frequency in vacuo [rad/s]\nc: chord [m]\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_Pazy","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_Pazy","category":"function","text":"create_Pazy(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates the Pazy wing\n\nReturns the wing model and geometrical properties\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\naeroSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic solver\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: indicial gust loads solver\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for aerodynamic derivatives\nwithSkin::Bool: flag for skin on\nsweepStructuralCorrections::Bool: flag to apply ad hoc structural corrections on sectional stiffness matrix with sweep angle\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters with airspeed\nupright::Bool: flag to set the wing in the upright position\nairfoil::Airfoil: airfoil section\npitchAngle::Real: pitch angle at the root [rad]\nsweepAngle::Real: sweep angle [rad]\nhasTipCorrection::Bool: flag for aerodynamic tip correction \nGAy::Real: shear stiffness in the x₂ direction\nGAz::Real: shear stiffness in the x₃ direction\naltitude::Real: altitude\ngravity::Real: acceleration of gravity (in the negative x₃ direction of inertial basis I)\nairspeed::Union{Real,<:Function}: airspeed\nsmallAngles::Bool: flag for small angles approximation\ntipLossType::String: tip loss function type\ntipLossFunctionIsAirspeedDependent::Bool: flag for tip loss function being airspeed dependent\ntipMass::Real: mass of a point inertia added to the tip of the wing\nηtipMass::Vector{<:Real}: position vector of the tip mass relative to the tip of the spar, resolved in the local basis\ntipMassInertia::Matrix{<:Real}: mass moment of inertia matrix of the tip mass\nadditionalBCs::Vector{BC}: additional BCs (beyond the clamp)\nflightDirection::Vector{<:Real}: flight direction vector, resolved in basis A\ngust::Union{Nothing,Gust}: gust\nhinged::Bool: flag for hinged wing\nhingeNode::Union{Nothing,Int}: hinge node\nfoldAngle::Union{Nothing,Real}: fold angle [rad] (for hinge)\nflareAngle::Union{Nothing,Real}: flare angle [rad] (for hinge)\nkSpring::Union{Nothing,Real}: stiffness of the spring around the hinge\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_SMW","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_SMW","category":"function","text":"create_SMW(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates the 16-meter wing (SMW) of the conventional HALE aircraft described by Patil, Hodges and Cesnik in: Nonlinear Aeroelasticity and Flight Dynamics of HALE (2001)\n\nReturns the wing model and its span\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\naeroSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic solver\nflapSolver::FlapAeroSolver: aerodynamic solver for flap loads\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: indicial gust loads solver\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for aerodynamic derivatives\nairfoil::Airfoil: airfoil section\npitchAngle::Real: pitch angle [rad]\nk1::Real: twisting curvature\nk2::Real: flapwise bending curvature\nnElem::Int: number of elements for discretization\naltitude::Real: altitude\nairspeed::Union{Real,<:Function}: airspeed\ng::Real: acceleration of gravity\nstiffnessFactor::Real: stiffness factor for beam structural properties\n∞::Real: value of rigid structural properties\nΨ::Real: torsion-IP-bending stiffness coupling factor\ntipF3::Union{Real,<:Function}: tip dead transverse force applied at the tip\ncd0::Real: parasite drag coefficient for the wing\ncnδ::Real: cn vs δ slope for the wing\ncmδ::Real: cm vs δ slope for the wing\ncdδ::Real: cd vs δ slope for the wing\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters\nhasInducedDrag::Bool: flag to include induced drag\nδAil::Union{Nothing,Real,<:Function}: aileron deflection\nadditionalBCs::Vector{BC}: additional BCs (beyond the clamp and tip load)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_TDWing","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_TDWing","category":"function","text":"create_TDWing(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates the wing described by Tang and Dowell in: Experimental and Theoretical Study on Aeroelastic Response of High-Aspect-Ratio Wings (2001)\n\nReturns the wing model\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\naeroSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic solver\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: indicial gust loads solver\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for aerodynamic derivatives\nairfoil::Airfoil: airfoil section\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters with airspeed\npitchAngle::Real: pitch angle\nnElem::Int: number of elements for discretization\naltitude::Real: altitude\nairspeed::Union{Real,<:Function}: airspeed\ng::Real: acceleration of gravity\n∞::Real: value of rigid structural properties\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_Helios","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_Helios","category":"function","text":"create_Helios(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a model based on the flying-wing aircraft described by Patil and Hodges in: Flight Dynamics of Highly Flexible Flying Wings (2006)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\naeroSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic solver\nflapSolver::FlapAeroSolver: flap aerodynamic solver\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: indicial gust loads solver\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for aerodynamic derivatives\naltitude::Real: altitude\ng::Real: local acceleration of gravity\nwingAirfoil::Airfoil: airfoil section of the wing\npodAirfoil::Airfoil: airfoil section of the pods\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters with airspeed\nbeamPods::Bool: flag to include pods\nstiffnessFactor::Real: stiffness factor for the wing structure\n∞::Real: value for rigid structural properties\nnElemStraightSemispan::Int: number of elements in the straight section of the semispan\nnElemDihedralSemispan::Int: number of elements in the dihedral section of the semispan\nnElemPod::Int: number of elements in the pods\npayloadPounds::Real: payload, in pounds\nairspeed::Real: local initial/trim airspeed\nδIsTrimVariable::Bool: flag for flap deflection being a trim variable\nthrustIsTrimVariable::Bool: flag for motors' thrust being a trim variable\nδ::Union{Nothing,Real,<:Function}: flap deflection\nthrust::Union{Real,<:Function}: motors' thrust\nreducedChord::Bool: flag to employ a reduced (7 ft) chord\npayloadOnWing::Bool: flag to set the payload on the wing's reference line\nwingRootAoA::Real: root angle of attack for clamped wing model [rad]\nwithAttachmentSprings::Bool: flag to include attachment springs for eigen-analyses\nμ::Real: stiffness of the attachment springs\ngust::Union{Nothing,Gust}: gust\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_cHALE","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_cHALE","category":"function","text":"create_cHALE(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a model based on the conventional HALE aircraft described by Patil, Hodges and Cesnik in: Nonlinear Aeroelasticity and Flight Dynamics of HALE (2001)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\naltitude::Real: altitude\naeroSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic solver\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for aerodynamic derivatives\nflapSolver::FlapAeroSolver: aerodynamic solver for flap loads\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: indicial gust loads solver\nstabilizersAero::Bool: flag for stabilizers with aerodynamic surfaces\nincludeVS::Bool: flag to include a vertical stabilizer in the model\nwAirfoil::Airfoil: wing airfoil section\nsAirfoil::Airfoil: stabilizers airfoil section\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters with airspeed\nnElemWing::Int: number of elements of the full wing\nnElemHorzStabilizer::Int: number of elements of the horizontal stabilizer\nnElemTailBoom::Int: number of elements of the tail boom\nnElemVertStabilizer::Int: number of elements of the vertical stabilizer\n∞::Real=1e12: value of rigid structural properties\nstiffnessFactor::Real: stiffness factor for the wing structure\nk1::Real: undeformed wing torsional curvature\nk2::Real: undeformed wing flapwise bending curvature\nairspeed::Real: local initial/trim airspeed\nδElevIsTrimVariable::Bool: flag for elevator deflection being a trim variable\nδAilIsTrimVariable::Bool: flag for aileron deflection being a trim variable\nδRuddIsTrimVariable::Bool: flag for rudder deflection being a trim variable    \nthrustIsTrimVariable::Bool: flag for motors' thrust being a trim variable\nδElev::Union{Nothing,Real,<:Function}: elevator deflection\nδAil::Union{Nothing,Real,<:Function}: aileron deflection\nδRudd::Union{Nothing,Real,<:Function}: rudder deflection\nthrust::Union{Real,<:Function}: motors' thrust\ng::Real: local acceleration of gravity\nwingCd0::Real: parasite drag coefficient for the wing\nwingcnδ::Real: cn vs δ slope for the wing\nwingcmδ::Real: cm vs δ slope for the wing\nwingcdδ::Real: cd vs δ slope for the wing\nstabsCd0::Real: parasite drag coefficient for the stabilizers\nstabscnδ::Real: cn vs δ slope for the stabilizers\nstabscmδ::Real: cm vs δ slope for the stabilizers\nstabscdδ::Real: cd vs δ slope for the stabilizers\nhasInducedDrag::Bool: flag to include induced drag\nrootClamped::Bool: flag for being clamped at wing root\nwithAttachmentSprings::Bool: flag to include attachment springs for eigen-analyses\nμ::Real: stiffness coefficient for the attachment springs\ngust::Union{Nothing,Gust}: gust input for the model\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_BWB","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_BWB","category":"function","text":"create_BWB(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a model based on the blended-wing-body described by Weihua Su's PhD thesis and in https://arc.aiaa.org/doi/10.2514/1.47317\n\nKeyword arguments\n\naltitude::Real: altitude\naeroSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic solver\nflapSolver::FlapAeroSolver: flap-induced loads solver\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: indicial gust loads solver\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for aerodynamic derivatives\nairfoil::Airfoil: airfoil of the wing\n∞::Real=1e12: value of rigid structural properties\nstiffnessFactor::Real: stiffness factor for the wing structure\nairspeed::Real = local initial/trim airspeed\nδElevIsTrimVariable::Bool: flag for elevator deflection being a trim variable\nthrustIsTrimVariable::Bool: flag for motors' thrust being a trim variable\nδElev::Union{Nothing,Real,<:Function}: elevator deflection\nthrust::Union{Real,<:Function}: motors' thrust\ng::Real: local acceleration of gravity\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters with airspeed\nhasTipCorrection::Bool: flag to employ aerodynamic tip correction\ntipLossDecayFactor::Real: tip loss decay factor\nwithAttachmentSprings::Bool: flag to include attachment springs for eigen-analyses\nμ::Real: attachment spring constant (effective only if withAttachmentSprings is true)\nwingRootAoA::Real: wing root pitch angle for wing model [rad]\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_HealyBaselineFFWT","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_HealyBaselineFFWT","category":"function","text":"create_HealyBaselineFFWT(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a version of Healy's baseline wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT). See Healy's PhD thesis, chapter 3.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\naeroSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic solver\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: indicial gust loads solver\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for aerodynamic derivatives\nairfoil::Airfoil: airfoil section\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters with airspeed\nhasTipCorrection::Bool: flag for aerodynamic tip correction\ntipLossDecayFactor::Real: tip loss decay factor, in case hasTipCorrection is true\nhingeConfiguration::Symbol: hinge hingeConfiguration (:free or :locked)\nflareAngle::Real: flare angle [rad]\npitchAngle::Real: root pitch angle [rad]\nfoldAngle::Union{Real,Nothing}: fold angle [rad]\nkSpring::Real: stiffness of the rotational spring around the hinge\naltitude::Real: altitude\nairspeed::Union{Real,<:Function}: local airspeed\ng::Real: local acceleration of gravity\nnElementsInner::Int: number of elements for discretization of inner part of the wing\nnElementsFFWT::Int: number of elements for discretization of the wingtip\ngust::Union{Nothing,Gust}=nothing: gust\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_HealySideslipFFWT","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_HealySideslipFFWT","category":"function","text":"create_HealySideslipFFWT(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a version of Healy's wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT) used for sideslip studies. See Healy's PhD thesis, chapter 7.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\naeroSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic solver\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: indicial gust loads solver\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for aerodynamic derivatives\nairfoil::Airfoil: airfoil section\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters with airspeed\nhasTipCorrection::Bool: flag for aerodynamic tip correction\ntipLossDecayFactor::Real: tip loss decay factor, in case hasTipCorrection is true\nwingtipConfiguration::Symbol: configuration of the wingtip, either :free, :locked or :removed\npitchAngle::Real: root pitch angle [rad]\nwingtipTwist::Real: wingtip twist angle [rad]\nfoldAngle::Union{Real,Nothing}: fold angle [rad]\nflareAngle::Real: flare angle [rad]\nkSpring::Real: stiffness of the spring around the hinge\naltitude::Real: altitude\ng::Real: local acceleration of gravity\nairspeed::Union{Real,<:Function}: local airspeed\nflightDirection::Vector{<:Real}: flight direction vector, resolved on basis A\nnElementsInner::Int: number of elements for discretization of inner part of the wing\nnElementsFFWT::Int: number of elements for discretization of the wingtip\ngust::Union{Nothing,Gust}=nothing: gust\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_HealyLCOFFWT","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_HealyLCOFFWT","category":"function","text":"create_HealyLCOFFWT(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a version of Healy's wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT) used for flutter and LCOs studies. See Healy's PhD thesis, chapter 7 and CHEUNG et al. (2020) - Analyzing the Dynamic Behavior of a High Aspect Ratio Wing Incorporating a Folding Wingtip [SCITECH].\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\naeroSolver::AeroSolver: aerodynamic solver\ngustSolver::GustAeroSolver: indicial gust loads solver\nderivationMethod::DerivationMethod: method for aerodynamic derivatives\nairfoil::Airfoil: airfoil section\nupdateAirfoilParameters::Bool: flag to update airfoil parameters with airspeed\nhasTipCorrection::Bool: flag for aerodynamic tip correction\ntipLossDecayFactor::Union{Real,Missing}: tip loss decay factor, in case hasTipCorrection is true\nverticalMount::Bool: flag for wing mounted vertically\nwingtipRemoved::Bool: flag for removed wingtip\nwingtipLocked::Bool: flag for locked wingtip\nwingtipTab::Bool: flag for wingtip tab\npitchAngle::Real: root pitch angle [rad]\nfoldAngle::Union{Real,Nothing}: fold angle [rad]\nflareAngle::Real: flare angle [rad]\nkSpring::Real: stiffness of the spring around the hinge\naltitude::Real: altitude\ng::Real: local acceleration of gravity\nairspeed::Union{Real,<:Function}: local airspeed\nflightDirection::Vector{<:Real}: flight direction vector, resolved on basis A\nnElementsInner::Int: number of elements for discretization of inner part of the wing\nnElementsOuter::Int: number of elements for discretization of outer part of the wing\nnElementsFFWT::Int: number of elements for discretization of the wingtip\ngust::Union{Nothing,Gust}=nothing: gust\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.tip_loss_function_HealyLCOFFWT","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.tip_loss_function_HealyLCOFFWT","category":"function","text":"tip_loss_function_HealyLCOFFWT(θ::Real,U::Real)\n\nComputes the tip loss function for Healy's wing with flared folding wingtip used in LCO studies\n\nArguments\n\nθ::Real: root pitch angle [rad]\nU::Real: airspeed [m/s]\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_Spring","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_Spring","category":"function","text":"create_Spring(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a spring, whose displacement is assumed to be small (changes in direction of the spring's loads are neglected)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nku::Real: translational stiffness constant\nkp::Real: rotational stiffness constant\nforceFunction::Union{Missing,<:Function}: function of two inputs (stiffness constant and deformation) that outputs the spring force. Default is a linear function: f(k,x) = k * x where k=ku\nmomentFunction::Union{Missing,<:Function}: function of two inputs (stiffness constant and deformation) that outputs the spring moment. Default is a linear function: f(k,x) = k * x where k=kp\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_LinearSolver","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_LinearSolver","category":"function","text":"create_LinearSolver()\n\nLinear system solver constructor\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_NewtonRaphson","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_NewtonRaphson","category":"function","text":"create_NewtonRaphson(; kwargs...)\n\nNewton-Raphson nonlinear system solver constructor\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmethod::Symbol: method for nonlinear solution\nabsTol::Float64: absolute convergence tolerance\nrelTol::Float64: relative convergence tolerance\nmaxIter::Int: maximum number of iterations\ndesIter::Int: desired number of iterations\nmaxAbsErr::Real: maximum absolute error for divergence detection\nmaxRelErr::Real: maximum relative error for divergence detection\nfullLoadFacTol::Float64: load step tolerance for the final solution convergence\ninitLoadFac::Real: initial load factor\nminLoadFac::Float64: minimum load factor\nmaxLoadFacStep::Float64: maximum load factor step\nminLoadFacStep::Float64: minimum load factor step\ntrackLoadSteps::Bool: flag to track partial load steps solutions\ndisplayStatus::Bool: flag to display status\nminConvRateJacUpdate::Real: minimum convergence rate to skip computation of structural Jacobians when using :NewtonChord method\nminConvRateAeroJacUpdate::Real: minimum convergence rate to skip computation of aerodynamic Jacobians when using :NewtonChord method\nallowAdvanceThroughUnconvergedAeroStates::Bool: flag to allow the advancement of the algorithm through unconverged aerodynamic states\naeroResRatioThreshold::Float64: threshold ratio of aerodynamic/total residuals in order to allow the above flag\naeroΔxErrorThreshold::Float64: threshold ratio of aerodynamic/total Δx in order to allow the above flag\ntrimSolverMethod::Symbol: method for linear system solution in trim problems\ndampingTrim::Float64: damping term for method = :LM\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_NLsolver","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_NLsolver","category":"function","text":"create_NLsolver(; kwargs...)\n\nNLSolve.jl nonlinear system solver constructor.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmethod::Symbol: nonlinear solve method\nlinesearch::Bool: flag to enable linesearch method for the :newton method\njcalc::Symbol: Jacobian calculation method, either :analytical or :numerical\ncheckJac::Bool: flag to compare analytical and numerical Jacobians at each iteration\nftol::Float64: absolute convergence tolerance for residual\nmaxIter::Int: maximum number of iterations\ndesIter::Int: desired number of iterations for the nonlinear solver\nfullLoadFacTol::Float64: load step tolerance for the final solution convergence\ninitLoadFac::Real: initial load factor for the load stepping procedure\nminLoadFac::Float64: minimum load factor for the load stepping procedure\nmaxLoadFacStep::Float64: maximum load factor step for the load stepping procedure\nminLoadFacStep::Float64: minimum load factor step for the load stepping procedure\ntrackLoadSteps::Bool: flag to track load steps during the nonlinear solve\ndisplayStatus::Bool: flag to display convergence status\ntrimSolverMethod::Symbol: method for linear system solution in trim problems\ndampingTrim::Float64: damping term for method = :LM\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_ArclengthSolver","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_ArclengthSolver","category":"function","text":"create_ArclengthSolver(; kwargs...)\n\nArclength (Riks/Crisfield spherical) continuation solver constructor. Traces the equilibrium path in the (x,σ) space, so it can go through limit points (snap-through and snap-back), where load-controlled Newton methods fail. Meant for static (non-trim) problems, with the load factor σ scaling the applied loads.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nabsTol::Float64: absolute convergence tolerance for the residual\nmaxIter::Int: maximum number of corrector iterations per arclength step\ndesIter::Int: desired number of corrector iterations per arclength step, used to adapt the arclength step\ninitArcLength::Real: initial arclength step, in the (x,σ) space\nminArcLength::Float64: minimum arclength step\nmaxArcLength::Real: maximum arclength step\nmaxLoadFac::Real: load factor at which the continuation stops (it may be greater than 1)\nmaxSteps::Int: maximum number of arclength steps\ninitLoadFac::Real: initial load factor\nminLoadFac::Float64: minimum load factor (only used to size the load step history)\ntrackLoadSteps::Bool: flag to track the solution at each step\ndisplayStatus::Bool: flag to display status\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_TrimSolver","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_TrimSolver","category":"function","text":"create_TrimSolver(; kwargs...)\n\nTrim solver constructor\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmethod::Symbol: method for trim (least-squares) system solution: either :LQ (LQ decomposition), :LM (Levenberg-Marquardt with fixed relative damping), or :SVD (Singular Value Decomposition) or :pinv (Moore-Penrose pseudo-inverse, which yields minimum norm and works with rank-deficient Jacobians)\ndamping::Union{Nothing,Real}: damping term for method = :LM\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.create_UnitsSystem","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.create_UnitsSystem","category":"function","text":"create_UnitsSystem(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a system composed of length, force, angle, frequency and mass units (this is only for plotting purposes and does not influence calculations)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nlength::String: length unit\nforce::String: force unit\nangle::String: angle unit\nfrequency::String: frequency unit\nmass::String: mass unit\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.feet2meters","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.feet2meters","category":"function","text":"feet2meters(x)\n\nConverts a length from feet to meters\n\nArguments\n\nx: length in feet\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.pounds2kilograms","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.pounds2kilograms","category":"function","text":"pounds2kilograms(x)\n\nConverts a mass from pounds to kilograms\n\nArguments\n\nx: mass in pounds\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rads2Hz","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rads2Hz","category":"function","text":"rads2Hz(x)\n\nConverts a frequency from radians per second to Hertz\n\nArguments\n\nx: frequency in radians per second\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.Hz2rads","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.Hz2rads","category":"function","text":"Hz2rads(x)\n\nConverts a frequency from Hertz to radians per second\n\nArguments\n\nx: frequency in Hertz\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rpm2rads","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rpm2rads","category":"function","text":"rpm2rads(x)\n\nConverts a frequency from revolutions per minute to radians per second\n\nArguments\n\nx: frequency in revolutions per minute\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rads2rpm","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rads2rpm","category":"function","text":"rads2rpm(x)\n\nConverts a frequency from radians per second to revolutions per minute\n\nArguments\n\nx: frequency in radians per second\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.round_off","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.round_off","category":"function","text":"round_off(x,tol)\n\nRounds the array to input tolerance\n\nArguments\n\nx::AbstractArray{<:Number}: array\ntol::Real: tolerance (defaults to machine epsilon)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.round_off!","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.round_off!","category":"function","text":"round_off!(x,tol)\n\nRounds the array to input tolerance in-place\n\nArguments\n\nx::AbstractArray{<:Number}: array\ntol::Real: tolerance (defaults to machine epsilon)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.average","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.average","category":"function","text":"average(x)\n\nComputes the arithmetic mean of an array x.\n\nArguments\n\nx::AbstractArray{<:Real}: array\n\n\n\n\n\naverage(args...)\n\nComputes the arithmetic mean of a list of numbers.\n\nArguments\n\nargs...: list of numbers\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rms","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rms","category":"function","text":"rms(x)\n\nComputes the root mean square of an array x\n\nArguments\n\nx::AbstractArray{<:Real}: array\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.tilde","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.tilde","category":"function","text":"tilde(v)\n\nComputes the skew-symmetric matrix associated with a three-element vector v\n\nArguments\n\nv::AbstractVector{<:Number}: 3-element vector\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.axial","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.axial","category":"function","text":"axial(R)\n\nComputes the axial part of a 3x3 matrix\n\nArguments\n\nR::AbstractMatrix{<:Number}: 3x3 matrix\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.gauss_legendre","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.gauss_legendre","category":"function","text":"gauss_legendre(f, a, b; n)\n\nComputes the Gauss–Legendre quadrature (integral) for the vector-valued function f in the interval [a, b], using n Gauss points (2 ≤ n ≤ 7)\n\nArguments\n\nf::Function: function to integrate\na::Real: lower limit\nb::Real: upper limit\nn::Int: number of Gauss points\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.isotropic_stiffness_matrix","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.isotropic_stiffness_matrix","category":"function","text":"isotropic_stiffness_matrix(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a 6x6 sectional stiffness matrix for a cross-section made of isotropic material\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\n∞::Real: value for rigid properties\nEA::Union{Missing,Real}: axial stiffness\nGAy::Union{Missing,Real}: shear stiffness in the x2 direction\nGAz::Union{Missing,Real}: shear stiffness in the x3 direction\nGJ::Union{Missing,Real}: torsional stiffness\nEIy::Union{Missing,Real}: bending stiffness in the x2 direction\nEIz::Union{Missing,Real}: bending stiffness in the x3 direction\nE::Union{Missing,Real}: Young's modulus, used to compute EA, EIy and EIz when those are not provided explicitly\nG::Union{Missing,Real}: shear modulus, used to compute GAy, GAz and GJ when those are not provided explicitly\nν::Union{Missing,Real}: Poisson's ratio, used to compute G = E / (2(1 + ν)) when G is not provided explicitly\nA::Union{Missing,Real}: area, used to compute EA and shear stiffnesses when those are not provided explicitly\nIy::Union{Missing,Real}: second moment of area about x2, used to compute EIy when it is not provided explicitly\nIz::Union{Missing,Real}: second moment of area about x3, used to compute EIz when it is not provided explicitly\nJ::Union{Missing,Real}: torsion constant, used to compute GJ when it is not provided explicitly\nKsy::Real: shear correction factor for x2, used to compute GAy = G*A*Ksy\nKsz::Real: shear correction factor for x3, used to compute GAz = G*A*Ksz\nKt::Real: torsion correction factor, used to compute GJ = G*J*Kt\nt2::Real: offset from reference line to tension center in the x2 direction\nt3::Real: offset from reference line to tension center in the x3 direction\ns2::Real: offset from reference line to shear center in the x2 direction\ns3::Real: offset from reference line to shear center in the x3 direction\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.anisotropic_stiffness_matrix","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.anisotropic_stiffness_matrix","category":"function","text":"anisotropic_stiffness_matrix(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a 6x6 sectional stiffness matrix for a cross-section made of anisotropic material\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\n∞::Real: value for rigid properties\nEA::Real: axial stiffness\nGAy::Real: shear stiffness in the x2 direction\nGAz::Real: shear stiffness in the x3 direction\nGJ::Real: torsional stiffness\nEIy::Real: bending stiffness in the x2 direction\nEIz::Real: bending stiffness in the x3 direction\nc12::Real: coupling stiffness between axial and shear in the x2 direction\nc13::Real: coupling stiffness between axial and shear in the x3 direction\nc14::Real: coupling stiffness between axial and torsion\nc15::Real: coupling stiffness between axial and bending in the x2 direction\nc16::Real: coupling stiffness between axial and bending in the x3 direction\nc23::Real: coupling stiffness between shear in the x2 and x3 directions\nc24::Real: coupling stiffness between shear in the x2 direction and torsion\nc25::Real: coupling stiffness between shear in the x2 direction and bending in the x2 direction\nc26::Real: coupling stiffness between shear in the x2 direction and bending in the x3 direction\nc34::Real: coupling stiffness between shear in the x3 direction and torsion\nc35::Real: coupling stiffness between shear in the x3 direction and bending in the x2 direction\nc36::Real: coupling stiffness between shear in the x3 direction and bending in the x3 direction\nc45::Real: coupling stiffness between torsion and bending in the x2 direction\nc46::Real: coupling stiffness between torsion and bending in the x3 direction\nc56::Real: coupling stiffness between bending in the x2 and x3 directions\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.inertia_matrix","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.inertia_matrix","category":"function","text":"inertia_matrix(; kwargs...)\n\nCreates a 6x6 sectional inertia matrix\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nρA::Union{Missing,Real}: mass per unit length\nρIy::Union{Missing,Real}: mass moment of inertia per unit length about the x2-axis\nρIz::Union{Missing,Real}: mass moment of inertia per unit length about the x3-axis\nρIyz::Union{Missing,Real}: mass product of inertia per unit length\nρ::Union{Missing,Real}: material density, used to compute ρA, ρIy, ρIz and ρIyz when those are not provided explicitly\nA::Union{Missing,Real}: area, used to compute ρA when it is not provided explicitly\nIy::Union{Missing,Real}: second moment of area about x2, used to compute ρIy when it is not provided explicitly\nIz::Union{Missing,Real}: second moment of area about x3, used to compute ρIz when it is not provided explicitly\nIyz::Union{Missing,Real}: product of area inertia, used to compute ρIyz when it is not provided explicitly\ne2::Real: offset from reference line to center of gravity in the x2 direction\ne3::Real: offset from reference line to center of gravity in the x3 direction\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_E321","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_E321","category":"function","text":"rotation_tensor_E321(p)\n\nComputes the rotation tensor according to Euler parameters sequence 3-2-1\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_E213","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_E213","category":"function","text":"rotation_tensor_E213(p)\n\nComputes the rotation tensor according to Euler parameters sequence 2-1-3.\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_E231","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_E231","category":"function","text":"rotation_tensor_E231(p)\n\nComputes the rotation tensor according to Euler parameters sequence 2-3-1.\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_E313","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_E313","category":"function","text":"rotation_tensor_E313(p)\n\nComputes the rotation tensor according to Euler parameters sequence 3-1-3\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_quaternion","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_quaternion","category":"function","text":"rotation_tensor_quaternion(q)\n\nComputes the rotation tensor according to quaternion parameters\n\nArguments\n\nq::AbstractVector{<:Number}: quaternion parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.transform_to_WM","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.transform_to_WM","category":"function","text":"transform_to_WM(p; from)\n\nTransforms rotation parameters from a given parametrization to Wiener-Milenkovic parameters\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nfrom::Symbol: rotation parametrization, either :E321 (Euler angles 3-2-1), :E213 (Euler angles 2-1-3), :E231 (Euler angles 2-3-1), :E313 (Euler angles 3-1-3) or :quaternion\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_WM","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_tensor_WM","category":"function","text":"rotation_tensor_WM(p)\n\nComputes the rotation tensor according to Wiener-Milenkovic parameters\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.scaled_rotation_parameters","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.scaled_rotation_parameters","category":"function","text":"scaled_rotation_parameters(p)\n\nReturns the scaled (extended) Wiener-Milenkovic rotation parameters\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.twist_angle","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.twist_angle","category":"function","text":"twist_angle(p; rotationParametrization, outputUnit)\n\nComputes the twist angle given the rotation parameters\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nrotationParametrization::Symbol: rotation parametrization, either :WM (Wiener-Milenkovic), :E321 (Euler angles 3-2-1), :E213 (Euler angles 2-1-3) or :E313 (Euler angles 3-1-3)\noutputUnit::Symbol: output unit for the twist angle, either :deg (degrees) or :rad (radians)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_angle","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_angle","category":"function","text":"rotation_angle(p)\n\nComputes the rotation angle given the Wiener-Milenkovic rotation parameters\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_angle_limited","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_angle_limited","category":"function","text":"rotation_angle_limited(p)\n\nComputes the rotation angle (in the range -360 to 360 degrees) given the Wiener-Milenkovic rotation parameters\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_parameters_WM","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_parameters_WM","category":"function","text":"rotation_parameters_WM(R)\n\nComputes the Wiener-Milenkovic rotation parameters given a rotation tensor\n\nArguments\n\nR::AbstractMatrix{<:Number}: rotation tensor\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_parameters_Rodrigues","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_parameters_Rodrigues","category":"function","text":"rotation_parameters_Rodrigues(R)\n\nComputes the Rodrigues rotation parameters given a rotation tensor\n\nArguments\n\nR::AbstractMatrix{<:Number}: rotation tensor\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.yrp_from_rotation_tensor","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.yrp_from_rotation_tensor","category":"function","text":"yrp_from_rotation_tensor(R; ϵround=1e-10,ϵsingularity=1e-3,assumeNullYawInSingularity=true)\n\nComputes the Euler angles from the sequence 3-2-1 (yaw, roll, pitch) given the rotation tensor\n\nArguments\n\nR::AbstractMatrix{<:Number}: rotation tensor\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nϵround::Real: tolerance for rounding off elements of R to zero\nϵsingularity::Real: tolerance to consider singular case\nassumeNullYawInSingularity::Bool: flag to assume zero yaw angle in singularity\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.quaternion_from_rotation_tensor","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.quaternion_from_rotation_tensor","category":"function","text":"quaternion_from_rotation_tensor(R)\n\nComputes the quaternion (Euler parameters) given a rotation tensor. Derived from Bauchau's book section 13.3.4. \n\nArguments\n\nR::AbstractMatrix{<:Number}: rotation tensor\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.WM_to_yrp","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.WM_to_yrp","category":"function","text":"WM_to_yrp(p)\n\nTransforms Wiener-Milenkovic parameters to Euler parameters of sequence 3-2-1 (yaw, roll, pitch)\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.yrp_to_WM","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.yrp_to_WM","category":"function","text":"yrp_to_WM(p)\n\nTransforms Euler parameters of sequence 3-2-1 (yaw, roll, pitch) to Wiener-Milenkovic parameters\n\nArguments\n\np::AbstractVector{<:Number}: rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.rotation_between_WM","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.rotation_between_WM","category":"function","text":"rotation_between_WM(p1,p2)\n\nComputes the Wiener-Milenkovic parameters describing the rotation from p1 to p2, i.e., p12 such that R2(p2) = R12(p12)*R1(p1)\n\nArguments\n\np1::AbstractVector{<:Number}: initial rotation parameters\np2::AbstractVector{<:Number}: final rotation parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.mode_tracking","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.mode_tracking","category":"function","text":"mode_tracking(sols; method,α,β,γ,maxCost)\n\nApplies mode tracking based on eigenvectors, frequencies and dampings match.\n\nArguments\n\nsols::AbstractVector{<:EigenSolution}: vector of eigensolutions to be tracked\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nmethod::Symbol: mode assignment method\nα::Real: weight for eigenvector similarity in hungarian method\nβ::Real: weight for frequency continuity in hungarian method\nγ::Real: weight for damping continuity in hungarian method\nmaxCost::Real: maximum allowable cost for mode matching in the hungarian method\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.filter_tracked_modes","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.filter_tracked_modes","category":"function","text":"filter_tracked_modes(freqs, damps, modeShapes; fmin, fmax, maxTracks, whichTracks, trackwise, dropEmpty, dropOutsideBounds, tolFreq, tolDamp)\n\nFilters tracked modes based on frequency range and removes empty tracks.\n\nArguments\n\nfreqs::AbstractVecOrMat{<:Real}: matrix of frequencies (nTracks x N)\ndamps::AbstractVecOrMat{<:Real}: matrix of damping ratios (nTracks x N)\nmodeShapes::AbstractVecOrMat{Union{ModeShape,Nothing}}: matrix of mode shapes (nTracks x N)\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nfmin::Real: minimum frequency to keep\nfmax::Real: maximum frequency to keep\nmaxTracks::Int: maximum number of tracks to keep\nwhichTracks::Symbol: whether to keep the first or last maxTracks tracks (:first or :last)\ntrackwise::Bool: whether to keep a track entirely if it lies within the frequency limits at any point\ndropEmpty::Bool: whether to drop tracks that are entirely NaN\ndropOutsideBounds::Bool: whether to drop tracks that have frequencies outside the [fmin, fmax] range\ntolFreq::Real: tolerance for considering frequencies as zero\ntolDamp::Real: tolerance for considering dampings as zero\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.find_flutter","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.find_flutter","category":"function","text":"find_flutter(controlParam,freqs,damps,extras...)\n\nFinds the flutter onset and offset points for each mode given the frequency and damping matrices and the control parameter vector.\n\nArguments\n\ncontrolParam::AbstractVector{<:Real}: vector of control parameter values (length N)\nfreqs::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: matrix of frequencies (nTracks x N)\ndamps::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: matrix of damping ratios (nTracks x N)\nextras::AbstractVector{<:Real}...: additional variables of the same length as controlParam\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nsortby::Symbol=:CP: determines the sorting criterion for the flutter points\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.find_divergence","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.find_divergence","category":"function","text":"find_divergence(controlParam,freqs,damps; method,atol)\n\nFinds the control parameter values and corresponding modes in damps for which an instability occurs (positive damping), indicating divergence.\n\nArguments\n\ncontrolParam::AbstractVector{<:Real}: vector of control parameter values (length N)\nfreqs::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: matrix of frequencies (nTracks x N)\ndamps::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: matrix of damping ratios (nTracks x N)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nmethod::Symbol: method to use for finding divergence, either :regular or :backExtrap\natol::Real: absolute tolerance for zero frequency comparison\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.blockdiag_dense","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.blockdiag_dense","category":"function","text":"blockdiag_dense(mats::AbstractMatrix...) -> Matrix\n\nCreates a block diagonal matrix from the given matrices\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.moving_average","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.moving_average","category":"function","text":"moving_average(v, n)\n\nComputes the moving average of a series\n\nArguments\n\nv::AbstractVector{<:Real}: series\nn::Int: moving average window size\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.get_FFT_and_PSD","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.get_FFT_and_PSD","category":"function","text":"get_FFT_and_PSD(t,y; tol)\n\nComputes the FFT and PSD of signal y(t)\n\nArguments\n\nt::AbstractVector{<:Real}: time signal\ny::Vector{<:Real}: quantity signal\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ntol::Real: tolerance for time signal being equally spaced\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.local_extrema","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.local_extrema","category":"function","text":"local_extrema(x)\n\nComputes the local extrema of a series, and corresponding indices\n\nArguments\n\nx::AbstractVector{<:Real}: series\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.backward_extrapolation","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.backward_extrapolation","category":"function","text":"backward_extrapolation(v; order)\n\nEstimates the values of an array from backward finite difference extrapolation\n\nArguments\n\nv::AbstractVector{<:Real}: vector\n\nKeyword arguments\n\norder::Int: order of the approximation\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.count_of_exceedance","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.count_of_exceedance","category":"function","text":"count_of_exceedance(time, series, datum, marker)\n\nComputes the count of exceedance of the quantities in the marker vector above the datum for the given time series\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ntime::AbstractVector{<:Real}: time vector\nseries::Vector{<:Real}: time series\ndatum::Real: datum value for the time series\nmarker::Vector{<:Real}: markers for the count of exceedance computation\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.frequency_of_exceedance","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.frequency_of_exceedance","category":"function","text":"frequency_of_exceedance(timeLength, count)\n\nComputes the frequency of exceedance\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ncount::Vector{<:Real}: count of exceedance\ntimeLength::Real: length of time\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.gcd_floats_approx","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.gcd_floats_approx","category":"function","text":"gcd_floats_approx(v; tol)\n\nComputes the approximate greatest common divisor of a vector of floats\n\nArguments\n\nv::Vector{Float64}: vector\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ntol::Real: tolerance\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.spectrogram","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.spectrogram","category":"function","text":"spectrogram(t, x; window, overlap, db)\n\nCompute the spectrogram (Short-Time Fourier Transform) of signal x sampled at times t\n\nArguments\n\nt::AbstractVector{<:Real}: time vector (must be equally spaced)\nx::AbstractVector{<:Real}: signal values\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nwindow::Int: window length in samples (default: 256)\noverlap::Real: fraction of window overlap (0–1, default: 0.5)\ndb::Bool: if true, return power in decibels (default: true)\n\nOutputs\n\nf::Vector: frequency vector\ntt::Vector: time vector (centers of analysis windows)\nS::Matrix: spectrogram matrix (|STFT|²), size (length(f), length(tt))\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.NASA_frames_loader","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.NASA_frames_loader","category":"function","text":"NASA_frames_loader(frame)\n\nLoads dynamic stall data from a frame of the NASA report (https://ntrs.nasa.gov/archive/nasa/casi.ntrs.nasa.gov/19830003778.pdf)\n\nArguments\n\nframe::Union{Int,String}: frame number\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.GU_frames_loader","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.GU_frames_loader","category":"function","text":"GU_frames_loader(frame)\n\nLoads dynamic stall data from a frame of the University of Glasgow (DOI:10.5525/gla.researchdata.464)\n\nArguments\n\nframe::Union{Int,String}: frame number\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.get_undeformed_airfoil_coords","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.get_undeformed_airfoil_coords","category":"function","text":"get_undeformed_airfoil_coords(element::Element)\n\nReturns the coordinates of the undeformed airfoil sections at the nodes of the element, as 3×N matrices (nodes 1 and 2), resolved in basis A\n\nArguments\n\nelement::Element: element (must have an aerodynamic surface)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_undeformed_assembly","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_undeformed_assembly","category":"function","text":"plot_undeformed_assembly(model::Model; kwargs...)\n\nPlots the undeformed assembly of beams in the model\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::Model\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\nview::Tuple{Real,Real}: view angles\nequalAspectRatio::Bool: flag to set equal aspect ratio plot\nplotNodes::Bool: flag to plot nodes\nnodesColor=:black: color of nodes\nlinesColor=:black: color of lines (beams)\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nplotSize::Tuple{Real,Real}: size of the plot (width, height) in pixels\nplotLimits::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real}}}: plot axis limits\navoidDistortion::Bool: flag to avoid distortion in 3D plots\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_steady_deformation","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_steady_deformation","category":"function","text":"plot_steady_deformation(problem::Problem; kwargs...)\n\nPlots the deformed state of the model in the given problem\n\nArguments\n\nproblem::Problem: problem\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\nplotBCs::Bool: flag to plot BCs\nplotUndeformed::Bool: flag to plot undeformed assembly\nplotDistLoads::Bool: flag to plot distributed loads (includes gravitational and aerodynamic loads)\nview::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Real,Real}}: view angles (by default, the plot is planar if the assembly lies on a plane of the basis A, and 3D otherwise)\nscale::Real: displacements and rotations scale\nlw::Real: linewidth\ncolorUndef: color for undeformed assembly\ncolorDef: color for deformed assembly\nplotLegend::Bool: flag to plot legend\nplotAxes::Bool: flag to plot axes\nplotGrid::Bool: flag to plot grid\nlegendPos: legend position\ntolPlane::Real: tolerance of the extent of the assembly along an axis to plot as plane\nplotAeroSurf::Bool: flag to plot aerodynamic surfaces (this forces a 3D plot)\nsurfα::Float64: transparency factor of aerodynamic surfaces\nelement2centralize::Union{Nothing,Int}: element for displacement offset\nσ2plot::Vector{<:Real}: (tentative) load factors to plot\npartialLoadStepsLineStyle::Symbol: linestyle for partial load steps\nplotLimits::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real}}}: plot axis limits\nshowScale::Bool: flag to show scale on plot\nscalePos::Vector{<:Real}: position of scale on plot\nloadsSizeScaler::Real: scaler for plot size of distributed and concentrated loads\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nplotSize::Tuple{Real,Real}: size of the plot (width, height) in pixels\navoidDistortion::Bool: flag to avoid distortion in 3D plots\nsave::Bool: flag to save the figure\nsavePath::String: relative path (from the package's directory) on which to save the figure\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_steady_deformations","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_steady_deformations","category":"function","text":"plot_steady_deformations(problems::Array{<:Problem}; kwargs...)\n\nPlots the deformed states of the models in the given problems, over the undeformed assembly of the first problem\n\nArguments\n\nproblems::Array{<:Problem}: problems\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\nplotUndeformed::Bool: flag to plot undeformed assembly\nplotBCs::Bool: flag to plot BCs\nplotDistLoads::Bool: flag to plot distributed loads (includes gravitational and aerodynamic loads)\nview::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Real,Real}}: view angles (by default, the plot is planar if the assemblies lie on a plane of the basis A, and 3D otherwise)\nscale::Real: displacements and rotations scale\nlw::Real: linewidth\ncolorUndef: color for undeformed assembly\ncolorScheme: color scheme for deformed assemblies\nplotAxes::Bool: flag to plot axes\nplotGrid::Bool: flag to plot grid\nlegendPos: legend position\nlegendEntries::Union{Nothing,Array{String}}: legend entries (one for each problem)\ntolPlane::Real: tolerance of the extent of the assemblies along an axis to plot as plane\nplotAeroSurf::Bool: flag to plot aerodynamic surfaces (this forces a 3D plot)\nsurfα::Float64: transparency factor of aerodynamic surfaces\nelements2centralize::Union{Nothing,AbstractArray{Int}}: elements for displacement offset (one for each problem)\nplotLimits::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real}}}: plot axis limits\nloadsSizeScaler::Real: scaler for plot size of distributed and concentrated loads\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nplotSize::Tuple{Real,Real}: size of the plot (width, height) in pixels\navoidDistortion::Bool: flag to avoid distortion in 3D plots\nsave::Bool: flag to save the figure\nsavePath::String: relative path (from the package's directory) on which to save the figure\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_steady_outputs","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_steady_outputs","category":"function","text":"plot_steady_outputs(problem::Problem; kwargs...)\n\nPlots outputs of a steady problem, over the arclength of the beams, at its last load step\n\nArguments\n\nproblem::Problem: problem\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\noutputs::Vector{String}: list of outputs: \"u\", \"p\", \"F\", \"M\", \"V\", \"Ω\", \"γ\", \"κ\", \"P\", \"H\" (all three components, or a single one, e.g. \"F3\") and, for the elements with aerodynamic surface, \"α\", \"cn\", \"cm\", \"ct\", \"cl\", \"cd\"\nbeamGroups: list of beams in each group for which arclengths are concatenated\nlw::Real: linewidth\ncolorScheme: color scheme\nlegendPos: legend position\nsave::Bool: flag to save figures\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nsaveFolder::String: relative path (from the package's directory) of the folder where to save figures\nfigureExtension::String: figure extension\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_mode_shapes","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_mode_shapes","category":"function","text":"plot_mode_shapes(problem::EigenProblem; kwargs...)\n\nPlots the mode shapes of the model in the given eigenproblem, over its steady configuration\n\nArguments\n\nproblem::EigenProblem: problem\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\nplotBCs::Bool: flag to plot BCs\nview::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Real,Real}}: view angles (by default, the plot is planar if the assembly and its modes lie on a plane of the basis A, and 3D otherwise)\nnModes::Union{Nothing,Int}: number of oscillatory modes to plot (first ones)\nmodes2plot::Union{Nothing,AbstractArray{Int}}: array of oscillatory modes to plot\nmodeShapes::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{<:Union{ModeShape,Nothing}}}: mode shapes to plot, instead of the problem's oscillatory modes (e.g., a column of the mode shapes output by filter_tracked_modes, whose nothing entries are skipped). Cannot be input with nModes or modes2plot\nmodeNumbers::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{Int}}: numbers of the mode shapes in modeShapes, used in the labels and colors (e.g., the kept tracks output by filter_tracked_modes). By default, the positions in modeShapes\nscale::Real: displacements and rotations scale\nelement2centralize::Union{Nothing,Int}: element for displacement offset\nnullDampingTol::Real: tolerance for consideration of null damping\nplotSteady::Bool: flag to plot steady solution\nlw::Real: linewidth\ncolorSteady: color for steadily deformed assembly\nmodalColorScheme: color scheme for mode shapes\nnumberOfColors::Union{Missing,Int}: number of colors in legend color gradient (if given, the modes are colored by their numbers)\nplotAxes::Bool: flag to plot axes\nplotGrid::Bool: flag to plot grid\nplotLimits::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real}}}: plot axis limits\nlegendPos: legend position\nmodeLabels::Union{Nothing,Vector{String}}: labels for each mode\ntolPlane::Real: tolerance of the extent of the assembly along an axis to plot as plane\nplotAeroSurf::Bool: flag to plot aerodynamic surfaces (this forces a 3D plot)\nsurfα::Float64: transparency factor of aerodynamic surfaces\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nplotSize::Tuple{Real,Real}: size of the plot (width, height) in pixels\navoidDistortion::Bool: flag to avoid distortion in 3D plots\nsave::Bool: flag to save the figure\nsavePath::String: relative path (from the package's directory) on which to save the figure\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_mode_shapes_animation","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_mode_shapes_animation","category":"function","text":"plot_mode_shapes_animation(problem::EigenProblem; kwargs...)\n\nPlots the animation of the mode shapes of the model in the given eigenproblem, over its steady configuration\n\nArguments\n\nproblem::EigenProblem: problem\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\nplotBCs::Bool: flag to plot BCs\nview::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Real,Real}}: view angles (by default, the plot is planar if the assembly and its modes lie on a plane of the basis A, and 3D otherwise)\nnModes::Union{Nothing,Int}: number of oscillatory modes to plot (first ones)\nmodes2plot::Union{Nothing,AbstractArray{Int}}: array of oscillatory modes to plot\nmodeShapes::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{<:Union{ModeShape,Nothing}}}: mode shapes to plot, instead of the problem's oscillatory modes (e.g., a column of the mode shapes output by filter_tracked_modes, whose nothing entries are skipped). Cannot be input with nModes or modes2plot\nmodeNumbers::Union{Nothing,AbstractVector{Int}}: numbers of the mode shapes in modeShapes, used in the labels and colors (e.g., the kept tracks output by filter_tracked_modes). By default, the positions in modeShapes\nmatchModeFrequency::Bool: flag to match frequencies of each mode in animation\nscale::Real: displacements and rotations scale\nnFramesPerCycle::Int: number of frames over vibration cycle\nfps::Int: frame rate for gif\nelement2centralize::Union{Nothing,Int}: element for displacement offset\nnullDampingTol::Real: tolerance for consideration of null damping\nplotSteady::Bool: flag to plot steady solution\nlw::Real: linewidth\ncolorSteady: color for steadily deformed assembly\nmodalColorScheme: color scheme for mode shapes\nnumberOfColors::Union{Missing,Int}: number of colors in legend color gradient (if given, the modes are colored by their numbers)\nplotAxes::Bool: flag to plot axes\nplotGrid::Bool: flag to plot grid\nplotLimits::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real}}}: plot axis limits\nshowLegend::Bool: flag to show legend\nlegendFontSize::Int: legend font size\nlegendPos: legend position\nmodeLabels::Union{Nothing,Vector{String}}: labels for each mode\ntolPlane::Real: tolerance of the extent of the assembly along an axis to plot as plane\nplotAeroSurf::Bool: flag to plot aerodynamic surfaces (this forces a 3D plot)\nsurfα::Float64: transparency factor of aerodynamic surfaces\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nplotSize::Tuple{Real,Real}: size of the plot (width, height) in pixels\navoidDistortion::Bool: flag to avoid distortion in 3D plots\nsave::Bool: flag to save the figure\nsavePath::String: relative path (from the package's directory) on which to save the figure (must have the .gif extension)\ndisplayProgress::Bool: flag to display progress of gif creation\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_dynamic_deformation","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_dynamic_deformation","category":"function","text":"plot_dynamic_deformation(problem::DynamicProblem; kwargs...)\n\nPlots the animated deformation of the model in the given problem\n\nArguments\n\nproblem::DynamicProblem: problem\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\nplotFrequency::Int: frequency of time steps to plot\nplotUndeformed::Bool: flag to plot undeformed assembly\nplotBCs::Bool: flag to plot BCs\nplotDistLoads::Bool: flag to plot distributed loads (includes gravitational and aerodynamic loads)\nplotAxes::Bool: flag to plot axes\nview::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Real,Real}}: view angles (by default, the plot is planar if the assembly lies on a plane of the observer's basis over the whole animation, and 3D otherwise)\nfps::Int: frame rate for gif\nscale::Real: displacements and rotations scale\nlw::Real: linewidth\ncolorUndef: color for undeformed assembly\ncolorDef: color for deformed assembly\nplotGrid::Bool: flag to plot grid\nlegendPos: legend position\ntolPlane::Real: tolerance of the extent of the assembly along an axis to plot as plane\nplotAeroSurf::Bool: flag to plot aerodynamic surfaces (this forces a 3D plot)\nsurfα::Float64: transparency factor of aerodynamic surfaces\nloadsSizeScaler::Real: scaler for plot size of distributed and concentrated loads\nplotLimits::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real}}}: plot axis limits\nfollow::Symbol: observer that the animation follows. With :none, the plot box is fixed and the motion is seen by an inertial observer at rest. With :basisA, the observer is attached to basis A and the plot box (of the size given by plotLimits) moves with it, while the axes values read the position relative to the inertial origin (resolved in basis A). With :assembly, the plot box is centered on the current position of the point of the assembly that initially coincides with the origin of basis A, while the axes values read the position seen by the inertial observer\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nplotSize::Tuple{Real,Real}: size of the plot (width, height) in pixels\navoidDistortion::Bool: flag to avoid distortion in 3D plots\nsave::Bool: flag to save the figure\nsavePath::String: relative path (from the package's directory) on which to save the figure (must have the .gif extension)\nshowScale::Bool: flag to show scale on plot\nshowTimeStamp::Bool: flag to show time stamp on plot\nscalePos::Vector{<:Real}: position of scale on plot (relative to the plot limits)\ntimeStampPos::Vector{<:Real}: position of time stamp on plot (relative to the plot limits)\ndisplayProgress::Bool: flag to display progress of gif creation\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_dynamic_deformations","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_dynamic_deformations","category":"function","text":"plot_dynamic_deformations(problems::Array{DynamicProblem}; kwargs...)\n\nPlots the animated deformation of the models in the given problems\n\nArguments\n\nproblems::Array{DynamicProblem}: problems\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\nplotFrequency::Int: frequency of time steps to plot (counted over the time steps saved by the problems)\ntimeTol::Real: tolerance to identify time step\nplotBCs::Bool: flag to plot BCs\nplotDistLoads::Bool: flag to plot distributed loads (includes gravitational and aerodynamic loads)\nplotAxes::Bool: flag to plot axes\nview::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Real,Real}}: view angles (by default, the plot is planar if the assemblies lie on a plane of the observer's basis over the whole animation, and 3D otherwise)\nfps::Int: frame rate for gif\nlw::Real: linewidth\ncolorScheme::Symbol: colors for assemblies in problems\nplotGrid::Bool: flag to plot grid\nlegendPos: legend position\nlegendEntries::Union{Nothing,Array{String}}: legend entries for problems (one for each problem)\ntolPlane::Real: tolerance of the extent of the assemblies along an axis to plot as plane\nplotAeroSurf::Bool: flag to plot aerodynamic surfaces (this forces a 3D plot)\nsurfα::Float64: transparency factor of aerodynamic surfaces\nloadsSizeScaler::Real: scaler for plot size of distributed and concentrated loads\nplotLimits::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real}}}: plot axis limits\nfollow::Symbol: observer that the animation follows. With :none, the plot box is fixed and the motion is seen by an inertial observer at rest. With :basisA, the observer is attached to basis A and the plot box (of the size given by plotLimits) moves with it, while the axes values read the position relative to the inertial origin (resolved in basis A). With :assembly, the plot box is centered on the current position of the point of the (last) assembly that initially coincides with the origin of basis A, while the axes values read the position seen by the inertial observer\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nplotSize::Tuple{Real,Real}: size of the plot (width, height) in pixels\navoidDistortion::Bool: flag to avoid distortion in 3D plots\nsave::Bool: flag to save the figure\nsavePath::String: relative path (from the package's directory) on which to save the figure (must have the .gif extension)\nshowTimeStamp::Bool: flag to show time stamp on plot\ndisplayProgress::Bool: flag to display progress of gif creation\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_time_outputs","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_time_outputs","category":"function","text":"plot_time_outputs(problem::DynamicProblem; kwargs...)\n\nPlots outputs of a dynamic problem, over time\n\nArguments\n\nproblem::DynamicProblem: problem\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\nnodes::Vector{Tuple{Int,Int}}: nodes for which to plot, each given by the global ID of an element and the local node (1 or 2) of that element\nelements::Vector{Int}: global IDs of elements for which to plot\nnodalOutputs::Vector{String}: nodal outputs to plot: \"u\", \"p\", \"F\", \"M\" (all three components, or a single one, e.g. \"u2\")\nelementalOutputs::Vector{String}: elemental outputs to plot: \"u\", \"p\", \"F\", \"M\", \"V\", \"Ω\", \"γ\", \"κ\", \"P\", \"H\", \"udot\", \"pdot\", \"Vdot\", \"Ωdot\" (all three components, or a single one, e.g. \"F3\") and, for the elements with aerodynamic surface, \"α\", \"cn\", \"cm\", \"ct\", \"cl\", \"cd\"\nlw::Real: linewidth\ncolorScheme: color scheme\nshowLegend::Bool: flag to show legend\nlegendPos: legend position\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nsave::Bool: flag to save figures\nsaveFolder::String: relative path (from the package's directory) of the folder where to save figures\nfigureExtension::String: figure extension\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"AeroBeams.plot_snapshots","page":"Public API","location":"publicAPI/#AeroBeams.plot_snapshots","category":"function","text":"plot_snapshots(problem::DynamicProblem; kwargs...)\n\nPlots the deformed states for the model in the given dynamic problem at specified time instances (snapshots)\n\nArguments\n\nproblem::DynamicProblem: problem\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbackend::Symbol: backend for plot\nplotBCs::Bool: flag to plot BCs\nplotDistLoads::Bool: flag to plot distributed loads (includes gravitational and aerodynamic loads)\nview::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Real,Real}}: view angles (by default, the plot is planar if the assembly lies on a plane of the inertial basis over all snapshots, and 3D otherwise)\nscale::Real: displacements and rotations scale\nlw::Real: linewidth\nls::Symbol: line style\ncolor: color\nplotAxes::Bool: flag to plot axes\nplotGrid::Bool: flag to plot grid\ntolPlane::Real: tolerance of the extent of the assembly along an axis to plot as plane\nplotAeroSurf::Bool: flag to plot aerodynamic surfaces (this forces a 3D plot)\nsurfα::Float64: transparency factor of aerodynamic surfaces\nsnapshots::Vector{<:Real}: (tentative) time instances to plot, in ascending order\nplotLimits::Union{Nothing,Tuple{Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real},Vector{<:Real}}}: plot axis limits\nshowScale::Bool: flag to show scale on plot\nscalePos::Vector{<:Real}: position of scale on plot (in the coordinates of the axes of planar plots)\nDPI::Int: pixel density\nplotSize::Tuple{Real,Real}: size of the plot (width, height) in pixels\navoidDistortion::Bool: flag to avoid distortion in 3D plots\nsave::Bool: flag to save the figure\nsavePath::String: relative path (from the package's directory) on which to save the figure\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Theory","page":"Theory","location":"theory/#Theory","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Structural formulation","page":"Theory","location":"theory/#Structural-formulation","category":"section","text":"The theory for the structural part of the code was developed by Hodges[1], and practical implementation aspects are based on the works of Yu and Blair[2] and Wang and Yu[3]. Here we will outline only the basics for a better understanding of how to use the package. In the following, we use Bauchau's[4] tensorial notation, in which first-order tensors (represented through vectors) are written with a single underline (underlinecdot) and second- and higher-order tensors (represented through matrices) with a double underline (underlineunderlinecdot)."},{"title":"Geometry and kinematics","page":"Theory","location":"theory/#Geometry-and-kinematics","category":"section","text":"The figure below illustrates the kinematics of the deformation of the beam. The primary basis (frame or reference) for the development of the theory is basis mathcalA, because the equations of motion are written in terms of tensors resolved in it (it can be thought of as a global, or body-attached basis). Its motion in an inertial, fixed basis mathcalI^circ is supposed to be known at all times. In AeroBeams, the user may input the motion of basis mathcalA relative to mathcalI^circ through the vectorial displacement, initial rotation, velocities and/or accelerations (u_A,p_A0,v_A,ω_A,vdot_A and ωdot_A arguments) of that basis to the model with the function create_Model.\n\n(Image: ) Kinematics of beam deformation\n\nIn the undeformed configuration, the beam is idealized as an initially twisted and curved reference line with a defined cross-section at each point, shown as a  gray rectangle in the figure. Vector underlinek defines the undeformed beam's curvatures per unit length (k_1 is the twist and k_2 and k_3 are the bending curvatures), which may be input to the function create_Beam through the argument k. The curvilinear coordinate x_1 defines an intrinsic parametrization of the reference line, i.e., denotes the arc-length along it. In this undeformed configuration, a basis b^+, called the undeformed beam basis, is defined at each point along the reference line. This basis is fixed with respect to mathcalA, and tensor components resolved in it are denoted with a superscript (cdot)^+. Rotation tensor underlineunderlineR_0 brings basis mathcalA to basis b^+. That rotation can be specified by the user to the function create_Beam through the arguments representing the rotation parameters and the choice of rotation parametrization, p0 and rotationParametrization, respectively.\n\nnote: Note\nArgument rotationParametrization is specific to the definition of p0. Internal computations of the systems of equations are done with Wiener-Milenkovic parameters, regardless of the choice of rotationParametrization.\n\nUpon deformation, each point of the beam's undeformed reference line moves to a new point, whose loci define a new reference line in the deformed configuration. At each of such points is defined an orthonormal basis B^star, the deformed beam basis, and tensor components resolved in that basis are denoted with a superscript (cdot)^star."},{"title":"Equations of motion","page":"Theory","location":"theory/#Equations-of-motion","category":"section","text":"The equations of motion are derived from Hamilton's principle:\n\nint_t_1^t_2 int_0^ell (delta (mathcalK-mathcalU) + overlinedelta mathcalW_textnormalext ) dx_1 dt = overlinedeltamathcalA\n\nwhere t_1 and t_2 represent initial and final instances of a time interval, ell is the length of the beam, delta is the variational operator, mathcalK and mathcalU are the kinetic energy and strain energy of the beam per unit length, respectively, overlinedelta mathcalW_textnormalext is the virtual work of applied loads per unit length of the beam, and overlinedeltamathcalA is the virtual action applied at the ends of the beam, at the initial and final times.\n\nThe form of the strain energy per unit length is assumed to be known as a function of the generalized strain measures, that is, mathcalU = mathcalU(underlinegamma^+underlinekappa^+). Therefore, its variation can be written as\n\ndeltamathcalU = delta underlinegamma^+^T underlineF^star + delta underlinekappa^+^T underlineM^star\n\nin which underlineF^star and underlineM^star are the sectional force and moment resultants (i.e., internal load vectors resolved in basis B^star) and underlinegamma^+ and underlinekappa^+ are the force and moment strains (i.e., unidimensional strain measures). These quantities are related through the generalized force-strain, or geometry-material constitutive relation:\n\nbeginbmatrix underlineF^star  underlineM^star endbmatrix = underlineunderlineS beginbmatrix underlinegamma^+  underlinekappa^+ endbmatrix \n\nin which underlineunderlineS is the sectional stiffness matrix. For simple cross-sections of isotropic materials it takes a diagonal form,\n\nunderlineunderlineS = beginbmatrix\nEA                 \n    GA_y            \n       GA_z         \n           GJ       \n             EI_y   \n                EI_z\nendbmatrix\n\nwhere EA is the axial stiffness, GA_y and GA_z are the shear stiffnesses, GJ is the torsional stiffness, and EI_y and EI_z are the bending stiffnesses. However, for complex cross-sections of anisotropic materials, underlineunderlineS may become a fully populated matrix that has to be numerically computed (for instance with GXBeam.jl). In AeroBeams, the sectional stiffness matrix of each element of the beam can be specified through the argument S to the function create_Beam.\n\nnote: Note\nIf the beam has a uniform cross-section and material, a single-valued array may be input for S.\n\nNow, the kinetic energy per unit length of the beam is\n\nmathcalK = frac12 ( mu underlineV^star^T underlineV^star - 2mu underlineOmega^star^T tildeV^star underlineeta^star + underlineOmega^star^T underlineunderlineiota^star underlineOmega^star )\n\nwhere mu = rho A is the mass per unit length, and underlineeta^star and underlineunderlineiota^star are the sectional first and second mass moments of inertia, respectively, underlineV^star is the sectional velocity, and underlineOmega^star is the sectional angular velocity of the beam. Note that the tilde(cdot) represents the skew-symmetric matrix.\n\nSince the kinetic energy per unit length is a function of the sectional generalized velocities, i.e., mathcalK = mathcalK(underlineV^starunderlineOmega^star), its variation may be expressed as\n\ndeltamathcalK = delta underlineV^star^T underlineP^star + delta underlineOmega^star^T underlineH^star\n\nin which underlineP^star and underlineH^star are the sectional linear and angular momenta. These are related to the sectional velocities through the generalized momentum-velocity relationship:\n\nbeginbmatrix underlineP^star  underlineH^star endbmatrix = underlineunderlinemathcalM beginbmatrix underlineV^star  underlineOmega^star endbmatrix \n\nwhere the sectional inertia matrix is given by\n\nunderlinemathcalM = beginbmatrix mu underlineunderlineI_3  - mu tildeeta^star  \n    mu tildeeta^star  underlineunderlineiota^star endbmatrix\n\nJust like the sectional stiffness matrix, the sectional inertia matrix may be analytically computable for simple cross-sections, but a numerical solution must be sought for arbitrary ones. In AeroBeams, the sectional stiffness matrix of each element of the beam can be specified through the argument I to the function create_Beam.\n\nnote: Note\nIf the beam has a uniform cross-section and material, a single-valued array may be input for I.\n\nThe virtual work per unit length, overlinedelta mathcalW_textnormalext, may be written as a function of the displacements and rotations, underlineu and underlinep, and the externally applied distributed forces and moments, underlinef and underlinem. These loads may be input in AeroBeams either being dead (fixed direction) or follower, and being initially resolved in basis mathcalA or b^+, through the arguments f_A_of_x1t, m_A_of_x1t, f_b_of_x1t, m_b_of_x1t, ff_A_of_x1t, mf_A_of_x1t, ff_b_of_x1t, mf_b_of_x1t of the function create_Beam. As their names suggest, these can be functions of the beam arclength coordinate, x_1, and of time, t. In problems where the initial conditions are nonzero, the arguments u0_of_x1, p0_of_x1, udot0_of_x1 and pdot0_of_x1 can be input to create_Beam to define the initial displacements and rotations (and their rates) resolved in basis b^+.\n\ntip: Tip\nYou can also use the functions add_distributed_loads_to_beam! and add_initial_conditions_to_beam!.\n\nnote: Note\nVectors underlinef and underlinem will also include any aerodynamic loads present.        \n\nnote: Note\nConcentrated dead and follower loads (and displacements/rotations) can also be defined as initially resolved in basis mathcalA or b^+, but they must be set as boundary conditions through the function create_BC. \n\nWith all the elements of the equations of motion at hand, we can adjoin the kinematic constraints (generalized strain-displacement relationships) to arrive at a mixed variational statement that encapsulates all the dynamics of this Timoshenko beam formulation. \n\nIn the finite-element discretization, we maintain the displacements and rotation parameters resolved in basis mathcalA, underlineu and underlinep, as well as the sectional loads and velocities, underlineF^star, underlineM^star, underlineV^star and underlineOmega^star as state variables. That allows us to use the simplest shape functions (linear or constant) for these variables and therefore avoid numerical quadrature, besides yielding analytically computable system arrays. These states (and the complementary states underlinegamma^+, underlinekappa^+, underlineP^star and underlineH^star) are assumed as constants over the beam element.\n\nnote: Note\nWhen an aerodynamic surface is attached to the beam, through the argument surface of create_Beam (or with the function add_surface_to_beam!), the generated aerodynamic mesh is coincident with the structural mesh, since strip theory is assumed. Thus, each element will also have its associated aerodynamic states, underlinechi, and aerodynamic coefficients/loads.\n\nPoint inertias may be defined with the function create_PointInertia. They can be attached to a specific element of the beam with the function add_point_inertia_to_beam!. Their effect is to add to the attached element's sectional inertia matrix.\n\nA similar situation arises with respect to linear springs, which can have one or both of their ends attached to nodes of a beam. They are instantiated with the function create_Spring, and can be attached to a beam with the functions add_spring_to_beam! and add_spring_to_beams!."},{"title":"References","page":"Theory","location":"theory/#References","category":"section","text":"[1]: Hodges, D. H. \"Nonlinear Composite Beam Theory\". 2006. American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics. 10.2514/4.866821\n\n[2]: Yu, W. and Blair, M. \"GEBT: A general-purpose nonlinear analysis tool for composite beams\". 2012. Composite Structures. 10.1016/j.compstruct.2012.04.007\n\n[3]: Wang, Q. and Yu, W. \"Geometrically nonlinear analysis of composite beams using Wiener-Milenković parameters\". 2017. Journal of Renewable and Sustainable Energy. 10.1063/1.4985091\n\n[4]: Bauchau, O. A. \"Flexible Multibody Dynamics\". 2011. Springer."},{"title":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","page":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/#Steady-aeroelastic-analysis-of-a-wing-with-flared-folding-wingtip-(FFWT)","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a steady aeroelastic analysis for a wing featuring a flared folding wingtip (FFWT). The influence of the sideslip angle on the behavior of this wing model was studied numerically and experimentally by Healy et al., and more details can be found in Healy's PhD Thesis.\n\n(Image: )\n\nTop view of aircraft model featuring a FFWT by Healy et al.\n\n(Image: )\n\nFront view of aircraft model featuring a FFWT by Healy et al.\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting the variables of our problem. In this example we will analyze the coasting angle of the FFWT under several combinations of wingtip twist, root pitch angle and sideslip angle, which are defined by the arrays φRange, θRange and βRange, respectively. The flare angle of the wingtip is of 20 degrees, and the airspeed is 22 m/s. We assume a fixed spanwise lift distribution through the parameter tipLossDecayFactor, in order to model three-dimensional aerodynamic effects.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Wingtip twist angle range [rad]\nφRange = deg2rad.([-6,0,9])\n\n# Root pitch angle range [rad]\nθRange = deg2rad.(vcat(-3:3:12))\n\n# Sideslip angle range [rad]\nβRange = deg2rad.(vcat(-10:1:30))\n\n# Flare angle [rad]\nΛ = deg2rad(20)\n\n# Airspeed [m/s]\nU = 22\n\n# Discretization\nnElementsInner = 15\nnElementsFFWT = 5\n\n# Tip loss options\nhasTipCorrection = true\ntipLossDecayFactor = 10\n\n# System solver\nsystemSolver = create_NLsolver(method=:newton,linesearch=true)\n\n# Initialize outputs\nϕHinge = Array{Float64}(undef,length(φRange),length(θRange),length(βRange))"},{"title":"Solving the problem","page":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/#Solving-the-problem","category":"section","text":"In the following loops, we create new model instances for each combination of wingtip twist, pitch angle and sideslip angle, create and solve the steady problem, and then extract the coasting angle of the FFWT (ϕHinge). The model creation process is streamlined with the function create_HealySideslipFFWT, taking the appropriate inputs. We run the simulations up to the maximum sideslip angle for which convergence is achieved.\n\n# Loop wingtip twist angle\nfor (i,φ) in enumerate(φRange)\n    # Loop root pitch angle\n    for (j,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n        # Initialize guess solution\n        x0 = zeros(0)\n        # Loop sideslip angle\n        for (k,β) in enumerate(βRange)\n            # Update model\n            model = create_HealySideslipFFWT(flareAngle=Λ,airspeed=U,pitchAngle=θ,wingtipTwist=φ,hasTipCorrection=hasTipCorrection,tipLossDecayFactor=tipLossDecayFactor,nElementsInner=nElementsInner,nElementsFFWT=nElementsFFWT,flightDirection=[sin(β),cos(β),0])\n            # Create and solve problem\n            problem = create_SteadyProblem(model=model,systemSolver=systemSolver,x0=x0)\n                solve!(problem)\n            # Coasting angle\n            ϕHinge[i,j,k] = rad2deg(problem.loadstepHistory.hacStates[end][1].ϕ)\n            # Set guess solution for next iteration\n            x0 = problem.systemArrays.x\n            # Skip remaining sideslip angles if unconverged\n            if !problem.systemSolver.state.converged\n                ϕHinge[i,j,k:end] .= NaN\n                break\n            end\n        end\n    end\nend"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Steady aeroelastic analysis of a wing with flared folding wingtip (FFWT)","location":"literate/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"The post-processing begins by loading the reference data.\n\n# Load reference data\nphim6_aoam3_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoam3_exp.txt\")\nphim6_aoam3_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoam3_num.txt\")\nphim6_aoa0_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa0_exp.txt\")\nphim6_aoa0_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa0_num.txt\")\nphim6_aoa3_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa3_exp.txt\")\nphim6_aoa3_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa3_num.txt\")\nphim6_aoa6_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa6_exp.txt\")\nphim6_aoa6_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa6_num.txt\")\nphim6_aoa9_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa9_exp.txt\")\nphim6_aoa9_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa9_num.txt\")\nphim6_aoa12_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa12_exp.txt\")\nphim6_aoa12_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phim6_aoa12_num.txt\")\n\nphi0_aoam3_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoam3_exp.txt\")\nphi0_aoam3_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoam3_num.txt\")\nphi0_aoa0_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa0_exp.txt\")\nphi0_aoa0_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa0_num.txt\")\nphi0_aoa3_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa3_exp.txt\")\nphi0_aoa3_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa3_num.txt\")\nphi0_aoa6_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa6_exp.txt\")\nphi0_aoa6_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa6_num.txt\")\nphi0_aoa9_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa9_exp.txt\")\nphi0_aoa9_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa9_num.txt\")\nphi0_aoa12_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa12_exp.txt\")\nphi0_aoa12_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi0_aoa12_num.txt\")\n\nphi9_aoam3_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoam3_exp.txt\")\nphi9_aoam3_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoam3_num.txt\")\nphi9_aoa0_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa0_exp.txt\")\nphi9_aoa0_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa0_num.txt\")\nphi9_aoa3_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa3_exp.txt\")\nphi9_aoa3_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa3_num.txt\")\nphi9_aoa6_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa6_exp.txt\")\nphi9_aoa6_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa6_num.txt\")\nphi9_aoa9_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa9_exp.txt\")\nphi9_aoa9_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa9_num.txt\")\nphi9_aoa12_exp = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa12_exp.txt\")\nphi9_aoa12_num = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast/phi9_aoa12_num.txt\")\n\nWe can now plot the coasting angle of the FFWT as a function of sideslip angle for each of the root pitch and wingtip twist angles. The following reference results (both numerical and experimental) were taken from Fig. 7.34 of Healy's PhD Thesis. Healy's numerical method is composed of a Rayleigh-Ritz structural model coupled to a VLM for aerodynamics, with the flared folding wingtip being modeled as a point inertia connected to the inner wing via a hinge. For the most part, the correlation of AeroBeams' results with the reference data is good, especially for the case of zero wingtip twist. This twist profile is very agressively introduced at the very tip of the wing, and similar convergence problems for these cases were also reported by Jan et al. with ASWING. Also note that the wingtip is limited to a fold angle of pm125^degree, a detail that is not implemented yet in AeroBeams.\n\ncolors = cgrad(:rainbow, length(θRange), categorical=true)\nlw = 2\nms = 6\nmsw = 0\ngr()\nfigureNames = [\"HealySideslipFFWTsteadySideslipRangeAoARangeCoast_phim6.svg\" \"HealySideslipFFWTsteadySideslipRangeAoARangeCoast_phi0.svg\" \"HealySideslipFFWTsteadySideslipRangeAoARangeCoast_phi9.svg\"]\n\n# Coast angle vs sideslip angle for each AoA, for several wingtip twist angles\nfor (i,φ) in enumerate(φRange)\n    if i==1\n        aoam3_exp = phim6_aoam3_exp\n        aoam3_num = phim6_aoam3_num\n        aoa0_exp = phim6_aoa0_exp\n        aoa0_num = phim6_aoa0_num\n        aoa3_exp = phim6_aoa3_exp\n        aoa3_num = phim6_aoa3_num\n        aoa6_exp = phim6_aoa6_exp\n        aoa6_num = phim6_aoa6_num\n        aoa9_exp = phim6_aoa9_exp\n        aoa9_num = phim6_aoa9_num\n        aoa12_exp = phim6_aoa12_exp\n        aoa12_num = phim6_aoa12_num\n    elseif i==2\n        aoam3_exp = phi0_aoam3_exp\n        aoam3_num = phi0_aoam3_num\n        aoa0_exp = phi0_aoa0_exp\n        aoa0_num = phi0_aoa0_num\n        aoa3_exp = phi0_aoa3_exp\n        aoa3_num = phi0_aoa3_num\n        aoa6_exp = phi0_aoa6_exp\n        aoa6_num = phi0_aoa6_num\n        aoa9_exp = phi0_aoa9_exp\n        aoa9_num = phi0_aoa9_num\n        aoa12_exp = phi0_aoa12_exp\n        aoa12_num = phi0_aoa12_num\n    else\n        aoam3_exp = phi9_aoam3_exp\n        aoam3_num = phi9_aoam3_num\n        aoa0_exp = phi9_aoa0_exp\n        aoa0_num = phi9_aoa0_num\n        aoa3_exp = phi9_aoa3_exp\n        aoa3_num = phi9_aoa3_num\n        aoa6_exp = phi9_aoa6_exp\n        aoa6_num = phi9_aoa6_num\n        aoa9_exp = phi9_aoa9_exp\n        aoa9_num = phi9_aoa9_num\n        aoa12_exp = phi9_aoa12_exp\n        aoa12_num = phi9_aoa12_num\n    end\n    plt = plot(xlabel=\"Sideslip angle [deg]\", ylabel=\"Coast angle [deg]\", title=\"Wingtip twist = \\$ $(round(Int,φ*180/π)) \\\\degree\\$\", xlims=[-10,30], ylims=[-150,150], yticks=-150:30:150)\n    if i==1\n        plot!(legendfontsize=7, legend=:topleft)\n    else\n        plot!(legend=false)\n    end\n    scatter!([NaN],[NaN], mc=:black, ms=ms, msw=msw, label=\"Healy (2023) - Exp.\")\n    plot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:dash, lw=lw, label=\"Healy (2023) - Num.\")\n    plot!([NaN],[NaN], lc=:black, ls=:solid, lw=lw, label=\"AeroBeams\")\n    for (j,θ) in enumerate(θRange)\n        plot!(βRange*180/π, -ϕHinge[i,j,:], lw=lw, ls=:solid, c=colors[j], label=\"\\$\\\\theta = $(round(Int,θ*180/π)) \\\\degree\\$\")\n        if j==1\n            plot!(aoam3_num[1,:], aoam3_num[2,:], lw=lw, ls=:dash, c=colors[j], label=false)\n            scatter!(aoam3_exp[1,:], aoam3_exp[2,:], ms=ms, msw=msw, c=colors[j], label=false)\n        elseif j==2\n            plot!(aoa0_num[1,:], aoa0_num[2,:], lw=lw, ls=:dash, c=colors[j], label=false)\n            scatter!(aoa0_exp[1,:], aoa0_exp[2,:], ms=ms, msw=msw, c=colors[j], label=false)\n        elseif j==3\n            plot!(aoa3_num[1,:], aoa3_num[2,:], lw=lw, ls=:dash, c=colors[j], label=false)\n            scatter!(aoa3_exp[1,:], aoa3_exp[2,:], ms=ms, msw=msw, c=colors[j], label=false)\n        elseif j==4\n            plot!(aoa6_num[1,:], aoa6_num[2,:], lw=lw, ls=:dash, c=colors[j], label=false)\n            scatter!(aoa6_exp[1,:], aoa6_exp[2,:], ms=ms, msw=msw, c=colors[j], label=false)\n        elseif j==5\n            plot!(aoa9_num[1,:], aoa9_num[2,:], lw=lw, ls=:dash, c=colors[j], label=false)\n            scatter!(aoa9_exp[1,:], aoa9_exp[2,:], ms=ms, msw=msw, c=colors[j], label=false)\n        else\n            plot!(aoa12_num[1,:], aoa12_num[2,:], lw=lw, ls=:dash, c=colors[j], label=false)\n            scatter!(aoa12_exp[1,:], aoa12_exp[2,:], ms=ms, msw=msw, c=colors[j], label=false)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Coordinated turn maneuver of a HALE aircraft","page":"Roll maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedRollManeuver/#Coordinated-turn-maneuver-of-a-HALE-aircraft","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a dynamic analysis of an aircraft in free flight. For that we take a high-altitude long-endurance (HALE) aircraft model described by Patil, Hodges and Cesnik:\n\n(Image: ) HALE model geometry by Patil, Hodges and Cesnik\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Roll maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedRollManeuver/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting up the variables of our problem.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Altitude [m]\nh = 20e3\n\n# Airspeed [m/s]\nU = 25\n\n# Wing and stabilizers parasite drag and flag for induced drag\nwingCd0 = stabsCd0 = 1e-2\nhasInducedDrag = true\n\n# Aerodynamic solver\naeroSolver = Indicial()\n\n# Discretization\nnElemWing = 20\nnElemTailBoom = 5\nnElemHorzStabilizer = 4\nnElemVertStabilizer = 4"},{"title":"Trim problem","page":"Roll maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedRollManeuver/#Trim-problem","category":"section","text":"We have to first trim the aircraft at the specified flight condition. Our trim variables are the engine's thrust (modeled as a follower force at the root of the wing), and the elevator deflection. For the trim problem, we set a Newton-Raphson solver through create_NLsolver with the pseudoinverse method for the linear step (trimSolverMethod=:pinv). We use the built-in function create_cHALE to streamline the model creation process. A dedicated example explains the inner workings of that function.\n\n# System solver\nNRtrim = create_NewtonRaphson(trimSolverMethod=:pinv)\n\n# Model for trim problem\ncHALEtrim,_ = create_cHALE(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,altitude=h,airspeed=U,wingCd0=wingCd0,stabsCd0=stabsCd0,hasInducedDrag=hasInducedDrag,nElemWing=nElemWing,nElemTailBoom=nElemTailBoom,nElemHorzStabilizer=nElemHorzStabilizer,nElemVertStabilizer=nElemVertStabilizer,δElevIsTrimVariable=true,thrustIsTrimVariable=true)\n\n# Create and solve trim problem\ntrimProblem = create_TrimProblem(model=cHALEtrim,systemSolver=NRtrim)\nsolve!(trimProblem)\n\n# Get wing, horizontal amd vertical tail elements\nleftWingRootElem = div(nElemWing,2)\nrightWingRootElem = leftWingRootElem + 1\nleftHTRootElem = nElemWing + nElemTailBoom + div(nElemHorzStabilizer,2)\nrightHTRootElem = leftHTRootElem + 1\nleftVTRootElem = nElemWing + nElemTailBoom + nElemHorzStabilizer + div(nElemVertStabilizer,2)\nrightVTRootElem = leftVTRootElem + 1\n\n# Extract trim variables and outputs\ntrimSol = trimProblem.trimSolution\ntrimAeroVars = trimProblem.loadstepHistory.aeroVariables[end]\ntrimAoA = (trimAeroVars[leftWingRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ + trimAeroVars[rightWingRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ)/2\ntrimThrust = trimSol.trimLoads[1]\ntrimδ = trimSol.trimδ[1]"},{"title":"Dynamic problem","page":"Roll maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedRollManeuver/#Dynamic-problem","category":"section","text":"The coordinate turn maneuver investigated is defined by checked aileron and rudder deflections, linearly ramped. The time-dependent aileron and rudder profiles, δAil and δRudd, are passed as arguments to create the model for the dynamic problem.\n\n# Set checked aileron and rudder deflection profiles\nΔδ = deg2rad(-15)\ntδinit = 1\ntδramp = 5\ntδpeak = tδinit+tδramp\ntδfinal = tδpeak+tδramp\nδRudd = δAil = t -> ifelse(\n    t <= tδinit,\n    0,\n    ifelse(\n        t <= tδpeak,\n        0 + Δδ * ((t-tδinit) / (tδpeak-tδinit)),\n        ifelse(\n            t <= tδfinal,\n            0 + Δδ - Δδ * ((t-tδpeak) / (tδfinal-tδpeak)),\n            0\n        )\n    )\n)\n\n# Model for dynamic problem\ncHALEdyn,_ = create_cHALE(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,altitude=h,airspeed=U,nElemWing=nElemWing,nElemTailBoom=nElemTailBoom,nElemHorzStabilizer=nElemHorzStabilizer,nElemVertStabilizer=nElemVertStabilizer,wingCd0=wingCd0,stabsCd0=stabsCd0,hasInducedDrag=hasInducedDrag,δElev=trimδ,δAil=δAil,δRudd=δRudd,thrust=trimThrust)\n\n# Time variables\nΔt = 5e-2\ntf = 30\n\n# Set system solver for dynamic problem\nNRdyn = create_NewtonRaphson()\n\n# Create and solve dynamic problem\ndynamicProblem = create_DynamicProblem(model=cHALEdyn,x0=trimProblem.systemArrays.x[cHALEtrim.assemblyData.rangeNominal],finalTime=tf,Δt=Δt,systemSolver=NRdyn)\nsolve!(dynamicProblem)"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Roll maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedRollManeuver/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"The first post-processing step is to unpack the desired outputs.\n\n# Unpack numerical solution\nsol = dynamicProblem.solutionHistory\nt = sol.t\nstates = sol.states\naeroVars = sol.aeroVariables\nwingAoA = rad2deg.([(aeroVars[i][leftWingRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ + aeroVars[i][rightWingRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ)/2 for i in eachindex(t)])\nhtAoA = rad2deg.([(aeroVars[i][leftHTRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ + aeroVars[i][rightHTRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ)/2 for i in eachindex(t)])\nvtAoA = rad2deg.([(aeroVars[i][leftVTRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ + aeroVars[i][rightVTRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ)/2 for i in eachindex(t)])\nleftWingtipAoA = rad2deg.([aeroVars[i][1].unsteadyKin.αₑ  for i in eachindex(t)])\nrightWingtipAoA = rad2deg.([aeroVars[i][nElemWing].unsteadyKin.αₑ for i in eachindex(t)])\nΔu1 = [(cHALEdyn.kinematicsBasisA.u_AVec[i][1] + states[i][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[1]) for i in eachindex(t)] .- states[1][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[1]\nΔu2 = [(cHALEdyn.kinematicsBasisA.u_AVec[i][2] + states[i][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[2]) for i in eachindex(t)] .- states[1][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[2]\nΔu3 = [(cHALEdyn.kinematicsBasisA.u_AVec[i][3] + states[i][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[3]) for i in eachindex(t)] .- states[1][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[3]\nairspeed = [norm(aeroVars[i][leftWingRootElem].steadyKin.U + aeroVars[i][rightWingRootElem].steadyKin.U)/2 for i in eachindex(t)]\n\nWe are now ready to visualize the results through the changes in lateral distance (x_1), forward distance (x_2) and altitude (x_3), angles of attack and airspeed.\n\ngr()\n\n# Lateral (x1) distance\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Lateral distance [m]\")\nplot!(t, Δu1, c=:black, lw=2, label=false)\n\n# Forward (x2) distance\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Forward distance [m]\")\nplot!(t, Δu2, c=:black, lw=2, label=false)\n\n# Altitude (Δx3)\nplt3 = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Altitude [m]\")\nplot!(t, Δu3, c=:black, lw=2, label=false)\n\n# AoA\nplt4 = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Root angle of attack [deg]\")\nplot!(t, wingAoA*180/π, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"Wing (root)\")\nplot!(t, leftWingtipAoA*180/π, c=:green, lw=2, label=\"Wing (left tip)\")\nplot!(t, rightWingtipAoA*180/π, c=:red, lw=2, label=\"Wing (right tip)\")\nplot!(t, htAoA*180/π, c=:blue, lw=2, label=\"HT (root)\")\nplot!(t, vtAoA*180/π, c=:cyan, lw=2, label=\"VT (root)\")\n\n# Airspeed\nplt5 = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\")\nplot!(t, airspeed, c=:black, lw=2, label=false)\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\nLet's also visualize the motion of the aircraft through the view of an observer that follows the aircraft, keeping it at the center of the plot box. For that, we use the plot_dynamic_deformation function with follow=:assembly and other appropriate arguments.\n\nplot_dynamic_deformation(dynamicProblem,follow=:assembly,view=(30,15),plotDistLoads=false,plotFrequency=4,fps=24,plotLimits=([-20,20],[-20,20],[-20,20]),save=true,savePath=\"/docs/build/literate/cHALECheckedRollManeuver_motion.gif\")\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Pitch maneuver of a HALE aircraft","page":"Pitch maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedPitchManeuver/#Pitch-maneuver-of-a-HALE-aircraft","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a dynamic analysis of an aircraft in free flight. For that we take a high-altitude long-endurance (HALE) aircraft model described by Patil, Hodges and Cesnik:\n\n(Image: ) HALE model geometry by Patil, Hodges and Cesnik\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Pitch maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedPitchManeuver/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting up the variables of our problem.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Altitude [m]\nh = 20e3\n\n# Airspeed [m/s]\nU = 25\n\n# Wing and stabilizers parasite drag and flag for induced drag\nwingCd0 = stabsCd0 = 1e-2\nhasInducedDrag = true\n\n# Aerodynamic solver\naeroSolver = Indicial()\n\n# Discretization\nnElemWing = 20\nnElemTailBoom = 5\nnElemHorzStabilizer = 4\nnElemVertStabilizer = 2"},{"title":"Trim problem","page":"Pitch maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedPitchManeuver/#Trim-problem","category":"section","text":"We have to first trim the aircraft at the specified flight condition. Our trim variables are the engine's thrust (modeled as a follower force at the root of the wing), and the elevator deflection. For the trim problem, we set a Newton-Raphson solver through create_NLsolver with the pseudoinverse method for the linear step (trimSolverMethod=:pinv). We use the built-in function create_cHALE to streamline the model creation process. A dedicated example explains the inner workings of that function.\n\n# System solver\nsystemSolver = create_NLsolver(trimSolverMethod=:pinv)\n\n# Model for trim problem\ncHALEtrim,_ = create_cHALE(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,altitude=h,airspeed=U,wingCd0=wingCd0,stabsCd0=stabsCd0,hasInducedDrag=hasInducedDrag,nElemWing=nElemWing,nElemTailBoom=nElemTailBoom,nElemHorzStabilizer=nElemHorzStabilizer,nElemVertStabilizer=nElemVertStabilizer,δElevIsTrimVariable=true,thrustIsTrimVariable=true)\n\n# Create and solve trim problem\ntrimProblem = create_TrimProblem(model=cHALEtrim,systemSolver=systemSolver)\nsolve!(trimProblem)\n\n# Get wing and horizontal tail elements\nleftWingRootElem = div(nElemWing,2)\nrightWingRootElem = leftWingRootElem + 1\nleftHTRootElem = nElemWing + nElemTailBoom + div(nElemHorzStabilizer,2)\nrightHTRootElem = leftHTRootElem + 1\n\n# Extract trim variables and outputs\ntrimSol = trimProblem.trimSolution\ntrimAeroVars = trimProblem.loadstepHistory.aeroVariables[end]\ntrimAoA = (trimAeroVars[leftWingRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ + trimAeroVars[rightWingRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ)/2\ntrimThrust = trimSol.trimLoads[1]\ntrimδ = trimSol.trimδ[1]"},{"title":"Dynamic problem","page":"Pitch maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedPitchManeuver/#Dynamic-problem","category":"section","text":"The pitch maneuver investigated is defined by a checked elevator deflection, linearly ramped. The amplitude of the deflection, Δδ, is negative so as to command a climb (at least initially). The time-dependent elevator profile, δ, is then passed as an argument to create the model for the dynamic problem.\n\n# Set checked elevator deflection profile\nΔδ = deg2rad(-10)\ntδinit = 5\ntδramp = 1\ntδpeak = tδinit+tδramp\ntδfinal = tδpeak+tδramp\nδ = t -> ifelse(\n    t <= tδinit,\n    trimδ,\n    ifelse(\n        t <= tδpeak,\n        trimδ + Δδ * ((t-tδinit) / (tδpeak-tδinit)),\n        ifelse(\n            t <= tδfinal,\n            trimδ + Δδ - Δδ * ((t-tδpeak) / (tδfinal-tδpeak)),\n            trimδ\n        )\n    )\n)\n\n# Model for dynamic problem\ncHALEdyn,_ = create_cHALE(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,altitude=h,airspeed=U,wingCd0=wingCd0,stabsCd0=stabsCd0,hasInducedDrag=hasInducedDrag,nElemWing=nElemWing,nElemTailBoom=nElemTailBoom,nElemHorzStabilizer=nElemHorzStabilizer,nElemVertStabilizer=nElemVertStabilizer,δElev=δ,thrust=trimThrust)\n\n# Time variables\nΔt = 5e-2\ntf = 60\n\n# Set system solver for dynamic problem\nNRdyn = create_NLsolver(method=:newton)\n\n# Create and solve dynamic problem\ndynamicProblem = create_DynamicProblem(model=cHALEdyn,x0=trimProblem.systemArrays.x[cHALEtrim.assemblyData.rangeNominal],finalTime=tf,Δt=Δt,systemSolver=NRdyn)\nsolve!(dynamicProblem)"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Pitch maneuver of a HALE aircraft","location":"literate/cHALECheckedPitchManeuver/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"The first post-processing step is to unpack the desired outputs.\n\n# Unpack numerical solution\nsol = dynamicProblem.solutionHistory\nt = sol.t\nstates = sol.states\naeroVars = sol.aeroVariables\nwingAoA = rad2deg.([(aeroVars[i][leftWingRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ + aeroVars[i][rightWingRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ)/2 for i in eachindex(t)])\nhtAoA = rad2deg.([(aeroVars[i][leftHTRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ + aeroVars[i][rightHTRootElem].unsteadyKin.αₑ)/2 for i in eachindex(t)])\nΔu1 = [(cHALEdyn.kinematicsBasisA.u_AVec[i][1] + states[i][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[1]) for i in eachindex(t)] .- states[1][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[1]\nΔu2 = [(cHALEdyn.kinematicsBasisA.u_AVec[i][2] + states[i][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[2]) for i in eachindex(t)] .- states[1][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[2]\nΔu3 = [(cHALEdyn.kinematicsBasisA.u_AVec[i][3] + states[i][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[3]) for i in eachindex(t)] .- states[1][leftWingRootElem].nodal.u2[3]\nairspeed = [norm(aeroVars[i][leftWingRootElem].steadyKin.U + aeroVars[i][rightWingRootElem].steadyKin.U)/2 for i in eachindex(t)]\n\nWe are now ready to visualize the results through the changes in forward distance and altitude, root angle of attack at the wing and tail, and airspeed.\n\ngr()\n\n# Forward distance\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Forward distance [m]\")\nplot!(t, Δu2, c=:black, lw=2, label=false)\n\n# Altitude\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Altitude [m]\")\nplot!(t, Δu3, c=:black, lw=2, label=false)\n\n# Root AoA\nplt3 = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Root angle of attack [deg]\")\nplot!(t, wingAoA, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"Wing\")\nplot!(t, htAoA, c=:blue, lw=2, label=\"HT\")\n\n# Airspeed\nplt4 = plot(xlabel=\"Time [s]\", ylabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\")\nplot!(t, airspeed, c=:black, lw=2, label=false)\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\nLet's also visualize the motion of the aircraft through the view of an observer at the body-attached basis A. For that, we use the plot_dynamic_deformation function with follow=:basisA and other appropriate arguments.\n\nplot_dynamic_deformation(dynamicProblem,view=(30,15),plotBCs=false,plotDistLoads=false,plotFrequency=10,plotLimits=([-20,20],[-10,250],[-20,20]),save=true,savePath=\"/docs/build/literate/cHALECheckedPitchManeuver_motion.gif\")\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Motion of a simply supported beam under initial conditions","page":"Motion of a simply supported beam under initial conditions","location":"literate/initialDispAndVelBeam/#Motion-of-a-simply-supported-beam-under-initial-conditions","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a dynamic analysis with initial conditions. To do that, we take a simply supported beam under a specified combination of sinusoidal initial displacement and velocity.\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Initial conditions","page":"Motion of a simply supported beam under initial conditions","location":"literate/initialDispAndVelBeam/#Initial-conditions","category":"section","text":"The setup of our problem begins by specifying the initial conditions, defined by the variable initialConditions. These can be defined by the displacement (u3), the angle of rotation (θ2), or both (in this example, each one with its time rate). The package makes the initial states of the dynamic problem consistent with whichever data are specified, so the three options lead to practically the same solution. The displacement and rotation (and their time rates) correspond to those of a simply supported beam's second bending mode shape. Notice that θ2 is the angle of rotation over the beam's arclength coordinate (x1) and p2 is the corresponding Wiener-Milenkovic rotation parameter.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Select initial conditions as either :displacement, :rotation or :both\ninitialConditions = :displacement\n\n# Initial conditions: sinusoidal displacement and velocity of u3 and/or rotation of θ2\nδ = 1e-3\nσ = 1e-1\nu3 = x1 -> δ*sin.(2π*x1/L)\nθ2 = x1 -> -δ*2π/L*cos.(2π*x1/L)\np2 = x1 -> 4*tan.(θ2(x1)/4)\nudot3 = x1 -> σ*sin.(2π*x1/L)\nθdot2 = x1 -> -σ*2π/L*cos.(2π*x1/L)\npdot2 = x1 -> sec.(θ2(x1)/4).^2 .* θdot2(x1)"},{"title":"Beam","page":"Motion of a simply supported beam under initial conditions","location":"literate/initialDispAndVelBeam/#Beam","category":"section","text":"We create a simple isotropic beam for this example. The only relevant properties are the beam's length (L), bending stiffness (EI) and mass per unit length (ρA). The initial conditions are appended to the beam accordingly, with the function add_initial_conditions_to_beam!.\n\n# Beam\nL = 1\nEI = 1\nρA = 1\n∞ = 1e4\nnElem = 40\nbeam = create_Beam(name=\"beam\",length=L,nElements=nElem,S=[isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=∞,EIy=EI)],I=[inertia_matrix(ρA=ρA)])\n\n# Set initial conditions\nif initialConditions == :displacement\n    add_initial_conditions_to_beam!(beam=beam, conditions=Dict(:u0_of_x1 => x1->[0; 0; u3(x1)], :udot0_of_x1 => x1->[0; 0; udot3(x1)]))\nelseif initialConditions == :rotation\n    add_initial_conditions_to_beam!(beam=beam, conditions=Dict(:p0_of_x1 => x1->[0; p2(x1); 0], :pdot0_of_x1 => x1->[0; pdot2(x1); 0]))\nelseif initialConditions == :both\n    add_initial_conditions_to_beam!(beam=beam, conditions=Dict(:u0_of_x1 => x1->[0; 0; u3(x1)], :p0_of_x1 => x1->[0; p2(x1); 0], :udot0_of_x1 => x1->[0; 0; udot3(x1)], :pdot0_of_x1 => x1->[0; pdot2(x1); 0]))\nelse\n    error(\"initialConditions must be either :displacement, :rotation or :both\")\nend"},{"title":"Boundary conditions","page":"Motion of a simply supported beam under initial conditions","location":"literate/initialDispAndVelBeam/#Boundary-conditions","category":"section","text":"The beam is simply-supported: pinned at one end and with a roller at the other.\n\n# BCs\npin = create_BC(name=\"pin\",beam=beam,node=1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0,:u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p3A=>0))\nroller = create_BC(name=\"roller\",beam=beam,node=nElem+1,conditions=Dict(:u3A=>0))"},{"title":"Model","page":"Motion of a simply supported beam under initial conditions","location":"literate/initialDispAndVelBeam/#Model","category":"section","text":"Our model consists of the beam and the associated boundary conditions. Notice that gravity is neglected.\n\n# Model\ninitialDispAndVelBeam = create_Model(name=\"initialDispAndVelBeam\",beams=[beam],BCs=[pin,roller])"},{"title":"Creating the dynamic problem","page":"Motion of a simply supported beam under initial conditions","location":"literate/initialDispAndVelBeam/#Creating-the-dynamic-problem","category":"section","text":"The time and frequency variables of our problem are defined by the beam's second bending mode frequency, ω2, whose analytical value is known. We'll run the simulation for one cycle, and with a time step one hundredth of that.\n\n# Time and frequency variables\nω2 = (2π/L)^2 * sqrt(EI/ρA)\nT = 2π/ω2\ncycles = 1\ntf = cycles * T\nΔt = T/100\n\nIn initial conditions problems, in general we do not know the initial values of all the states of the formulation (displacements, rotations, internal loads and sectional velocities) and of their time rates. Thus, they have to be determined together with the initial step, which is automatically done by the dynamic problem: the unspecified displacements and rotations are those of the static equilibrium, the unspecified velocities follow the specified ones as in a quasi-static motion, and the accelerations satisfy the equations of motion at the initial time. This procedure does not depend on any tuning parameters. Now we can create the problem and solve it.\n\n# Create and solve the problem\nproblem = create_DynamicProblem(model=initialDispAndVelBeam,finalTime=tf,Δt=Δt)\nsolve!(problem)"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Motion of a simply supported beam under initial conditions","location":"literate/initialDispAndVelBeam/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"First, we unpack the numerical solution of our variables of interest and compute analytical values.\n\n# Unpack numerical solution\nsol = problem.solutionHistory\nt = sol.t\nstates = sol.states\nNt = length(t)\ntNorm = t/T\nmidElem = div(nElem,2)\nquarterElem = div(nElem,4)\nu3_quarter = [states[i][quarterElem].nodal.u2[3] for i in 1:Nt]\nV3_quarter = [(states[i][quarterElem].midpoint.V[3]+states[i][quarterElem+1].midpoint.V[3])/2 for i in 1:Nt]\nVdot3_quarter = [(states[i][quarterElem].rates.Vdot[3]+states[i][quarterElem+1].rates.Vdot[3])/2 for i in 1:Nt]\nθ2_root = [states[i][1].nodal.θ[2] for i in 1:Nt]\nΩ2_mid = [(states[i][midElem].midpoint.Ω[2]+states[i][midElem+1].midpoint.Ω[2])/2 for i in 1:Nt]\nΩdot2_mid = [(states[i][midElem].rates.Ωdot[2]+states[i][midElem+1].rates.Ωdot[2])/2 for i in 1:Nt]\n\n# Compute analytical values\nu3_quarter_analytic = δ*cos.(ω2*t)*sin(2π*1/4) + σ/ω2*sin.(ω2*t)*sin(2π*1/4)\nV3_quarter_analytic = -δ*ω2*sin.(ω2*t)*sin(2π*1/4) + σ*cos.(ω2*t)*sin(2π*1/4)\nVdot3_quarter_analytic = -δ*ω2^2*cos.(ω2*t)*sin(2π*1/4) + -σ*ω2*sin.(ω2*t)*sin(2π*1/4)\nθ2_root_analytic = -δ*2π/L*cos.(ω2*t)*cos(2π*0) + -σ*2π/L/ω2*sin.(ω2*t)*cos(2π*0)\nΩ2_mid_analytic = δ*2π/L*ω2*sin.(ω2*t)*cos(2π*1/2) + -σ*2π/L*cos.(ω2*t)*cos(2π*1/2)\nΩdot2_mid_analytic = δ*2π/L*ω2^2*cos.(ω2*t)*cos(2π*1/2) + σ*2π/L*ω2*sin.(ω2*t)*cos(2π*1/2)\n\nNow let's plot the displacement, rotation, velocities and accelerations at the appropriate points of the beam. The correspondance with the analytical reference is very good.\n\ngr()\n\n# Displacement at quarter-length\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$t/T\\$\", ylabel=\"\\$u_3\\$ at \\$x_1=L/4\\$ [m]\")\nplot!(tNorm,u3_quarter, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!(tNorm[1:5:end],u3_quarter_analytic[1:5:end], c=:blue, ms=5, msw=0, label=\"Analytical\")\n\n# Velocity at quarter-length\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$t/T\\$\", ylabel=\"\\$V_3\\$ at \\$x_1=L/4\\$ [m/s]\")\nplot!(tNorm,V3_quarter, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!(tNorm[1:5:end],V3_quarter_analytic[1:5:end], c=:blue, ms=5, msw=0, label=\"Analytical\")\n\n# Acceleration at quarter-length\nplt3 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$t/T\\$\", ylabel=\"\\$\\\\dot{V}_3\\$ at \\$x_1=L/4\\$ [m/\\$s^2\\$]\")\nplot!(tNorm,Vdot3_quarter, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!(tNorm[1:5:end],Vdot3_quarter_analytic[1:5:end], c=:blue, ms=5, msw=0, label=\"Analytical\")\n\n# Rotation at root\nplt4 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$t/T\\$\", ylabel=\"\\$\\\\theta/(2\\\\pi)\\$ at \\$x_1=0\\$\")\nplot!(tNorm,θ2_root/(2π), c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!(tNorm[1:5:end],θ2_root_analytic[1:5:end]/(2π), c=:blue, ms=5, msw=0, label=\"Analytical\")\n\n# Angular velocity at mid-length\nplt5 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$t/T\\$\", ylabel=\"\\$\\\\Omega_2\\$ at \\$x_1=L/2\\$ [rad/s]\")\nplot!(tNorm,Ω2_mid, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!(tNorm[1:5:end],Ω2_mid_analytic[1:5:end], c=:blue, ms=5, msw=0, label=\"Analytical\")\n\n# Angular acceleration at mid-length\nplt6 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$t/T\\$\", ylabel=\"\\$\\\\dot{\\\\Omega}_2\\$ at \\$x_1=L/2\\$ [rad/\\$s^2\\$]\")\nplot!(tNorm,Ωdot2_mid, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!(tNorm[1:5:end],Ωdot2_mid_analytic[1:5:end], c=:blue, ms=5, msw=0, label=\"Analytical\")\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\nFinally, we can visualize the motion of the beam using the function plot_dynamic_deformation with the appropriate inputs.\n\n# Animation\nplot_dynamic_deformation(problem,scale=1/δ/10,scalePos=[0.15;-0.05;0],timeStampPos=[0.5;-0.05;0],plotFrequency=1,plotLimits=([0,L],[-L/2,L/2],[-L/3,L/3]),save=true,savePath=\"/docs/build/literate/initialDispAndVelBeam_motion.gif\")\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Gust response of an airfoil section","page":"Gust response of an airfoil section","location":"literate/OMCgustTests/#Gust-response-of-an-airfoil-section","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up an aerodynamic analysis of gust response. While AeroBeams can't run a strictly aerodynamic analyses (only aeroelastic ones), that can be done in practice by using a one-element stiff beam supported at both ends, effectively removing structural deformation effects. We'll evaluate the response of the NACA 0012 airfoil to one-minus-cosine gusts through several tests, each with different pitch angle and gust length. The performance of the available aerodynamic solvers the gust indicial solvers will be assessed too.\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Core function setup","page":"Gust response of an airfoil section","location":"literate/OMCgustTests/#Core-function-setup","category":"section","text":"The following core function solves our problem given the test case, the aerodynamic solver the gust indicial solver. Notice that we use the function create_OneMinusCosineGust with the appropriate arguments to streamline the process of creation of the gust.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Core function\nfunction OMCgustTestsCore(aeroSolver,gustSolver,testCase)\n\n    # Set test case data\n    if testCase == 1\n        Ma = 0.2            # Mach number\n        U = 68.06           # Airspeed [m/s]\n        H = π               # Normalized gust length\n        b = 0.40663         # Airfoil semichord [m]\n        τ = 2*H*b/U         # Gust duration [s]\n        w = 2.3748          # Gust peak velocity [m/s]\n        t₀ = 60*b/U         # Time of gust encounter [s]\n        tf = t₀ + 30*b/U    # Total simulation time [s]\n        θ = deg2rad(0)         # Airfoil pitch angle [rad]\n    elseif testCase == 2\n        Ma = 0.2\n        U = 68.06\n        H = π\n        b = 0.40663\n        τ = 2*H*b/U\n        w = 2.3748\n        t₀ = 60*b/U\n        tf = t₀ + 30*b/U\n        θ = deg2rad(10)\n    elseif testCase == 3\n        Ma = 0.2\n        U = 68.06\n        H = π\n        b = 0.40663\n        τ = 2*H*b/U\n        w = 2.3748\n        t₀ = 60*b/U\n        tf = t₀ + 30*b/U\n        θ = deg2rad(15)\n    elseif testCase == 4\n        Ma = 0.2\n        U = 68.06\n        H = 8π\n        b = 0.40663\n        τ = 2*H*b/U\n        w = 2.3748\n        t₀ = 60*b/U\n        tf = t₀ + 80*b/U\n        θ = deg2rad(0)\n    elseif testCase == 5\n        Ma = 0.2\n        U = 68.06\n        H = 8π\n        b = 0.40663\n        τ = 2*H*b/U\n        w = 2.3748\n        t₀ = 60*b/U\n        tf = t₀ + 80*b/U\n        θ = deg2rad(10)\n    elseif testCase == 6\n        Ma = 0.2\n        U = 68.06\n        H = 8π\n        b = 0.40663\n        τ = 2*H*b/U\n        w = 2.3748\n        t₀ = 60*b/U\n        tf = t₀ + 80*b/U\n        θ = deg2rad(15)\n    end\n\n    # Gust\n    gust = create_OneMinusCosineGust(initialTime=t₀,duration=τ,verticalVelocity=w)\n\n    # Wing surface\n    airfoil = deepcopy(NACA0012)\n    update_all_airfoil_parameters!(airfoil,Ma)\n    derivationMethod = AD()\n    surf = create_AeroSurface(ppSolver=aeroSolver,gustSolver=gustSolver,derivationMethod=derivationMethod,airfoil=airfoil,c=2*b,normSparPos=1/4,updateAirfoilParameters=true)\n\n    # Wing beam\n    L = 1\n    nElem = 1\n    wing = create_Beam(name=\"beam\",length=L,nElements=nElem,S=[isotropic_stiffness_matrix(∞=1e12)],I=[inertia_matrix(ρA=1)],p0=transform_to_WM([0,0,θ],from=:E321),surface=surf)\n\n    # BCs\n    clamp1 = create_BC(name=\"clamp1\",beam=wing,node=1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0,:u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\n    clamp2 = create_BC(name=\"clamp2\",beam=wing,node=nElem+1,conditions=Dict(:u1A=>0,:u2A=>0,:u3A=>0,:p1A=>0,:p2A=>0,:p3A=>0))\n\n    # Model\n    OMCgustTests = create_Model(name=\"OMCgustTests\",beams=[wing],BCs=[clamp1,clamp2],v_A=[0,U,0],gust=gust)\n\n    # Time variables\n    timeSteps = 1500\n    Δt = (tf-t₀)/timeSteps\n\n    # Create and solve dynamic problem\n    problem = create_DynamicProblem(model=OMCgustTests,finalTime=tf,Δt=Δt)\n    solve!(problem)\n\n    # Unpack numerical solution\n    sol = problem.solutionHistory\n    aeroVars = sol.aeroVariables\n    t = sol.t\n    cn = [aeroVars[i][1].aeroCoefficients.cn for i in eachindex(t)]\n    ct = [aeroVars[i][1].aeroCoefficients.ct for i in eachindex(t)]\n    cl = @. cn*cos(θ) + ct*sin(θ)\n\n    # Load reference data\n    if testCase == 1\n        ΔclRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/gustTests/Hpi_A0.txt\")\n    elseif testCase == 2\n        ΔclRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/gustTests/Hpi_A10.txt\")\n    elseif testCase == 3\n        ΔclRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/gustTests/Hpi_A15.txt\")\n    elseif testCase == 4\n        ΔclRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/gustTests/H8pi_A0.txt\")\n    elseif testCase == 5\n        ΔclRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/gustTests/H8pi_A10.txt\")\n    elseif testCase == 6\n        ΔclRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/gustTests/H8pi_A15.txt\")\n    end\n\n    # Time index of gust encounter\n    ind = argmin(abs.(t .- t₀)) - 1\n\n    # Non-dimensional time and cl increment vectors\n    τ = U/b * (t[ind:end] .- t[ind])\n    Δcl = cl[ind:end] .- cl[ind]\n\n    return τ,Δcl,ΔclRef\n\nend"},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Gust response of an airfoil section","location":"literate/OMCgustTests/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"To setup the problem, we define which tests will be run, and which aerodynamic and gust indicial solvers will be used. Notice that BLi stands for our modified Beddoes-Leishman model, whereas BLo is the original version.\n\n# Tests range\ntests = 1:6\n\n# Aerodynamic and gust indicial solvers\naeroSolvers = [QuasiSteady(); Indicial(); Inflow(); BLi(); BLo()]\ngustLoadsSolvers = [IndicialGust(:Kussner), IndicialGust(:BerciRighi)]\n\n# Initialize outputs\nτ = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(aeroSolvers),length(gustLoadsSolvers),length(tests))\nΔcl = Array{Vector{Float64}}(undef,length(aeroSolvers),length(gustLoadsSolvers),length(tests))\nΔclRef = Array{Matrix{Float64}}(undef,length(aeroSolvers),length(gustLoadsSolvers),length(tests))"},{"title":"Problem solving","page":"Gust response of an airfoil section","location":"literate/OMCgustTests/#Problem-solving","category":"section","text":"We now loop the configurations to solve for each one of them.\n\n# Loop aerodynamic solver\nfor (i,aeroSolver) in enumerate(aeroSolvers)\n    # Loop gust solver\n    for (j,gustSolver) in enumerate(gustLoadsSolvers)\n        # Loop test cases\n        for (k,testCase) in enumerate(tests)\n            # Solve for current configuration\n            τ[i,j,k],Δcl[i,j,k],ΔclRef[i,j,k] = OMCgustTestsCore(aeroSolver,gustSolver,testCase)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nLet's compare the accuracy of the aerodynamic formulations by plotting the development of the lift coefficient. The indicial functions by Kussner (approximation of analytical) and by Berci and Righi (semi-analytical) compare well to the CFD data by Mallik and Raveh. The attached-flow models show good results (except the quasi-steady one, which assumes instantaneous changes in the angle of attack) for test 2, in which the airfoil is pitched by 10 degrees. However, at test 6 the dynamic stall models fare better, since the airfoil is at a high angle of attack (15 degrees) and stalls upon encountering the gust.\n\ncolors = cgrad(:rainbow, length(aeroSolvers), categorical=true)\nlinestyles = [:solid :dash :dot :dashdot :dashdotdot]\ngr()\n\n# Test 2 - Kussner indicial function\nplt12 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$\\\\tau\\$ [semichords]\", ylabel=\"\\$\\\\Delta c_l\\$\", xlims=[0,30], ylims=[0.0,0.25], title=\"Test 2 - Kussner indicial function\")\nscatter!(ΔclRef[1,1,2][1,:], ΔclRef[1,1,2][2,:], color=:black, ms=4, label=\"Mallik & Raveh (2019)\")\nfor (i,aeroSolver) in enumerate(aeroSolvers)\n    plot!(τ[i,1,2], Δcl[i,1,2], color=colors[i], lw=2, ls=linestyles[i], label=aeroSolver.name)\nend\n\n# Test 2 - Berci and Righi indicial function\nplt22 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$\\\\tau\\$ [semichords]\", ylabel=\"\\$\\\\Delta c_l\\$\", xlims=[0,30], ylims=[0.0,0.25], title=\"Test 2 - Berci and Righi indicial function\")\nscatter!(ΔclRef[1,2,2][1,:], ΔclRef[1,2,2][2,:], color=:black, ms=4, label=\"Mallik & Raveh (2019)\")\nfor (i,aeroSolver) in enumerate(aeroSolvers)\n    plot!(τ[i,2,2], Δcl[i,2,2], color=colors[i], lw=2, ls=linestyles[i], label=aeroSolver.name)\nend\n\n# Test 6 - Kussner indicial function\nplt16 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$\\\\tau\\$ [semichords]\", ylabel=\"\\$\\\\Delta c_l\\$\", xlims=[0,80], ylims=[-0.06,0.25], title=\"Test 6 - Kussner indicial function\")\nscatter!(ΔclRef[1,1,6][1,:], ΔclRef[1,1,6][2,:], color=:black, ms=4, label=\"Mallik & Raveh (2019)\")\nfor (i,aeroSolver) in enumerate(aeroSolvers)\n    plot!(τ[i,1,6], Δcl[i,1,6], color=colors[i], lw=2, ls=linestyles[i], label=aeroSolver.name)\nend\n\n# Test 6 - Berci and Righi indicial function\nplt26 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$\\\\tau\\$ [semichords]\", ylabel=\"\\$\\\\Delta c_l\\$\", xlims=[0,80], ylims=[-0.06,0.25], title=\"Test 6 - Berci and Righi indicial function\")\nscatter!(ΔclRef[1,2,6][1,:], ΔclRef[1,2,6][2,:], color=:black, ms=4, label=\"Mallik & Raveh (2019)\")\nfor (i,aeroSolver) in enumerate(aeroSolvers)\n    plot!(τ[i,2,6], Δcl[i,2,6], color=colors[i], lw=2, ls=linestyles[i], label=aeroSolver.name)\nend\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"AeroBeams.jl","page":"Home","location":"#AeroBeams.jl","category":"section","text":"A Julia package for the simulation of aeroelastic problems in beam structures\n\nDocumentation for AeroBeams.jl."},{"title":"Overview","page":"Home","location":"#Overview","category":"section","text":"AeroBeams is a finite-element implementation of the geometrically exact beam theory of Hodges[1] [2], augmented with aerodynamic formulations in order to solve aeroelastic problems."},{"title":"Installation","page":"Home","location":"#Installation","category":"section","text":"To install AeroBeams, simply go to the package manager mode in the Julia REPL by typing ], and then\n\npkg> add AeroBeams"},{"title":"Features","page":"Home","location":"#Features","category":"section","text":"The software is a general-purpose aeroelastic solver for flexible aircraft structures that can be modeled as an assembly of anisotropic, initially curved and twisted beams undergoing arbitrarily large displacements and rotations. Multibody dynamics capabilities include only simple hinges, with possible hinge axis constraints (such as in flared folding wingtips).\n\nThe assembly of beams can be subjected to time-dependent boundary conditions that can be specified either in a global or local frame of reference, including: \n\nNodal displacements and rotations\nConcentrated, dead or follower nodal forces and moments\nDistributed, dead or follower forces and moments\nGravitational loads\n\nOther assembly capabilities include:\n\nConcentrated elemental inertias\nAttached nodal springs\n\nAerodynamic models are based on strip theory augmented with specified spanwise corrections. The following are available:\n\nQuasi-steady\nLinear unsteady (indicial[5] and inflow[6] methods) \nDynamic stall (original[7] and modified Beddoes-Leishman models[8]) \n\nThe beam assembly can also be immersed in gust fields, such as:\n\nDiscrete gusts (sharp-edged, one-minus-cosine, ...)\nContinuous gusts (based on von Kármán or Dryden 2-dimensional spectra)\n\nFour types of analysis can be simulated:\n\nSteady: when all time derivatives are zero.\nTrim: a steady analysis in which the attitude, flap/elevator deflections and loads (such as thrust) are treated as unknowns (trim variables) to be solved for. \nVibration/Stability: an eigenvalue-based (linearized) analysis of small motions about a deformed state.\nDynamic: time-marching.\n\nA simple built-in post-processing tool allows the visualization of the results. Here is an example of the large-displacement motion of limit-cycle oscillations of the Pazy wing benchmark after encountering a gust:\n\n(Image: Pazy Wing Gust Response)\n\nPurely structural problems (in the absence of aerodynamic loads) can be analyzed as well, such as the \"flying spaghetti\"[9]:\n\n(Image: flying spaghetti)"},{"title":"Limitations","page":"Home","location":"#Limitations","category":"section","text":"A few limitations of the theory to keep in mind when using this package:\n\nThe strain measures considered yield a \"Timoshenko\" beam theory, which is valid for beams of solid or thin-walled, but closed cross-sections, and beams of strip-like cross-sections. It is not accurate for beams of thin-walled, open cross-section (like \"C\", \"I\", \"L\", \"T\", \"Z\", etc) for which the warping of the cross-section caused by torsion is important. For these, a \"Vlasov\" beam theory would be required.\nThe force-strain relationship of the material is assumed to be linear, i.e., the strains are assumed to be small.\nStructural damping is not modeled.\nThe formulation does not account for the trapeze effect, which is the tendency of a twisted beam to untwist (twist) as a traction (compression) force is applied. This effect can be important for beams of open cross-section (whose torsional compliance is high) under axial loads.\nThree-dimensional aerodynamic effects in wings are not modeled directly (such as in panel methods), but rather only through a specified distribution function."},{"title":"Related packages","page":"Home","location":"#Related-packages","category":"section","text":"GXBeam is a similar package for solving problems in beam structures. It also makes use of Hodges' equations for Timoshenko beams, though modified to include structural damping. It can also compute the sectional properties (stiffness and inertia matrices) and recover the cross-sectional strains and stresses for arbitrary (geometry- and material-wise) cross sections.\n\nBeamDyn is a module of the OpenFAST wind turbine simulation tool. It also employs a geometrically exact beam theory, though it uses Legendre spectral finite elements for faster grid convergence, specially when dealing with varying properties along the beam arclength.\n\nGEBT[3] [4] was the first open source implementation of the geometrically exact beam theory proposed by Hodges. All previously mentioned packages (including AeroBeams.jl) are based on it.\n\nGEBTAero couples GEBT with the 2D aerodynamic model of finite states[6] to yield an aeroelastic program for very flexible wings. It is geared towards maximum computational efficiency, and can compute sectional beam properties.\n\nSHARPy is an aeroelastic simulation toolbox for very flexible aircraft and wind turbines. On the structural side, it employs a displacement-based geometrically exact beam formulation, whereas the aerodynamic modeling uses the Unsteady Vortex Lattice Method (UVLM) and the Source Panel Method (SPM).\n\nThe University of Michigan's Nonlinear Aeroelastic Simulation Toolbox (UM/NAST) is a framework for simulating the flight dynamics of flexible aircraft structures modeled as an assembly of beams. It implements a strain-based geometrically exact beam theory[10]. It is not, however, an open source code.\n\nAeroFlex is a MATLAB toolbox for studying the flight dynamics of highly flexible airplanes, which makes use of the same formulation as UM/NAST, and is open source.\n\nASWING is a program for the aerodynamic, structural, and control-response analysis of aircraft with flexible wings and fuselages of high to moderate aspect ratio. It uses a displacement-based nonlinear beam structural formulation coupled to a general extented lifting-line aerodynamic model to simulate the flight of aircraft with or without a user-defined control law. ASWING is licensed for commercial use."},{"title":"Theory","page":"Home","location":"#Theory","category":"section","text":"A basic understanding of the theory will help the user of this package:\n\nPages = [\n    \"theory.md\"\n ]\n Depth = 1"},{"title":"Examples","page":"Home","location":"#Examples","category":"section","text":"The following examples are a useful starting point for those new to the package. They explain several of its functionalities.\n\nPages = [\n    \"literate/cHALEmodel.md\",\n    \"literate/OMCgustTests.md\",\n    \"literate/typicalSectionFlutterAndDivergence.md\",\n    \"literate/archUnderFollowerPressure.md\",\n    \"literate/compositeCantileverMD.md\",\n    \"literate/PazyWingTorsionTest.md\",\n    \"literate/PazyWingPitchRange.md\",\n    \"literate/HealyBaselineFFWTfreeFlutterAoARangeURange.md\",\n    \"literate/HealySideslipFFWTsteadyTwistRangeAoARangeSideslipRangeCoast.md\",\n    \"literate/PazyWingFlutterPitchRange.md\",\n    \"literate/sweptTipRotor.md\",\n    \"literate/twoStoryFrame.md\",\n    \"literate/initialDispAndVelBeam.md\",\n    \"literate/flyingScissors.md\",\n    \"literate/heliosTrim.md\",\n    \"literate/BWBflutter.md\",\n    \"literate/cHALECheckedPitchManeuver.md\",\n    \"literate/cHALECheckedRollManeuver.md\",\n]"},{"title":"References","page":"Home","location":"#References","category":"section","text":"[1]: Hodges, D. H. \"Nonlinear Composite Beam Theory\". 2006. American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics. 10.2514/4.866821\n\n[2]: Hodges, D. H., Shang, X. and Cesnik, C. E. S. \"Finite element solution of nonlinear intrinsic equations for curved composite beams\". 1996. Journal of the American Helicopter Society. 10.2514/6.1995-1174\n\n[3]: Yu, W. and Blair, M. \"GEBT: A general-purpose nonlinear analysis tool for composite beams\". 2012. Composite Structures. 10.1016/j.compstruct.2012.04.007\n\n[4]: Wang, Q. and Yu, W. \"Geometrically nonlinear analysis of composite beams using Wiener-Milenković parameters\". 2017. Journal of Renewable and Sustainable Energy. 10.1063/1.4985091\n\n[5]: Leishman, J. G. \"Principles of Helicopter Aerodynamics\". 2006. Cambridge University Press.\n\n[6]: Peters, D. A., Karunamoorthy, S. and Cao, W. \"Finite state induced flow models. I: Two-dimensional thin airfoil\". 1995. Journal of Aircraft. 10.2514/3.46718\n\n[7]: Leishman, J. G. and Beddoes, T. S. \"A Semi‐Empirical Model for Dynamic Stall\". 1989. Journal of the American Helicopter Society. 10.4050/JAHS.34.3.3\n\n[8]: dos Santos, L. G. P. and Marques, F. D. \"Improvements on the Beddoes-Leishman dynamic stall model for low speed applications\". 2021. Journal of Fluids and Structures. 10.1016/j.jfluidstructs.2021.103375\n\n[9]: J. C. Simo and L. Vu-Quoc. \"On the Dynamics of Flexible Beams Under Large Overall Motions—The Plane Case: Part II\". 1986. Journal of Applied Mechanics. 10.1115/1.3171871\n\n[10]: Su, W and Cesnik, C. E. S. \"Strain-based geometrically nonlinear beam formulation for modeling very flexible aircraft\". 2011. International Journal of Solids and Structures. 10.1016/j.ijsolstr.2011.04.012"},{"title":"Dynamic analysis of an articulated beam","page":"Dynamic analysis of an articulated beam","location":"literate/flyingScissors/#Dynamic-analysis-of-an-articulated-beam","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a dynamic analysis, using the articulated beam in free flight proposed by Simo and Vu-Quoc:\n\n(Image: ) Articulated beam: definition and motion by Simo and Vu-Quoc\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Beam","page":"Dynamic analysis of an articulated beam","location":"literate/flyingScissors/#Beam","category":"section","text":"The set up of this problem begins with the definition of the articulated links (beams), each with its own inertia properties. Notice, however, that we create a single beam, which has different properties for each half of its elements. This beam has a hinge defined by the arguments hingedNodes and hingedNodesDoF of the function create_Beam.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Beam\nL = 10\nEA = GA = GJ = 1e6\nEI = 1e4\nρA = ρI1 = 1\nρI2 = 10\nθ₀ = atan(4/3)\nnElem = 20\nmidElem = div(nElem,2)\nI1 = inertia_matrix(ρA=ρA,ρIy=ρI1,ρIz=ρI1)\nI2 = inertia_matrix(ρA=ρA,ρIy=ρI2,ρIz=ρI2)\nI = vcat([I2 for _ in 1:midElem],[I1 for _ in 1:midElem])\nbeam = create_Beam(name=\"beam\",length=L,nElements=nElem,S=[isotropic_stiffness_matrix(EA=EA,GAy=GA,GAz=GA,GJ=GJ,EIy=EI,EIz=EI)],I=I,p0=transform_to_WM([0,θ₀,0],from=:E321),hingedNodes=[div(nElem,2)+1],hingedNodesDoF=[[false,true,false]])"},{"title":"Boundary conditions","page":"Dynamic analysis of an articulated beam","location":"literate/flyingScissors/#Boundary-conditions","category":"section","text":"The boundary conditions consist of the force and moment impulses applied at one end of the beam.\n\n# BCs\nM₀ = 160\nτ = 0.5\nM2 = t -> ifelse.(t.<=τ, M₀, 0)\nF1 = t -> M2(t)/4\nforces = create_BC(name=\"forces\",beam=beam,node=nElem+1,conditions=Dict(:F1A=>t->F1(t),:M2A=>t->M2(t)))"},{"title":"Model","page":"Dynamic analysis of an articulated beam","location":"literate/flyingScissors/#Model","category":"section","text":"Our model is composed of the beam and the boundary conditions. We name it flyingScissors.\n\n# Model\nflyingScissors = create_Model(name=\"flyingScissors\",beams=[beam],BCs=[forces])"},{"title":"Problem","page":"Dynamic analysis of an articulated beam","location":"literate/flyingScissors/#Problem","category":"section","text":"The problem is solved for a total time tf, with a time step Δt.\n\n# Time variables\ntf = 5\nΔt = 5e-2\n\n# Create and solve the problem\nproblem = create_DynamicProblem(model=flyingScissors,finalTime=tf,Δt=Δt)\nsolve!(problem)"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Dynamic analysis of an articulated beam","location":"literate/flyingScissors/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"The first post-processing step is to retrieve the outputs of interest, namely the displacements at each end of the articulated beam.\n\n# Unpack numerical solution\nsol = problem.solutionHistory\nt = sol.t\nstates = sol.states\nu1_tipA = [states[i][1].nodal.u2[1] for i in 1:length(t)]\nu3_tipA = [states[i][1].nodal.u2[3] for i in 1:length(t)]\nu1_tipB = [states[i][nElem].nodal.u2[1] for i in 1:length(t)]\nu3_tipB = [states[i][nElem].nodal.u2[3] for i in 1:length(t)]\nu1_hinge = [states[i][div(nElem,2)].nodal.u2[1] for i in 1:length(t)]\nu3_hinge = [states[i][div(nElem,2)].nodal.u2[3] for i in 1:length(t)]\n\nLet's plot the displacements at the tip of each link and at the hinge.\n\nlw = 2\ngr()\n\n# Nomalized u1 displacements\nlabels = [\"Tip A\" \"Hinge\" \"Tip B\"]\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$t\\$ [s]\", ylabel=\"\\$u_1/L\\$\")\nplot!(t,[u1_tipA/L, u1_hinge/L, u1_tipB/L], lw=lw, label=labels)\n\n# Nomalized u3 displacements\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"\\$t\\$ [s]\", ylabel=\"\\$u_3/L\\$\")\nplot!(t,[u3_tipA/L, u3_hinge/L, u3_tipB/L], lw=lw, label=labels)\n\n(Image: ) (Image: )\n\nFinaly, we may visualize the motion of the beam using the function plot_dynamic_deformation with the appropriate inputs. It correlates well with the one displayed by Simo and Vu-Quoc.\n\n# Animation\nplot_dynamic_deformation(problem,plotFrequency=2,plotLimits=([0,2*L],[-L/2,L/2],[-L,0]),save=true,savePath=\"/docs/build/literate/flyingScissors_motion.gif\")\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."},{"title":"Flutter of a Blended-Wing-Body","page":"Flutter of a Blended-Wing-Body","location":"literate/BWBflutter/#Flutter-of-a-Blended-Wing-Body","category":"section","text":"This example illustrates how to set up a flutter analysis of an aircraft in free flight. For that we take a Blended-Wing-Body (BWB) vehicle, a swept flying-wing with trailing-edge control surfaces. This aircraft model was described in Weihua Su's PhD thesis:\n\n(Image: ) BWB model geometry by Su\n\n(Image: ) BWB's body properties by Su\n\n(Image: ) BWB's wing properties by Su\n\ntip: Tip\nThe code for this example is available here."},{"title":"Problem setup","page":"Flutter of a Blended-Wing-Body","location":"literate/BWBflutter/#Problem-setup","category":"section","text":"Let's begin by setting up the variables of our problem.\n\nusing AeroBeams\n\n# Airspeed range [m/s]\nURange = collect(30:2:160)\n\n# Flight altitude [m]\nh = 0e3\n\n# Aerodynamic solver\naeroSolver = Inflow()\n\n# Trim system solver\nNR = create_NewtonRaphson(trimSolverMethod=:LM)\n\n# Number of modes to track\nnModes = 8\n\n# Eigensolver method and minimum number of eigenvalues to be computed\neigenmethod = :krylovkit\nnev = 80\n\n# Initialize outputs\neigenProblem = Array{EigenProblem}(undef,length(URange))\ntrimAoA = Array{Float64}(undef,length(URange))\ntrimThrust = Array{Float64}(undef,length(URange))\ntrimδ = Array{Float64}(undef,length(URange))\neigensols = Array{EigenSolution}(undef,length(URange))\n\nNext, we address an important step to be taken when performing flutter analyses in free flight with AeroBeams: to attach the model to light springs in displacement and rotation. This step is necessary for the solver to find the flight dynamic (rigid-body) modes of the vehicle, by introducing some sensitivity of the finite element states to those degrees-of-freedom. In the present case, we will attach two springs to the vehicle, one at each of the nodes where the transition from the body (fuselage) to the wing begins. An appropriate value for the stiffness of the springs is specified by the variable μ.\n\n# Attachment spring stiffness\nμ = 1e-2"},{"title":"Problem solution","page":"Flutter of a Blended-Wing-Body","location":"literate/BWBflutter/#Problem-solution","category":"section","text":"At this point we can sweep the airspeed vector to find the solution at each value.\n\n# Initial guess solution\nx0 = zeros(0)\n# Sweep airspeed range\nfor (i,U) in enumerate(URange)\n    # The first step of the solution is to trim the aircraft at that flight condition (combination of altitude and airspeed). We leverage the built-in function in AeroBeams to create our model for the trim problem.\n    BWBtrim = first(create_BWB(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,altitude=h,airspeed=U,δElevIsTrimVariable=true,thrustIsTrimVariable=true))\n    # Root element's global ID.\n    rootElemGlobalID = BWBtrim.beams[3].elements[1].assemblyData.globalID\n    # Now we create and solve the trim problem.\n    trimProblem = create_TrimProblem(model=BWBtrim,systemSolver=NR,x0=x0)\n    solve!(trimProblem)\n    # We extract the trim variables at the current airspeed and set them into our pre-allocated arrays. The trimmed angle of attack at the root, `trimAoA`, is not necessary for the flutter analyses, it is merely an output of interest.\n    trimSol = trimProblem.trimSolution\n    aeroVars = trimProblem.loadstepHistory.aeroVariables\n    trimAoA[i] = rad2deg(aeroVars[end][rootElemGlobalID].unsteadyKin.αₑ)\n    trimThrust[i] = trimSol.trimLoads[1]\n    trimδ[i] = trimSol.trimδ[1]\n    # Set initial guess solution for next iteration.\n    global x0 = trimProblem.systemArrays.x\n    # Let's create a new model with attachment springs.\n    BWBtrimSpringed = first(create_BWB(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,altitude=h,airspeed=U,δElevIsTrimVariable=true,thrustIsTrimVariable=true,withAttachmentSprings=true,μ=μ))\n    # Then create and solve the trim problem for the springed model.\n    trimProblemSpringed = create_TrimProblem(model=BWBtrimSpringed,systemSolver=NR,x0=x0)\n    solve!(trimProblemSpringed)\n    # Let's make sure that the trim outputs of the springed model are very close to the one without springs, that is, the springs did not affect the trim solution. Notice that all ratios are very close to or at 1.\n    trimAoASpringed = rad2deg(trimProblemSpringed.loadstepHistory.aeroVariables[end][rootElemGlobalID].unsteadyKin.αₑ)\n    trimThrustSpringed = trimProblemSpringed.trimSolution.trimLoads[1]\n    trimδSpringed = trimProblemSpringed.trimSolution.trimδ[1]\n    println(\"Trim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = $(round(trimAoASpringed/trimAoA[i],sigdigits=4)), thrust = $(round(trimThrustSpringed/trimThrust[i],sigdigits=4)), δ = $(round(trimδSpringed/trimδ[i],sigdigits=4))\")\n    # All the variables needed for the stability analysis are now in place. We create the model for the eigenproblem, using the trim variables found previously with the springed model in order to solve for the stability around that exact state.\n    BWBeigen = first(create_BWB(aeroSolver=aeroSolver,altitude=h,airspeed=U,δElev=trimδSpringed,thrust=trimThrustSpringed,withAttachmentSprings=true,μ=μ))\n    # Now we create and solve the eigenproblem. Notice that by using `solve_eigen!()`, we skip the step of finding the steady state of the problem, making use of the known trim solution (with the keyword argument `refTrimProblem`)\n    eigenProblem[i] = create_EigenProblem(model=BWBeigen,eigenmethod=eigenmethod,nev=nev,refTrimProblem=trimProblemSpringed)\n    solve_eigen!(eigenProblem[i])\n    # The final step in the loop is extracting the eigensolution\n    eigensols[i] = eigenProblem[i].eigensolution\nend\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 0.9998\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0\nTrim outputs' ratios springed/nominal: AoA = 1.0, thrust = 1.0, δ = 1.0"},{"title":"Post-processing","page":"Flutter of a Blended-Wing-Body","location":"literate/BWBflutter/#Post-processing","category":"section","text":"We can use the AeroBeams built-in function mode_tracking to enhance the chances of correctly tracking the frequencies and dampings of each mode\n\n# Mode tracking\nfreqs,damps,modeShapes,_ = mode_tracking(eigensols)\n\n# Get frequencies and dampings of oscillatory modes\nfreqsOsc,dampsOsc,modeShapesOsc,keptTracks = filter_tracked_modes(freqs,damps,modeShapes; fmin=1e0, fmax=150, maxTracks=nModes, dropOutsideBounds=true)\n\nThe flutter onset speeds and frequencies are computed\n\n# Flutter onset speeds, frequencies and modes\nflutterSpeeds,flutterFreqs,flutterModes,flutterInds,_ = find_flutter(URange,freqsOsc,dampsOsc,1:length(URange))\nfor (mode,speed,freq) in zip(flutterModes,flutterSpeeds,flutterFreqs)\n    println(\"Mode $mode: flutter speed = $(round(speed,sigdigits=4)) m/s, flutter frequency = $(round(freq,digits=2)) rad/s\")\nend\nMode 5: flutter speed = 118.1 m/s, flutter frequency = 47.88 rad/s\nMode 3: flutter speed = 132.3 m/s, flutter frequency = 58.59 rad/s\nMode 4: flutter speed = 141.9 m/s, flutter frequency = 61.6 rad/s\n\nWe can load the reference solution found with the University of Michigan's Nonlinear Aeroelastic Simulation Tool (UM/NAST) in its 2024 version (not the version in Su's thesis).\n\n# Load reference data\ntrimAoARef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/BWB/trimAoA.txt\")\ntrimThrustRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/BWB/trimThrust.txt\")\ntrimδRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/BWB/trimDelta.txt\")\nfreqsRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/BWB/freqs.txt\")\ndampsRef = readdlm(pkgdir(AeroBeams)*\"/test/referenceData/BWB/damps.txt\")\n\nWe are ready to plot the results. The following plots show the trim root angle of attack, motor thrust and elevator deflection as functions of the airspeed. The correlation with the reference solution is very good, except for the thrust. This difference arises because of the way in which the airfoil tangential force is computed when a flap deflection is present: either as proportional to the total normal force, which includes the flap-induced component (in AeroBeams), or proportional to the effective angle of attack (in UM/NAST).\n\nusing Plots\ngr()\n\n# Root AoA\nplt1 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Trim root AoA [deg]\", xlims=[0,160], xticks=vcat(30:10:160))\nplot!(URange, trimAoA, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!(trimAoARef[1,:],trimAoARef[2,:], c=:black, ms=4, label=\"UM/NAST\")\n\n# Thrust\nplt2 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Trim thrust [N]\", xlims=[30,160], xticks=vcat(0:10:160), legend=:bottomright)\nplot!(URange, trimThrust, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!(trimThrustRef[1,:],trimThrustRef[2,:], c=:black, ms=4, label=\"UM/NAST\")\n\n# Elevator deflection\nplt3 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Trim elevator deflection [deg]\", xlims=[30,160], xticks=vcat(0:10:160), legend=:bottomright)\nplot!(URange, trimδ*180/π, c=:black, lw=2, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!(trimδRef[1,:],trimδRef[2,:], c=:black, ms=4, label=\"UM/NAST\")\n\n(Image: ) (Image: ) (Image: )\n\nThe stability results can be visualized through the following root locus and V-g-f (frequency and damping evolution) plots. The lowest flutter speed is computed by AeroBeams at approximately 118.1 m/s, at a frequency of 47.9 rad/s. Conversely, the flutter speed and frequency predicted by UM/NAST are around 137.7 m/s and 47.5 rad/s, yielding differences of 14% and 0.8%, on speed and frequency, respectively. Despite the flutter speed disagreement, there is qualitatively good agreement among the models for the behavior of the roots.\n\n# Colormap\nmodeColors = cgrad(:rainbow, nModes, categorical=true)\n\n# Root locus\nplt4 = plot(xlabel=\"Damping [1/s]\", ylabel=\"Frequency [rad/s]\", xlims=[-35,5],ylims=[0,125])\nscatter!([NaN],[NaN], c=:black, shape=:circle, ms=4, msw=0, label=\"AeroBeams\")\nscatter!([NaN],[NaN], c=:black, shape=:utriangle, ms=4, msw=0, label=\"UM/NAST\")\nfor mode in 1:nModes\nscatter!(dampsRef[mode+1,:], Hz2rads.(freqsRef[mode+1,:]), c=:black, shape=:utriangle, ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\nscatter!(dampsOsc[mode,:], freqsOsc[mode,:], c=modeColors[mode], shape=:circle, ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\nend\n\n# V-g-f\nplt51 = plot(ylabel=\"Frequency [rad/s]\", xlims=[30,160], xticks=0:10:160, ylims=[0,125])\nfor mode in 1:nModes\nplot!(URange, freqsOsc[mode,:], c=modeColors[mode], shape=:circle, ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\nend\nplt52 = plot(xlabel=\"Airspeed [m/s]\", ylabel=\"Damping ratio\", xlims=[30,160], ylims=[-0.3,0.2], xticks=30:10:160, yticks=-0.3:0.1:0.2)\nfor mode in 1:nModes\nplot!(URange, dampsOsc[mode,:]./freqsOsc[mode,:], c=modeColors[mode], shape=:circle, ms=4, msw=0, label=false)\nend\nplt5 = plot(plt51,plt52, layout=(2,1))\n\n(Image: ) (Image: )\n\nFinally, we may visualize the mode shapes of the last eigenproblem (at highest airspeed), making use of the plot_mode_shapes function with the appropriate inputs. Modes 1 and 2 seem to respectively be lateral-directional and longitudinal flight dynamic modes, whereas the others are structural.\n\n# Plot mode shapes\nmodesPlot = plot_mode_shapes(eigenProblem[end],modeShapes=modeShapesOsc[:,end],modeNumbers=collect(1:nModes),scale=1,view=(45,30),element2centralize=11,legendPos=(0.6,0.95),modalColorScheme=:rainbow)\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl."}]
}
